window.RV_LOADED['ib-english-b-hl'] = {"name":"IB English B HL","topics":[{"name":"Experiences","subtopics":[{"name":"Customs & Traditions","questions":[{"ref":"EB0028","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0028\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Cherry Blossom Viewing in the UK}}$\n\nEvery spring, people in the UK gather to celebrate the blooming of cherry blossoms, a custom inspired by Japan's ancient tradition called **hanami,** or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$flower viewing.” $\\underline{\\textrm{This}}$ event encourages friends, families, and communities to come together in parks and gardens to admire the delicate pink and white blossoms that mark the arrival of spring. Though cherry trees are not native to the UK, many varieties were brought over from Japan, and today, $\\underline{\\textrm{they}}$ are a cherished part of the British spring landscape.\n\nIn London, places like Regent's Park, Greenwich Park, and Kew Gardens attract visitors each year with $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ stunning displays of cherry blossoms. People stroll beneath the blossoms, taking photographs, having picnics, and enjoying the seasonal beauty. The National Trust has also launched an annual #BlossomWatch initiative, inviting people across the UK to share photos of cherry blossoms on social media.\n\nThe cherry blossom season reminds people of the fleeting nature of life, a concept called **mono no aware** in Japanese, or the awareness of life's impermanence. Across the UK, these delicate blooms serve as a beautiful symbol of renewal, connecting people to nature and inspiring reflection as the flowers bloom and fade each year.\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.**  \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$  $\\underline{\\textrm{This}}$ event encourages friends...    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ ...and today, $\\underline{\\textrm{they}}$ are a cherished part... [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ ...visitors each year with $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ stunning displays...    [1 mark]\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What custom has led to the viewing of Cherry Blossoms in the UK?    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do the cherry blossoms signify?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the significance of **mono no aware** in the UK?     [1 mark]\n\n$\\underline{\\textrm{}}$","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the blooming of cherry blossoms}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This refers to the event mentioned earlier in the paragraph, which is the gathering of people in the UK to celebrate the blooming of cherry blossoms.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Cherry trees}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$though cherry trees are not native to the UK, many varieties were brought over from Japan, and today, they are a cherished part of the British spring landscape” indicates that they refers back to the cherry trees.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Places like Regent's Park, Greenwich Park, and Kew Gardens}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The phrase mentions that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$places like Regent's Park, Greenwich Park, and Kew Gardens attract visitors each year with their stunning displays of cherry blossoms,” indicating that their refers to these parks' displays of blossoms.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(Japan's ancient tradition called) Hanami}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$every spring… the blooming of cherry blossoms, a custom inspired by Japan's ancient tradition called hanami” thus Hanami led to the tradition of cherry blossom viewing in the UK\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ Accept any:\n\n- $\\textrm{The arrival of Spring}$ or\n\n- $\\textrm{a beautiful symbol of renewal}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text clearly states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... come together in parks and gardens to admire the delicate pink and white blossoms that mark the arrival of spring.”\\\n\nIn paragraph 3 the text also mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$these delicate blooms serve as a beautiful symbol of renewal” which is also a valid response to this question as the words signify and symbol(ise) are similar in meaning.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the awareness of life's impermanence}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The cherry blossom season... a concept called mono no aware in Japanese, or the awareness of life's impermanence.” this indicates that another terms for this is the awareness of life's impermanence."},{"ref":"EB0029","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0029\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{The Meaning of Mardi Gras in New Orleans}}$\n\n*Mardi Gras* in New Orleans is one of the world's most famous festivals, celebrated annually in February or March, before Lent begins. Its roots trace back to French colonial traditions, yet over centuries, it has blended with African, Indigenous, Spanish, and Creole influences to create something uniquely New Orleans. For weeks leading up to Mardi Gras day, the streets come alive with vibrant parades featuring elaborate floats, dancers in dazzling costumes, and music that fills the air. Each parade krewe (group) spends the year planning their floats, music, and performances to tell a story through art, rhythm, and color. \n\nFor many New Orleanians, *Mardi Gras* is about freedom, joy, and community; it's a time to set aside everyday concerns and fully express oneself. The festival also celebrates the rich diversity of New Orleans society, where people from all walks of life join together to revel in the festivities. *Mardi Gras* attracts millions of tourists each year, eager to experience the sights, sounds, and lively atmosphere of this cultural celebration. For both locals and visitors, *Mardi Gras* is an unforgettable experience that captures the spirit of New Orleans.\n\n**Match the two halves of the sentences.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The unique character of Mardi Gras comes from…\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$In the weeks before Mardi Gras, the streets are filled with…\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$For people who live in New Orleans, the festival means…    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Every year, millions of visitors come to New Orleans for…\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$a Lent celebration.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$colorful parades, dancers, and the sound of lively music.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$millions of tourists.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$an exciting atmosphere of celebration.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$traditional European parades.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$a time to relax and feel part of the community.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$a celebration of French traditions.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$a mixture of diverse cultures and traditions.\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in paragraph 2.**\n\n5.$\\hspace{1em}$Mardi Gras is an experience that can be enjoyed by….\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n5.$\\hspace{1em}$The text describes this celebration as an …\n\n    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{H}$   [1 mark]\n\n- \"it has blended with African, Indigenous, Spanish, and Creole influences\"\n\n\\\n\n*Blend* and *mix* are similar in meaning. They refer to something that combines different things, in this case, traditions and cultures from different places.\n\n- **Option E** is incorrect. \"Traditional European parades\" wrongly suggests the celebration is purely European; the text makes clear that the parades come from a blend of many cultural influences.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “vibrant parades featuring elaborate floats, dancers in dazzling costumes, and music that fills the air\" \\\n\n*Vibrant* and *colorful* are similar in meaning. They refer to something bright and full of life, in this case, the parades and dancers. \n\n- **Option C** is incorrect. The text mentions “*Mardi Gras* attracts millions of tourists each year,” but it does not say that tourists are present in the previous weeks.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “it's a time to set aside everyday concerns and fully express oneself\" \\\n\n“Set aside everyday concerns\" and *relax* are similar in meaning. They refer to taking a break from daily worries, in this case, during the festival. \n\n- **Option G** is incorrect. *Mardi Gras* has its roots in French colonial traditions. However, people do not celebrate this.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “the sights, sounds, and lively atmosphere of this cultural celebration\" \n\n\\\n\n*Lively* and *exciting* are similar in meaning. They refer to something full of energy and activity, in this case, the festival's atmosphere. People from all over the world come to New Orleans to enjoy the atmosphere.\n\n- **Option A** is incorrect. Tourists come for the lively festival, not to celebrate Lent. In fact, the text mentions Mardi Gras takes place “in February or March, before Lent begins.” \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{locals and visitors}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “For both locals and visitors”\n\n- The text mentions everyone (locals and visitors) can enjoy *Mardi Grass*.\n\n \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{unforgettable experience}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Mardi Gras is an unforgettable experience”\\\n\n*Mardi Grass* is described as an unforgettable experience in the end of the text."},{"ref":"EB0030","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0030\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Kingston Kitchen}}$\n\nIf you love good food and good vibes, Kingston Kitchen, in Jamaica, is a festival you need to visit. More than just a food event, Kingston Kitchen is a celebration of Jamaican culture and flavor. Founded in 2011, it was created to show the world how rich and exciting Jamaican cuisine really is and it has delivered on this promise since day one!\n\nAt Kingston Kitchen, the atmosphere is always animated and welcoming. What makes :u[it] really special is the sense of community. People from all backgrounds come together to eat, dance, laugh, and connect. \n\nThe day of the festival, local chefs, street food truck owners, and young food enthusiasts come together to serve :u[their] best dishes, from smoky jerk chicken to traditional classics with a modern twist. \n\nKingston Kitchen proves that food is more than just something you eat, :u[it’s] an experience.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.** [3 marks]\n\nIf you’re into **[-1-]** food and cool vibes, Kingston Kitchen in Jamaica is a festival worth checking out. The event is more than just a food festival; it also celebrates **[-2-]** Jamaica’s heritage. Founded in 2011, Kingston Kitchen has highlighted how **[-3-]** and vibrant Jamaican cooking can be and has stayed true to its mission from the very beginning.\n\n- A.  $\\hspace{1em}$interesting \n\n- B.  $\\hspace{1em}$well-known\n\n- C.  $\\hspace{1em}$diverse\n\n- D.  $\\hspace{1em}$special\n\n- E.  $\\hspace{1em}$traditional\n\n- F.  $\\hspace{1em}$incredible\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.**[3 marks]\n\n4.$  \\hspace{1em}$ …makes it really special…\\\n\n5.$  \\hspace{1em}$ …to serve their best dishes…\\\n\n6.$  \\hspace{1em}$ …it’s an experience.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “If you love good food and good vibes”\\\n\nThe original paragraph describes the food and atmosphere very positively, which is why the summary uses a strong adjective: *incredible* to keep the same enthusiastic tone.\n\n- **Option A** is incorrect. *Interesting* suggests mild curiosity, while the original text shows strong, positive language.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “celebrates the vibrant flavors and culinary heritage of Jamaica.”\\\n\nThe original paragraph highlights Jamaica’s heritage in food and culture, referring to customs and practices passed down over time, which aligns with the meaning of *traditional*.\n\n- **Option B** is incorrect. *Well-known* only means familiar to many people and does not express cultural depth or importance.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “the diverse culinary traditions that make Jamaica’s food scene so unique.”\\\n\nThe original paragraph clearly states that Jamaican cuisine includes many different traditions, which matches the meaning of *diverse* in the paraphrase.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. *Special* is vague and does not clearly communicate the idea of diverse culinary influences.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Kingston Kitchen}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Also accept*: the festival\n\n\\\n\n*It* refers back to Kingston Kitchen, the place introduced at the start of the paragraph.\\\n\n\"The atmosphere\" is confusing because it is the noun closest to the pronoun \"it.\" However, in this paragraph, the writer is describing Kingston Kitchen as a whole, not just one feature of it. The atmosphere is one quality of the place.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{local chefs, street food truck owners, and young food enthusiasts \n\n}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Their* refers to the people listed immediately before in the sentence: the local chefs, street food truck owners, and young food enthusiasts who serve the dishes.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{food}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *It* refers to food, which the writer says is more than just something to eat; it is an experience."},{"ref":"EB0157","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0157\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a food blogger explaining the ways different cultures eat.**\n\n:audio{#e6c4e3e3-fb4a-48ce-9766-b38595e7010a aid=\"e6c4e3e3-fb4a-48ce-9766-b38595e7010a\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.**[4 marks]\n\nWith the development of agricultural practices, the use of knives and forks at the table became the **[-1-]** method for people to cut and eat larger pieces of food. \n\nThis also reflects the greater **[-2-]** of meat in Western societies.  \n\nEating with hands has been **[-3-]** by many other cultures.\n\nTouching the food **[-4-]** an enhanced dining experience for those who eat with hands.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Which of the following is not a reason for cultural differences in eating habits?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Geographical location\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Societal influences\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Personal preferences","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{predominant}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Predominant* means being the most noticeable or the largest in number.  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Most common\" is a synonym.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{consumption}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *To consume* means to eat or use up. *Consumption* in this text refers to the act of eating, and *intake* (take-in) is a close synonym which is the amount of something (food, air) taken into the body.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{embraced}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Embrace* means to accept or welcome, and *adopt* is a very close synonym, indicating a practice or habit that is used by many people within a culture.  \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fosters}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *To foster* means to encourage the growth or development of something. (for example: one could foster a child, or a good habit) To *cultivate* also means to try to improve or develop something (cultivate tolerance, for example).\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Personal preferences are not mentioned within the text as a reason for eating in a particular way.  The text states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$historical, environmental, and cultural factors” are the main reasons we eat the way we do.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$environmental factors” are given as a major influence in how we eat, and geographical location will greatly influence the environment in which a culture develops. Therefore, **A** is incorrect.\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cultural factors” are stated as one of the most important elements in shaping how we eat, and societal influences is a synonym for that phrase. Therefore, **B** is incorrect."},{"ref":"EB0158","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0158\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on greeting guests.**\n\n:audio{#5a120cfe-47ff-4b3b-b700-60ff926aa0bb aid=\"5a120cfe-47ff-4b3b-b700-60ff926aa0bb\"}\n\n**Choose the three true statements.**[3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$In all cultures, greetings must include physical contact.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The level of eye contact in a greeting is influenced by societal norms.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Bowing is a universal way of greeting guests.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The formality of a greeting depends on the social rank of the person being welcomed.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Stylized greeting gestures include bowing and handshakes.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$All cultures use the same type of hand gestures when greeting.\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.**\n\nGreetings are intended to convey complex emotions such as **[-4-]**.    [1 mark]\n\nA host's greeting should communicate **[-5-]**.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, D, E}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$degree of eye contact is [are] dependent upon cultural expectation”\\\n\n*Societal norms* are the rules or expectations that define behavior in a group or society. They are a synonym for *cultural expectations*.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Verbal greetings may be short and spontaneous, or formal and composed depending on the situation and the status of the person being welcomed.”\\\n\n*Status* is the social position of a person. Status may be based on their profession, family connection, wealth, etc. *Social rank* is a synonym for *status*. The level of formality in greetings will rise with higher levels of status of arriving guests.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Many gestures are highly stylized, including bowing, kneeling, handshakes, ... kisses”\\\n\nThe text provides a long list of gestures that may be included in greeting a guest but may vary (be different), depending on the societal norms of the culture.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*   A, C, F\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...dependent upon cultural expectation, as is the amount of physical contact.” \\\n\nNot all cultures include touching a guest as part of greeting them. Bowing and kneeling - two examples given in the text - do not include physical contact.\n\n- **C**: Bowing is listed as one of the gestures which may be included in greeting a guest, but the text does not say it is universal (meaning that it is used everywhere and by all cultures).\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...may include...handshakes, hand gestures...”\n\nThere are many types of hand gestures, with handshakes given as one specific type in the text, but the text also states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$may include\", indicating that not all gestures are necessarily used by all cultures.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{warmth, joy and respect}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Convey* and *express* are synonyms which mean to show or demonstrate.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{genuine happiness}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Convey* and *communicate* are also synonyms."},{"ref":"EB0159","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0159\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a gallery employee discussing the modernization of traditional crafts.**\n\n:audio{#4b530e7d-a65b-494d-bcaf-4be9c9d75041 aid=\"4b530e7d-a65b-494d-bcaf-4be9c9d75041\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The people producing traditional crafts are referred to in three different ways in the text.  Give **two** examples of the words used.    [2 marks]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What innovation has ensured the continued success of traditional crafts in modern society?    [1 mark]\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.**\n\nArtisans preserve the **[-3-]** of crafts.    [1 mark]\n\nThe blending of ancient craft with modern taste is referred to as **[-4-]**  in the text.    [1 mark]\n\nThe continued **[-5-]** of modern crafts has been enabled by online platforms.$    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any two of the following answers, in any order:* \n\n- $\\textrm{artisans}$\n\n- $\\textrm{craftsmen}$\n\n- $\\textrm{makers}$        [3 marks]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...artisans are blending time-honored techniques...”\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...craftsmen adapting their work...”\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...connect makers to a global audience...”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe people engaged in producing crafts may be referred to in several ways, with three examples given in the text.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{digital platforms}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Digital platforms connect makers to a global audience, showcasing their work and ensuring the continued relevance and sustainability of these treasured creations.” \\\n\n\\\n\nBy connecting to a larger (*global*) audience online, craftsmen demonstrate (*showcase*) their work and expand their customer base. This allows them to continue to produce work that people want and love, while maintaining their livelihood (having work and earning money). *Innovation* (in the question) is a noun referring to a new idea or product.  In this text, digital platforms are the innovation.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{history and symbolism}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$while preserving the history and symbolism of the original products.”\\\n\n\\\n\nCraftsmen are blending the meaning and tradition of products with the interests of a modern customer.  For example: patterns and colors which are symbolic of a culture could be used in a modern item, such as a mouse pad.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fusion}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Fusion* is the process of joining two things together to form something new.  In this text, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$time-honored techniques” (the way things are made) are blended with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$modern design and expression” to create a new form of craft.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{relevance (and sustainability)}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Digital platforms connect makers to a global audience, showcasing their work and ensuring the continued relevance and sustainability of these treasured creations.”\\\n\n\\\n\n*Relevance* describes how meaningful or useful something is to what is happening at the present time.  Traditional crafts may not have as much relevance to a modern audience as they did to an audience 100 years ago, because society has changed.\\\n\n*Sustainability* is the ability to be kept (maintained) at a certain level or rate.\\\n\nWith online marketing and sales, craftsmen can connect with customers and keep producing modern crafts."},{"ref":"EB0260","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0260\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nDays before October 31st, children start getting anxious about an event that takes place every year on that precise date. Halloween was originally a Celtic celebration that was brought to America by the first European settlers. \n\nThe Celtics used to believe that on October 31st, creepy ghosts and dead people came back to the living world; therefore, they dressed up like devils and put candles in front of their houses. This tradition was merged with the Christian celebration of All Saints, which is held on November 1st.\n\nAs time went by, people took up this custom and kept on dressing like evil creatures for fun. Nowadays, children do not just wear scary costumes; they go to fancy dress shops and choose among different characters, such as fairies, heroes, princesses, or even robots.\n\nFamilies set up haunted house decorations like mysterious jack-o-lanterns, garlands, skeletons, enchanted cauldrons, and creepy tombstones. Once houses are decorated and children are dressed up in their costumes, the celebration starts. Everyone goes trick-or-treating and gets a ton of candy to celebrate Halloween.\n\n**Find the word or phrase in the text which means the following:** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$frightening    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$combined    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$adopted    [1 mark]\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Kids wear spooky costumes during Halloween.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Houses are decorated with flags and garlands.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$It is a custom to eat sweets on Halloween.   [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{creepy}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$creepy ghosts and dead people came back to the living world”\\\n\nThe word *creepy* is a synonym of the word *frightening*, it means that the ghosts are scary.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{merged}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This tradition was merged with the Christian celebration of All Saints”\\\n\nThe word *merge* is a synonym of the word *combine*. This means that two or more things are joined together. In this context, the two things would be the Celtic tradition and Christian influences.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{took up}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people took up this custom”\\\n\nThe phrasal verb *take up* means to start doing a new hobby or habit. In this context people *adopted* or *started* celebrating this event. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: children do not just wear scary costumes\\\n\nThe text mentions that children not only wear *spooky* or *scary* costumes but also dress up as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fairies, heroes, princesses, or even robots.” which are not scary.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: haunted house decorations\\\n\nThe text offers a list of decorations used in houses: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mysterious jack-o-lanterns, garlands, skeletons, enchanted cauldrons, and creepy tombstones.” Flags are not included in this list, and they are not scary.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: gets a ton of candy to celebrate Halloween.\\\n\nThe text mentions that everyone gets candy to celebrate Halloween."},{"ref":"EB0261","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0261\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**Remembrance Day**\n\nRemembrance Day takes place in Britain every year on the Sunday closest to November 11th. This commemoration marks the day the First World War ended: at 11:00 a.m. on the 11th day of the 11th month in the year 1918. The reason for this memorial service is to remember all those who died while fighting in World Wars I and II.\n\nPrior to the actual event, many schools and organizations rehearse parades and prepare the traditional paper poppy flowers, $\\underline{\\textrm{which}}$ people wear on their lapels during the event. Due to $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ bright red colour, the poppy symbolizes the hardship and sacrifice of soldiers. They can also be seen, even today, in battlefields around Europe.\n\nOn the actual day, war veterans join the different parades and proudly display $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ medals. Children lay poppy wreaths on the monuments and clap at the soldiers. In the evening, a remembrance service is held at the Royal Albert Hall, where people sing hymns and give speeches. \n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does \"takes place\" mean in context of the text?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$occurs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$is cancelled\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$is located\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$reveals\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the word  “mark” mean in the context of the text?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$recognize\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$indicate\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$underline\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$prepare\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.** \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... $\\underline{\\textrm{which}}$ people wear on...    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... Due to $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ bright red colour,...    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... display $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ medals..    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The phrasal verb *take place* means to happen or *occur* when referring to an event. In this context, the commemoration *occurs* in Britain.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is incorrect. The phrase is *cancelled* means that an event is not going to occur. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is incorrect, as the phrase *is located* means that something *can be found* in a place. Taking into consideration the context, the event occurs, but it is not an item that *can be found* somewhere. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is incorrect. The word *reveal* means to make something visible that was hidden. According to the text, the commemoration is not revealed since it was not a secret. \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The word *indicate* means to point out a specific date. In the context of the text, the event is commemorated on that specific moment and date because it indicates the end of World War I.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is incorrect. The word *recognize* means to acknowledge. In the context of this text, nobody denies the end of World War I, therefore, there is no need to recognize it.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is incorrect. The word *underline* means to draw a line under a word in a text. Its literal meaning can be confused with the literal meaning of the word *mark*, which also means to draw a line. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is incorrect. The word *prepare* means to get something ready for an event, but there is no connection with the idea of the word *mark* with the preparation of the event. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(paper) poppy flowers}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... prepare the traditional paper poppy flowers, $\\underline{\\textrm{which}}$ people wear on their lapels .”\\\n\nThe word *which* refers back to the words $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$paper poppy flowers”. People first prepare the flowers and then place them on their lapels since they are a symbol of this commemoration. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{poppy (flowers)}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$..., the poppy symbolizes the hardship and the sacrifice of the soldiers.”\\\n\nThe word *their* refers to the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$poppy flowers” because people connect the red colour of the poppy with the blood of the soldiers.  \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{war veterans}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$war veterans join the different parades and proudly display $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ medals.”\\\n\nWar veterans participate in parades. When they march in the parade, they show the medals they have obtained."},{"ref":"EB0262","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0262\n\n**Maximum mark: 5** \n\nDear Tom,\n\nHow are you? I hope you are fine. I have so much to tell you about my trip to England that I don't know where to begin! Visiting England has been amazing so far!\n\nI am staying with the Thompsons; the mom is called Susan, the dad is Paul, and Jenny is their 12-year-old girl. I am living just five blocks from Buckingham Palace. It is fantastic since I can visit it almost every day. One day, I saw King Charles' car from the window of my bedroom. You know, I'm a Royal fan, and I feel :u[thrilled] at the simple idea of :u[catching a glimpse] of the King or Queen. \n\nI learnt a lot about traditions and customs, too! One day, Susan fixed dinner, and she prepared a :u[mouthwatering] Shepherd's pie. Let me tell you that it was the best dish I have ever tasted, and when we get together, we are going to try to do it. Susan told me that the recipe had been passed down through generations and that her mom had taught her the “secret” ingredient. First, you have to make the mashed potato, and then you prepare the filling with onions, carrots, minced lamb, and then… you add the “secret” ingredient: tomato paste. Making it sounds easy, but it is not! \n\nAs soon as I get back, I'll phone you to tell you more about the recipe and my trip. Take care!\n\nWith love, Susan.\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$fine |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$well |\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$fantastic |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$excited |\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$thrilled |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$joyful |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$enthusiastic |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$ wonderful |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$healthy |\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in the text.**  \n\n4.$  \\hspace{1em}$ The instructions of how to cook the dish were transmitted...    [1 mark]\n\n5.$  \\hspace{1em}$ Preparing the recipe seems...    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- In the phrase “I hope you are fine,” the replacement word *well* fits naturally and is used in greetings.\n\n- **Option H** is confusing, but *healthy* is more specific and not usually used in greetings.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Wonderful* matches the positive meaning of *fantastic* in describing the experience.\n\n- **Option C** is incorrect. *Joyful* describes a feeling of great happiness.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Excited* clearly matches the emotion expressed when thinking about seeing the King or Queen.\n\n- **Option E** is confusing. *Enthusiastic* describes attitude or interest rather than emotion.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{... through generations}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... the recipe had been passed down through generations”. \\\n\n\\\n\nThe *instructions* refer to the *recipe*. The phrase *were transmitted* is similar in meaning to the phrasal verb *passed down* used in the text.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{... easy, (but it is not!)}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Making it sounds easy, but it is not!”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe phrase *making it* refers to *preparing the recipe* and the word *seems* can be replaced by the word *sounds* in the context of this text. The sentence “but it is not!” is optional because it does not change the original idea."},{"ref":"EB0409","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0409\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a news feature about an unusual annual competition.**\n\n:audio{#880c08f2-ccb1-42c0-a488-f6aebad97a0d aid=\"880c08f2-ccb1-42c0-a488-f6aebad97a0d\"}\n\n**Choose the five true statements.** [5 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The competition takes place on the same day every year.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Ashton is a large, well-known city in England.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Participants come to Ashton from all over the globe.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The championship has been running for less than four decades.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Conkers are roughly the same size as a tennis ball.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$A match ends when one of the conkers can no longer continue. \n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Players must thread their conker onto a piece of string of a specific length.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Participants are free to prepare their conker however they wish before competing.\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1.4em}$Both players strike at the same time during a match.\n\n- J.$\\hspace{1.2em}$The last competitor with an unbroken conker earns a special title.","markScheme":"$1 - 5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, F, G, J}$        [5 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On the first Sunday of every October\"\\\n\nThe text confirms the event always falls on the same day, making it a fixed annual date.\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$People from over thirty countries arrive\"\\\n\nThe text states that competitors travel from more than thirty different countries, showing the event has worldwide reach.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The matches continue until only one conker is left\"\\\n\nThe text confirms that play continues until one conker is destroyed, meaning only the surviving conker advances to the next match.\n\n- **G**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Each conker must be threaded onto 20 centimeters of string\"\\\n\nThe text specifies an exact string length, confirming that competitors must follow a precise measurement.\n\n- **J**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the owner crowned the King - or Queen - Conker\"\\\n\nThe text states that the final winner is crowned as either the King or Queen, which makes this statement correct.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, D, E, H, I\n\n- **B**: The text describes Ashton as a quiet village, not a city, so this statement is incorrect about its size.\n\n- **D**: The competition started in 1965, which means it has been running for over five decades.\n\n- **E**: The text compares conkers to a ping-pong ball, which is significantly smaller than a tennis ball.\n\n- **H**: The text states that treating or hardening a conker before a match is not allowed, so competitors do not have free choice in how they prepare.\n\n- **I**: The text explains that players take turns swinging, so they do not hit simultaneously."},{"ref":"EB0410","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0410\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a young woman talking about a traditional skill she has learned.**\n\n:audio{#d5c1e3ac-af35-4e5c-8997-4cf696f982ed aid=\"d5c1e3ac-af35-4e5c-8997-4cf696f982ed\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What has the speaker done every day since Kara arrived?    [1 mark] \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the wingspan of the speaker's eagle?    [1 mark] \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker do when Kara lands?    [1 mark] \n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$In the speaker's community, eagle training had become...    [1 mark] \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$something done by both men and women.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$something taught by grandmothers to their grandchildren.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$something reserved for men only.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The girls who train eagles in Western Mongolia see themselves as...    [1 mark] \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$continuing a very ancient tradition.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$unusual pioneers in their community.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$following their grandmothers' example.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fed her by hand}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I have fed her by hand every day since then\"\\\n\nThe speaker says Kara came to them as a chick, and she has fed her by hand every day.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{almost two meters}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$her wingspan is almost two meters\"\\\n\nThe speaker describes how big her eagle is. Kara weighs 5 kilograms and her wingspan - the distance from the tip of one wing to the tip of the other when they are stretched out - is almost two meters.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{stand (very) still}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I have learned to stand very still when she takes off and lands on me\"\\\n\nThe speaker mentions that when the eagle takes off and lands, she has to stay very still.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{c}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$eagles were only handled by men\"\\\n\nThe speaker states that the tradition was exclusively male. \n\n- *Incorrect options:* \n\n- **Option A** is incorrect.  The text says men and women worked with eagles only in the very old days, not something that was currently done. \n\n- **Option B** makes a mistake about the grandmother's role. She encouraged the speaker but did not teach the tradition to her.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$we are proud to be part of something very old that is becoming new again\"\\\n\nThis highlights the idea that the girls feel connected to an ancient tradition. \n\n- *Incorrect options:* \n\n- **Option B** contradicts the text - the speaker says they do not see themselves as unusual. \n\n- **Option C** is only true for the speaker, not the group."},{"ref":"EB0411","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0411\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a live radio report about an annual tradition on the River Thames.**\n\n:audio{#a3392806-10c9-4aed-aced-c060ed6ea131 aid=\"a3392806-10c9-4aed-aced-c060ed6ea131\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.**\n\n**Program Notes**\n\nSwan Upping is an annual **[-1-]** of the swan population on the River Thames. This centuries-old event takes place each July and has been a tradition since the **[-2-]**. Traveling upstream by boat, teams work together to **[-3-]** and count each swan found along a set section of the river. Our reporter watched as a **[-4-]** was carefully lifted from the water, while the team recorded the bird's **[-5-]** and checked it for injuries.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How long does the Swan Upping count last?    [1 mark] \n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What happens to swans that are found to be injured?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{count}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Swan Upping - an annual count of the swan population on the river\"\\ \n\nSwan Upping is described as an official count of the swan population on the Thames.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{twelfth century}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a tradition that has taken place every July since the twelfth century\" \\\n\nThe reporter tells us that Swan Upping has been happening since the 1100s, ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the twelfth century”), making it a very old tradition.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:* \n\n- $\\textrm{catch}$\n\n- $\\textrm{check}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Teams of people travel upriver to catch, check, and count every swan\" \\\n\nThe census requires the birds to be counted and to be checked to see if they are healthy.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{baby swan}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'm watching my team lift a baby swan, out of the water\" \\\n\nThe reporter gets to watch the capture of a baby swan, and tells us what is happening in real time.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{weight}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They're recording its weight and checking for injuries\" \\\n\nOnce the bird is out of the water, the team records how heavy it is and makes sure it is healthy and with no injuries before releasing it.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{five days}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The count runs for five days\" \\\n\nThe reporter states clearly that the entire Swan Upping count takes five days to complete.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{passed to (wildlife) vets}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Accept other wording with similar meaning.\\\n\n\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$those cases will be passed to wildlife vets\" \\\n\nSwans found to be injured are not treated on the spot - they are referred to wildlife vets for professional care."}]},{"name":"Holidays & Travel","questions":[{"ref":"EB0019","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0019\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nGoing abroad on holidays is a once-in-a-lifetime chance to fully immerse oneself in a different culture. Unusual sights, sounds, and smells of another country greet passengers upon arrival. Experiencing local customs and traditions is one of the most rewarding benefits of traveling. For instance, trying traditional delicacies, attending a local festival, or simply being immersed in the everyday life, such experiences broaden your horizons and give you deeper insights into the world.\n\nWhen it comes to cultural exposure, learning a few words in the right language may help to bridge the gap and allow you to get a deeper feeling for how someone lives. Even the courtesy of greeting and expressing gratitude may lead to a new friend or story.\n\nBeyond the cultural panorama, travel offers a thrilling opportunity to explore marvellous landscapes, historical sites, and architectural wonders. Whether you prefer busy urban areas or the serenity of natural scenery, you can explore each differently. Sometimes, merely crossing the threshold into a new environment can change one's outlook on things. Do not underestimate the power of travelling; you will be flying back home in a state of awe and appreciation of life.\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.**\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$unusual|$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$national|\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$local|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$meaningful|\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$rewarding|$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$unique|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$strange|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$regional|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$entertaining|\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in paragraphs 2 - 3**\n\n4.$  \\hspace{1em}$ \n\nLearning a few words in the local language can help visitors understand…\n\n [1 mark]\n\n5.$  \\hspace{1em}$ \n\nTraveling is an opportunity to discover…    [1 mark]\n\n6.$  \\hspace{1em}$ \n\nVisiting new places can change a person’s …    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- In the text, “unusual sights, sounds, and smells” refers to things that are different from what travelers normally experience. *Unique* is a good replacement word because it focuses on distinctiveness.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. *Strange* suggests something is weird. The text has a positive tone, so *strange* is too negative.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Local customs and traditions” means traditions that belong to a specific area or region. Regional describes things connected to a particular place.\n\n- **Option A** is confusing. *National* relates to a particular country, not a region. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- In the text, “rewarding benefits of traveling” refers to experiences that add value to a person’s life. *Meaningful* is a good replacement word becasue it reflects this benefit. \n\n- **Option F** is incorrect. *Entertaining* only focuses on the amusement, not the deeper benefits implied in *rewarding*.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{how someone lives\n\n}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “learning a few words in the right language… get a deeper feeling for how someone lives”\n\n- The text mentions that learning some words might “bridge the gap”  (help you feel closer) with the locals and help you understand the way people live.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ *Any one of:*\n\n-  $\\textrm{marvellous landscapes }$   [1 mark]\n\n-  $\\textrm{historical sites\n\n}$   [1 mark]\n\n-  $\\textrm{architectural wonders\n\n}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Beyond the cultural panorama, travel offers a thrilling opportunity to explore marvellous landscapes, historical sites, and architectural wonders”\\\n\nThe text offers three possible options for this sentence. *Discover* and *explore* have a similar meaning in the context. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{outlook on things}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Sometimes, merely crossing the threshold into a new environment can change one’s outlook on things”\\\n\nThe text mentions that new experiences affect how people see life."},{"ref":"EB0020","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0020\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nAs you embark on your journeys around the globe, always keep in mind to tread respectfully on the Earth. Embracing sustainable initiatives is key to preserving the beauty and richness of our planet, along with safeguarding our legacy. To ensure enjoyment without causing unnecessary strain on visiting destinations and to make your travels have a positive impact on both the environment and the communities you visit, it is vital to be mindful of your actions.\n\nHere are a few guidelines for travelling responsibly:  \n\n**[-1-]**\\\n\nSeek out eco-friendly lodgings that minimise their environmental impact.\n\n**[-2-]**\\\n\nUse sustainable means of transportation and be mindful of water usage. Besides, do not carry plastic use-and-throw items such as disposables. Instead, pack some reusables, like water bottles and shopping bags, so as to cut down the generation of waste.\n\n**[-3-]**\\\n\nPurchase at local businesses, contributing to the economy of the local community.\n\n**[-4-]**\\\n\nObserve the rules in national parks and wildlife reserves. Do not litter, and refrain from disturbing animals.\n\n**[-5-]**\\\n\nDress modestly, learn a few native words to show understanding and appreciation, and be sensitive to cultural norms and traditions. Avoid behaving intrusively and disrespectfully.\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [5 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Support local entrepreneurs\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Safeguard natural environments\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Language tips\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Choose sustainable accommodations\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Hacks to reduce impact on the environment\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Wildlife watching\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Respect local cultures\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Pack responsibly\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1em}$Choose lodgings according to your budget\n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraph 1 which means the following:**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$impact that is unjustified    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$conscious    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- In the paragraph, *eco-friendly* means *sustainable* and *lodgings* is a synonym of *accomodations*. Looking for these types of places to stay minimises the impact of tourists on the environment.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The paragraph gives a few specific tricks (*hacks*) to travel responsibly. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This is the only subheading that refers to the economy of local communities. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The paragraph points out some ways in which travellers should behave and act to protect the natural habitat of the local wildlife. This part specifically deals with the natural spaces of the wildlife. Not bothering (*distubing*) animals is only one of the suggestions.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- According to this paragraph, travellers can show appreciation and respect for the local culture if they make an effort to communicate with locals in their native language and if they don't offend them by wearing inappropriate clothes, breaking their cultural norms, or mocking their traditions.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* C, F, H, I\n\n- **C**: The paragraph about showing appreciation and respect for local cultures offers more than one way in which this can be done. The idea of learning a bit about the language is only one suggestion to show appreciation for locals.\n\n- **F**: The paragraph about *not disturbing animals* in their natural habitat does not say that the simple and only act of watching wildlife contributes to the preservation of natural environments. The text also suggests aspects related to respecting rules and not throwing rubbish to respect their natural habits.\n\n- **H**: The paragraph about ways to reduce the impact on the environment when travelling makes suggestions about packing,   but not with the purpose of suggesting that travellers should carry less weight. It only mentions packing resusable items to contribute to preserving the environment. \n\n- **I**: The subheading “Choose lodgings according to your budget\" means to stay at places you can afford according to the money you can spend on the trip. This idea of the traveller's budget does not connect directly with the concept of choosing a lodging to protect the environment. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{unnecessary strain}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Unjustified* is a synonym of *unnecessary* and *impact* is a good replacement for *strain* since this last word in the context refers to a strong and negative change that travellers may cause on a destination when entertaining themselves. \n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Mindful}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Conscious* is a synonym of *mindful*. Both mean *to be aware of something*."},{"ref":"EB0021","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0021\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nOctober 25th, 2022\n\nParadise Hotel\\\n\nHotel Manager\n\nDear Sir or Madam,\n\nI am writing to express my absolute indignation with our recent stay at your hotel last week. \n\nOur room was located on the 7th floor, where there was a massive electrical failure shortly after arrival. Moreover, there were no well-trained hotel workers on duty to fix the issue for days. Hence we were moved to a first-floor room, as it was the only available accommodation in that hotel. However, what really caused our annoyance was that on the first floor there were construction works in progress in some rooms during the entire stay, and all day long, the sound was unbearable as well as the smell of freshly painted walls.\n\nTo make matters worse,  the front desk receptionists were visibly irritated and overwhelmed by the many complaints from other guests. Far from being resourceful, they were unresponsive when we expressed our displeasure. \n\nThis chain of complications did not allow my wife and I to have a proper rest, and our exhaustion forced us to cancel some of the activities we had planned to do.\n\nWe demand a refund and we look forward to a prompt reply.\n\nFaithfully,\n\nPeter Bolt\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify your answer using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Unfortunately, an electrical failure had occurred on the seventh floor before their arrival.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The hotel failed to take proactive measures to solve the electrical incident.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The noise from the repairs on the first floor significantly interfered with guests' relaxation.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer of the letter and his wife were the only guests who felt dissatisfied.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The hotel staff could not offer solutions to the writer of the letter and his wife's complaints.     [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer expects the hotel to    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$apologise to him and his wife.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$compensate him with a new vacation stay.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$give back the money they paid for their stay.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$None of the above\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the primary purpose of the letter?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To praise the hotel's facilities\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To express his dissatisfaction with his stay at the hotel.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To request a refund.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$To give details about his displeasure with his stay and to request a reimbursement.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$shortly after arrival\"\n\n- This means the electrical problem occurred a short time after the couple arrived at the hotel.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$no well-trained hotel workers on duty to fix the issue for days.\"\\\n\n*well-trained workers* means workers with the skills or abilities to repair the problem. *On duty* is a key phrase since it means the workers were not currently at the hotel (their workplace) to do their job.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$all day long, the sound was unbearable\" \\\n\n*Unbearable* means that the noise from the construction works in progress was too loud, impossible to tolerate, and, as a result, the guests could not sleep, rest, or relax properly. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:**  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$by the many complaints from other guests\"\\\n\n*Complaints* means expressing dissatisfaction. Many other people staying at the hotel were expressing this feeling to the hotel staff at the reception desk. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$they were unresponsive\"\\\n\n*Unresponsive* suggests the staff at the reception desk ignored the guests' complaints about the problems they were experiencing at the hotel. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This is the only valid answer since when the writer closes the letter, he specifically says that he demands a *refund* which means to be given the money he paid for his stay back. \n\n- **Option A** is incorrect since, even though it is implied that the writer would like the hotel to apologise for the inconveniences, the writer does not explicitly mention this. As regards **option B**, there is no evidence in the letter that the writer wants to stay again at the hotel for a new vacation. \n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Option D** is the correct option since, in the letter, the writer describes in detail all the problems he and his wife suffered during his stay at the hotel. Actually, he also specifically says he feels $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$absolute indignation\" and he demands a reimbursement when he uses the word *refund*. *Reimbursement* and *refund* are synonyms, and they mean *to be paid back*. \n\n- **Option A** is completely invalid as *praise* means *to express approval or admiration* and the writer clearly does not approve of any of the aspects of his stay at the hotel. \\\n\n**Option B** touches on one important purpose of the letter (to express his disapproval), but the writer also expects the hotel to return the money he paid for the stay since he is not satisfied, and this idea is left out of this option. **Option C** is wrong because it only focuses on the writer's demand for his money back."},{"ref":"EB0119","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0119\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a flight attendant sharing their daily routine.**\n\n:audio{#9378a6a7-244c-46c0-a7f7-5ad6e1e3d359 aid=\"9378a6a7-244c-46c0-a7f7-5ad6e1e3d359\"}\n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.** [4 marks]\n\nFlight attendants have many different responsibilities:\n\nReviewing **[-1-]** is one of the first tasks flight attendants complete during their day.\n\nThe flight **[-2-]** ensures that the cabin and safety equipment are ready before passengers board.\n\nGreeting passengers and making them feel comfortable are examples of the **[-3-]** aspect of the job.\n\nHandling crises, like **[-4-]** and turbulence.\n\n**Answer the following question.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What aspects of the job does the speaker enjoy? Give **two** examples.      [2 marks]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Safety procedures}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “The crew takes time to carefully review safety procedures”\n- Safety procedures are reviewed by the crew as part of the process of getting an airplane ready for flight before passengers arrive.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{crew}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “The crew takes time to carefully review safety procedures”\n- *Crew* is a word used to describe a group of people who work together, and is specifically used for the people who work on ships or airplanes.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{hospitality}$   [1 mark]\n\n-  “much of my job is hospitality: greeting passengers, serving meals, and making sure everyone is comfortable”\n- *Hospitality* is the act of being friendly and welcoming to guests and visitors. A part of the flight attendant's job is to ensure passengers are well taken care of.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{medical situations}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I am also trained to handle emergencies - from turbulence to medical situations”\n- *Emergencies* and *crises* in this context have a similar meaning. The speaker mentions they are trained to deal with issues that might happen during flight.\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$*Answers can be written in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{meeting new people}$\n-   $\\textrm{seeing new places}$\n\n- “But I love meeting new people and seeing new places, so it’s the perfect job for me…”\n- The speaker mentions at the end of the extract that meeting people and seeing new places makes this “the perfect job” which indicates these are aspects they enjoy.\n [2 marks]"},{"ref":"EB0120","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0120\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on the benefits of a staycation: staying at home for a holiday instead of traveling.**\n\n:audio{#454e26d1-0362-496d-b622-9ff12ab1f395 aid=\"454e26d1-0362-496d-b622-9ff12ab1f395\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.** [3 marks]\n\nA staycation can be the ideal alternative for you if the thought of going on holiday feels more stressful than relaxing. Booking costly airline tickets, and struggling in **[-1-]** to reach an **[-2-]** can turn a holiday into a terrible experience. Sometimes, the stress of escaping your  **[-3-]** can be worse than the rest you’ll get.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Which activity is suggested for a staycation? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Taking a long road trip\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Having a picnic in your backyard\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Staying in a hotel\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the text suggest you might explore during a staycation? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$New places to eat and local neighborhoods\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Other countries and exotic cuisines\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$New cities far from your home\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is NOT mentioned as a staycation activity? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Visiting local museums\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Hosting a dinner party at home\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Exploring new neighborhoods\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is one advantage of a staycation compared to a traditional holiday? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$You don’t have to spend money on food.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$You avoid stressful travel-related situations.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$You won’t have to leave your house.\n\n$8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the main focus of the text? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To give alternatives to traditional holidays\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To discourage people from traveling to crowded airports\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To introduce staycations to people considering holiday options","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{traffic}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “booking a hotel, packing a suitcase, and then fighting traffic”\n- The text mentions *fighting traffic* which is similar in meaning to *struggling in traffic*. Both expressions imply that traffic can be difficult and it might be hard to get to the airport, as there might be traffic jams.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{airport}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “fighting traffic to get to an overcrowded airport”\n- *Reach* and *get to* are synonyms, both meaning to arrive. The text mentions that getting to the airport con be stressful.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{daily life}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “The stress of traveling to “get away” from your daily life can be more harmful than the rest you get while you’re away.”\n- The phrasal verb *get away* is a synonym of *escape*. The text mentions you take holidays to escape your daily life.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “you could set up a cozy outdoor picnic in your backyard.”\n\n- The phrasal verb *set up* means to make arrangements so something can take place.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: There is no mention of a road trip as part of a staycation.  “Fighting traffic” was given as a reason to not choose a traditional holiday that requires a drive to a busy airport.\n- **Option C**: Staying in a hotel is what is done in a traditional holiday, but a staycation is a holiday you take in your own home.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “…spend your time exploring neighborhoods you’ve never had time to see, or trying new restaurants…”\n\n- The text mentions a “Staycation” is an opportunity to explore your own place, and discover new neighborhoods.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **Option B**: Although the text suggests trying new restaurants, there is no mention of “exotic cuisines”.  There is also no mention of traveling to another country.\n- **Option C**: The text focuses on activities in or near your home, not far “turn your home into an inviting retreat… a cozy outdoor picnic in your backyard.” It suggests “exploring neighborhoods you’ve never had time to see… or visiting the local museum”, both of which are close to home.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text suggests both visiting museums and exploring new neighborhoods, but there is no mention of hosting dinner parties.  The text does suggest “an outdoor picnic in your backyard”, but that is not the same as a dinner party. As a party is a more sophisticated activity than a picnic.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: One suggestion for things to do includes “visiting the local museum.”\n- **Option C**: Another suggestion given in the text is “exploring neighborhoods you’ve never had time to see”\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text suggests that stress can be a negative side-effect of traditional holidays that require travel, and lists several elements of planning a trip that may be stressful. “The stress of traveling to ‘’get away’’ from your daily life can be more harmful than the rest you get while you’re away.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: Food is mentioned in the text twice: as a picnic, and as a suggestion to try local restaurants.  Food is something that will need to be bought wherever a person is, so a staycation does not remove the need to spend money on food.\n- **Option C**: The text suggests several activities that would require a person on a staycation to leave their house: “exploring neighborhoods you’ve never had time to see, or trying new restaurants, or visiting the local museum.” A staycation does not mean that a person would never leave their home for the length of the holiday.\n\n$8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- After listing some of the stressors that may be associated with a traditional holiday, the staycation is introduced: “Maybe a “staycation” is a good option for you.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: The text introduces only one alternative to traditional holidays, therefore it is not the main purpose of the text.\n- ** Option B**: The text lists some of the inconvenient or unpleasant aspects of traditional holiday travel, including: “fighting traffic to get to an overcrowded airport.” However, the purpose of the text is not to stop people from going to airports, but to reconsider the purpose of their holiday.  If it is to relax and not be stressed, then the staycation is an appropriate alternative to traditional holidays."},{"ref":"EB0121","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0121\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a reporter explain the attraction of regional cuisines for food-loving travelers.**\n\n:audio{#db2f3256-19c0-4a00-aa10-ef316d7e62d3 aid=\"db2f3256-19c0-4a00-aa10-ef316d7e62d3\"}\n\n1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the four correct statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Regional cuisine reflects the history and culture of the people in a location.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Regional cuisine can be easily duplicated in other parts of the world.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Many travelers revisit places specifically to enjoy the local food.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Regional cuisine is influenced by the environment and local beliefs.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Regional cuisine is unrelated to the cultural history of an area.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Travelers do not consider regional food when planning a holiday to a new location.\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Regional cuisine is a blend of local traditions, produce, and residents' appreciation of history.\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The environment does not affect the flavors of regional cuisine.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is one way food connects travelers to locals? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Through the shared appreciation of the area’s natural resources and traditions\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$By introducing travelers to foods that they can easily recreate at home\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$By teaching travelers how to prepare traditional dishes","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, D, G}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **A**. “Regional cuisine – food that is based on the long history of the people who have lived in a specific location for generations – combines history, culture, and, of course, food…”\n\n- The text mentions local cuisine is a reflection of the history and culture of the place.\n\n- **C**. The text states that some travelers “…visit new places – or revisit old favorites – to savor the food to be found only there.”  To savor means to taste and eat food and drink with great enjoyment or pleasure.\n\n- **D**. “The climate, local traditions and beliefs… unite to form the flavors of a region…” The unique aspects of a location, including environmental and cultural, contribute to the creation of a regional cuisine.\n\n- **G**. Regional cuisine is made up of: “…food that is based on the long history of the people who have lived in a specific location for generations…”, “local traditions”, and foods that “thrive in the area.”\n\n- *Incorrect options*: B, E, F, H\n\n- **B**.  The text states: “…cannot be duplicated anywhere else in the world.” *Replicate* is a synonym of *duplicate*.\n\n- **E**. Regional cuisine is dependent upon “…history, culture…” to shape its unique flavors.\n\n- **F**. Many travelers choose a destination specifically to eat the local food. “Many travelers will tell you that the reason they visit new places – or revisit old favorites – is to savor the food to be found only there.”\n\n- **H**. “The environment, local traditions and beliefs,... unite to form the flavors of a region.”\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “...long history of the people who have lived in a specific location for generations.” The use of words like “culture, history” and “traditions and beliefs” indicate that the food created in the area has important meaning for the local residents who make it.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **B**: Regional food cannot be easily made at home: “Regional cuisine – food that is based on the long history of the people who have lived in a specific location for generations – combines history, culture, and, of course, food, in a way that cannot be duplicated anywhere else in the world.”\n- **C**: Although taking cooking lessons from local chefs is a very popular activity for tourists, that is not mentioned in this text."},{"ref":"EB0272","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0272 \n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nDear Rachel,\n\nI'm having a wonderful time in Scotland! The island of Skye is an astonishing place to visit and summer is the ideal time to come. The dramatic landscapes and sea cliffs create scenes that I believe are perfect for filming a movie or series, especially one of medieval times. Yesterday I visited the Old Man of Storr hill and I can tell you that going up was tiring, but unforgettable. The hike took about one hour or a bit more, because of the wind and drizzle. Let me tell you that it was exhausting!. Do you know why people call it $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Old Man of Storr”? It is crazy, but you can see the face of an old man if you look at it from the right angle. I couldn't see it; however, I liked the story. I can guarantee that this place is one of the most photographed spots in Scotland.\n\nI'm staying at a gorgeous lodge. It is a historic country house, which I believe resembles the house in the movie Pride and Prejudice. In the interior, you can see pictures of the first owners and some antique furniture. It seems like a little museum!\n\nWell, that's all for now. See you next week!\n\nLove,\n\nMary\n\n**1 to 4. Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Summer is the best season to visit the Island\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The island would make a great setting for movies due to its scenery.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Filming a movie would be an easy task in those landscapes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The hike was less than one hour.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The walk was tiring due to the weather.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The legend states that the mountain resembles an old man's profile.  \n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Mary could clearly identify the old man's features.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The Old Man of Storr is the most visited place in Scotland. \n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraph 2 which means the following:** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$inn    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$vintage    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, E, F}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... summer is the ideal moment to come.”\\\n\nThe text states that the *ideal* moment to visit the island is summer; the word *ideal* suggests this is *the best* season to visit. \n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The dramatic landscapes and sea cliffs create scenes that I believe are perfect for filming a movie or series especially one of medieval times”\\\n\nThe text mentions that the place would be ideal to film a movie (or series) set in medieval times. The exercise uses the more general word *scenery* to describe the *landscapes*.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$going up was tiring [...] because of the wind and drizzle”\\\n\nThe writer states that the walk was short; however, because of the bad weather conditions (wind and drizzle), it felt really long and tiring. \n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...can see the face of an old man if you see it from the right angle.”\\\n\nThe word *story* makes reference to the *legend* about the mountain. The word *profile* is similar to *face*. \n\n- *Incorrect options:*  C, D, G, H\n\n- **C**: C is incorrect. The writer never mentions that filming a movie in that landscape would be easy or difficult.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The hike took about one hour or a bit more.” \\\n\nThe writer states that the trip took $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$about” one hour or a little bit more. That means that the trip was not shorter than one hour. \n\n- **G**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$you can see the face of an old man [...] I couldn't see it”\\\n\nThe writer states that she could not identify the *face* or *features* of the man.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I can guarantee that this place is one of the most photographed spots in Scotland.”\\\n\nThe writer states that the Old Man of Storr is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one of the most photographed spots in Scotland.” However, that does not mean that it is the most visited place. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{lodge}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The words *lodge* and *inn* are similar in meaning. A *lodge* is a place to stay and it is often linked to nature while *inn* is a small hotel.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{antique}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The words *antique* and *vintage* are synonyms and both mean that the furniture comes from a past era."},{"ref":"EB0273","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0273\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nJune 2nd\n\n**How can you become an eco-traveler?**\n\nHey guys! Today, we'll talk about how to be eco-travelers. I became one when I visited New Zealand and booked a room at one of the eco-lodges along the coastline. Since it was my first time at an eco-hotel, everything felt like an adventure: from the bamboo walls to the lack of air conditioning, from the organic breakfast in the morning to the chorus of crickets outside my window at night!\n\nThe first step to becoming an eco-traveler is to realize that mass tourism is no longer a sustainable option. Large hotels and overcrowded places are no longer a choice for me. Helping the environment, buying at a local store, and reducing my carbon footprint every time I travel are my life objectives.\n\nLet me tell you the steps to be an eco traveler:\n\n- Understand what ecotourism means (read the information in my previous post!)\n\n- Pick an eco-hotel with a LEED or Green Key certification (there are other certifications, just check)\n\n- Support local communities (Try local food, explore markets)\n\n- Choose green transportation (Use bicycles or walk)\n\n- Minimize waste (recycle and reuse)\n\nI hope all this information is helpful and inspiring. Consider it next time you travel. Leave your comments!\n\n**Answer the following questions.**  \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What word in paragraph 1 indicates that you made a reservation at a place to stay?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the writer feel staying at an eco-hotel for the first time?    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Mention one characteristic of mass tourism.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What word in paragraph 2 means $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$aims”?    [1 mark]\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in paragraphs 3-4.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Find a hotel that has a recognized ...[1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$A bicycle is an example of  ...    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$If the blog is helpful, the writer wants you to think about it...[1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{booked}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I visited New Zealand and booked a room at one of the eco-lodges along the coastline.”\\\n\nTo *book a room* means to make a reservation, in this case, at an eco-hotel. \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{like an adventure}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Since it was my first time at an eco-hotel, everything felt like an adventure”\\\n\nThe answer must have the word *like*, as this indicates the writer felt this experience was similar to an adventure (not exactly an adventure.)\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any **one** of the following answers:* \n\n- $\\textrm{large hotels}$\n\n- $\\textrm{overcrowded places}$        [1 mark]\n\n- “mass tourism [...] Large hotels and overcrowded places” \\\n\nThe writer gives two characteristics of mass tourism that they do not want to experience anymore.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{objectives}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The words *objectives* and *aims* are synonyms. Therefore, *aims* is a good replacement word for *objectives*.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{certification}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Pick an eco-hotel with a LEED or Green Key certification”\\\n\nThe writer suggests picking a hotel that has a *certification* that assesses it as an ecological option.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{green transportation}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The writer mentions that to become an eco-traveler, you should $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$choose green transportation,” and the options mention *bicycles* or *walk*. Cycling and walking are both good examples of green transportation. \n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{next time you travel}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Consider it next time you travel.”\\\n\nThe writer mentions that if the information in the blog is helpful, they want the readers to *consider* or *think about* it in their future trips."},{"ref":"EB0274","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0274\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Tips for a safari photography trip.**\n\nVisiting South Africa is one of the most $\\underline{\\textrm{gripping}}$ experiences you can have in your entire life. Here are some $\\underline{\\textrm{pointers}}$ you can follow for taking the best photos while going on a safari trip.  \n\n**[-3-]**\\\n\nThis is essential because you will be in open areas in which a wide-angle lens can be the right choice for landscapes, or you will need a telephoto lens when you want to isolate an animal from its surroundings. Therefore, selecting the correct one is important. \n\n**[-4-]**\\\n\nPhotographing animals in their natural habitat can be a challenge. Finding a spot and hiding are essential tips to avoid disturbing them. You must be ready to wait for long periods of time, often in silence. The most important thing is not to interfere with their natural behaviour. \n\n**[-5-]**\\\n\nThe animal's environment is a crucial part of the story you want to convey in your photograph, so take it into consideration when framing your shot.\n\n**[-6-]**\\\n\nDo not feed animals, shout, or get too close to them. Bear in mind that wild animals are territorial; therefore, getting too close may cause a direct attack.\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [2 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$gripping |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$tips |\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$pointers |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$interesting |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$thrilling |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$clues |\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Background is not important in a photo.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Be aware of a possible attack.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Be patient and observant.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Choose the right lens.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Interact with animals.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Pay attention to the landscape.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Not all lenses are the same.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Respect wildlife.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The word *thrilling* is a synonym of the word *gripping*, meaning very exciting.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is a confusing option here, however, *interesting* does not convey the excitement and strong emotions of the experience. \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This text is a set of guidelines to be followed when visiting South Africa. This text type gives tips or advice. The word *pointers* refers to tips that people can follow when taking photos in a safari.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is incorrect because *clues* are hints, related to a mystery or problem to solve.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a wide-angle lens can be the right choice for landscapes, or you will need a telephoto lens when you want to isolate an animal” \\\n\nParagraph 1 explains that choosing the right lens is important in order to capture the best shot. The writer suggests two types of lenses, depending on the type of photo you want to take. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option G** is not a suitable heading for paragraph 1 since the paragraph states that there are different lenses; however, the main idea is about choosing the correct one.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$You must be ready to wait for long periods of time, often in silence”\\\n\nParagraph 2 explains that if you want to take an interesting photo of a group of animals, you will have to stay quiet and be patient for some period of time. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option E** is not a suitable heading for paragraph 2 because the idea is completely the opposite. The text states $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not to interfere with their natural behaviour” and to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ avoid disturbing them”\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The animal's environment is a crucial part of the story you want to convey in your photograph”\\\n\nAccording to paragraph 2, it is important to pay attention to the *landscape* or *environment* since that will give people an idea of how the photo was taken and the meaning of it. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is not a suitable heading for paragraph 3 because the idea is completely the opposite. The background of the photo is essential. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{H}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Do not feed animals, shout, or get too close to them”\\\n\nAccording to paragraph 4, it is vital to respect wild animals by not feeding them, shouting, or getting too close. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is not a suitable heading for paragraph 4 since the paragraph states that if you get too close, you may be attacked. However, you do not have to be alert all the time about this problem."},{"ref":"EB0323","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0323 \n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a TV presenter introducing a segment.**\n\n:audio{#14d69ade-99be-4042-9b98-8f4b7718be87 aid=\"14d69ade-99be-4042-9b98-8f4b7718be87\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why is Tom Stuker famous for?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does Tom Stuker fly first-class anywhere?    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Who else enjoys the flights with Tom?    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the four true statements.**[4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Tom feels at home at airports. \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$He gets benefits every time he travels. \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Sometimes he uses bonus points to buy cars. \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Tom also pays for premium accommodation with his points. \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$He has bought luxury items with points. \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Tom established a children's foundation. \n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Tom has always been an enthusiastic flyer. \n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$There is a movie about him.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(he has) flown more than 23 million miles}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Tom Stuker, the man who has flown more than 23 million miles”\\\n\nThe audio mentions this is what makes Tom's story $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fascinating.”\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(he invested 510.000 in) a lifetime pass}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Back in 1990, Stuker made a bold move: he invested \\$510,000 in a lifetime pass”\\\n\nThis is how he can fly $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ anywhere, anytime,” he has bought a pass that allows it.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a companion}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$That golden ticket allows him and a companion”\\\n\nTom can take a companion when he flies.\n\n$4 - 7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, D, H}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$he's turned airports into his second home”\\\n\nThe phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$second home” means Tom feels comfortable, as if he lived there.\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$earning millions of reward points along the way”\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Reward points” are benefits, an advantage you gain from something, in this case, flying.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He has used these points to hire cars, and pay for luxury hotels”\\\n\n*Luxury hotels* and *premium accommodation* are similar in meaning.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$even inspired a Hollywood film”\\\n\nThe text mentions he has $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$inspired a Hollywood film.” This means that a film was created based on his story.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*   C, E, F, G\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He has used these points to hire cars”\\\n\nTo *hire* a car is to rent it for some time, not buy it.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He has used these points to hire cars, and pay for luxury hotels”\\\n\nThe text mentions he has used the points to pay for luxury hotels. Hotels are services, not items (things).\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Stuker has also used those points to support a children's cancer charity”\\\n\nStuker supports a charity, but he has not funded or established it. \n\n- **G**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He was once afraid of flying!”\\\n\nStuker was not a fan of flying before."},{"ref":"EB0324","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0324\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about their first cruise experience.**\n\n:audio{#768e70f2-ee1f-406a-8fe0-d09f6655644b aid=\"768e70f2-ee1f-406a-8fe0-d09f6655644b\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.** [5 marks]\n\n**How to Make the Most of Your Cruise Experience.**\n\n- Begin your cruise with an open mind, it is an experience to remember.\n\n- Explore your dining options. The buffet has incredible international options and lots of **[-1-]**.\n\n- You can also book an exclusive experience at one of the restaurants and meet **[-2-]**.\n\n- Don't miss the shows. There are many alternatives to choose from. My favorite was the cinema **[-3-]**.\n\n- Bear in mind that some shows and attractions which are popular can be **[-4-]**. \n\n- Appreciate the incredible service. The crew was warm and the room was **[-5-]**.\n\n- Finally, relax and enjoy, your cruise should be fun and unforgettable!","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(fresh) seafood}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$there was an impressive variety, from fresh seafood to international dishes”\\\n\nThe original text mentions seafood before international dishes.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the chef}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the chef came out to greet all the customers”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions they were able to *meet* and *greet* the chef in an intimate setting. Although *meet* and *greet* are not synonyms, in this context, they convey the experience of interacting with the chef.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{under the stars}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The highlight for me was the outdoor movie under the stars”\\\n\nThe original sentence mentions a *movie* while the exercise refers to it as *cinema*. Both are similar in context.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{overcrowded}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$some activities were overcrowded”\\\n\n*Activities* is paraphrased with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$shows and attractions”\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{spotless}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$my cabin was always spotless”\\\n\nThe text refers to the room as a *cabin*, which is a room in a cruise ship."},{"ref":"EB0325","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0325\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about their camping holidays.**\n\n:audio{#55ba16e2-4cc8-49c4-b1cf-b7999fe06ca5 aid=\"55ba16e2-4cc8-49c4-b1cf-b7999fe06ca5\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the family do every year?    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$When they went to the mountains...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$they prepared thoroughly.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$they felt exhausted.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$they were careful.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$However, as soon as they got there...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$it had been raining.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$the weather was not ideal.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$it rained for 10 days.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$They stayed in the tent because...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$they did not have dry wood.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$they were soaked and cold.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$they did not want to get dirty.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The family feels...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$this was a great camping experience.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$this was an interesting camping experience.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$this was the worst camping experience.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{buy new gear}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Year after year, we buy new gear”\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Year after year” means they do it every year. One possible mistake to look out for is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$going campling.” The speaker says they go camping for holidays but they do not mention it is every year.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$packed everything carefully”\\\n\n*Thoroughly* and *carefully* are synonyms. The speaker mentions they *packed* or *prepared* everything in detail.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- Option **B** is incorrect. The speaker says they were *excited*, not *exhausted*. \n\n- Option **C** is confusing. The speaker says they $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$packed carefully,” but this does not mean they were *careful*.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On the first night, it started raining”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions that night it began to rain and did not stop for 10 days.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- Option **A** is incorrect. It had not been raining before they got there. It started raining the night they arrived.\n\n- Option **B** is confusing. This option is too vague and does not include the detail of 10 days of rain.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$our clothes were always wet, and we were freezing”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions they were *wet* (*soaked*) and *freezing* (*cold*), so they stayed inside the tent.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- Option **A** is confusing. The speaker says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the firewood was soaked” but this alone is not the reason to stay in the tent. Because the wood was wet, they had wet clothes and were freezing.\n\n- Option **C** is incorrect. The speaker mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the ground was muddy,” but this is not the reason not to leave the tent.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it was the worst weather, but the best family memories”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions that, in spite of the bad weather, this camping created $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the best family memories”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- Option **B** is incorrect. When something is interesting, it catches your attention or curiosity. However, the text mentions that the trip was memorable.\n\n- Option **C** is incorrect. Although it rained for 10 days, the trip created $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the best family memories” so it was not the worst overall."}]},{"name":"Leisure Activities","questions":[{"ref":"EB0016","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0016\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\LARGE{\\textrm{🍂🍁 The Chestnut Festival on Madeira 🍁🍂}}$ \n\nEvery year, the picturesque village of Curral das Freiras on Madeira Island fills with the sweet aroma of roasted chestnuts as the locals celebrate the Chestnut Festival. This vibrant event is a testament to Madeira's deep agricultural roots and the community's love for this humble nut.\n\nHeld in November, the festival is more than just a celebration of chestnuts. It's an immersive experience, offering visitors a glimpse into the traditions and culture of Madeira. Stalls line the streets, selling a variety of chestnut-based delicacies, from chestnut cakes and soups to liqueurs and breads. There's even a chestnut beer! \n\nThe festival also features music, dances, and traditional Madeiran crafts. One of the highlights is the chestnut harvest contest, where locals and visitors compete to gather the most chestnuts in a limited time.\n\nWhether you're a foodie, a culture enthusiast, or simply someone who enjoys a good festival, the Chestnut Festival is a must-visit. The warm and welcoming atmosphere, combined with the stunning backdrop of Madeira's mountains, makes it an unforgettable experience. So, mark your calendars and join in the festivities—you'll leave with a newfound appreciation for chestnuts and the charming island of Madeira.\n\nCurious yet? It's time to pack your bags and dive into the chestnut madness!\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark]**. [4 marks]\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$This festival is a tribute to Madeira's farming traditions.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Attendees at the festival can enjoy a range of traditional food and beverages made from chestnuts.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The Chestnut Festival features a contest where participants engage in various activities like singing and dancing.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Curral das Freiras is located in a mountainous region of Madeira, providing a picturesque setting for the festival.\n\n**Choose the correct answer** [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the primary purpose of the text about the Chestnut Festival on Madeira?\n\n- A. $\\hspace{1em}$To describe the historical significance of the chestnut in Madeira's culture.\n\n- B. $\\hspace{1em}$To provide a detailed recipe for chestnut-based delicacies.\n\n- C. $\\hspace{1em}$To encourage readers to attend the Chestnut Festival by highlighting its activities and atmosphere.\n\n- D. $\\hspace{1em}$To compare the Chestnut Festival with other festivals on Madeira.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{0.5em}\\textrm{True}$ [1 mark]\n\n- Justification:$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This vibrant event is a testament to Madeira's deep agricultural roots”\n\n- *Agricultural roots* equals *farming traditions*, while *testament* is the synonym of *tribute*, so the statement is true.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{0.5em}\\textrm{True}$ [1 mark]\n\n- Justification:$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Stalls line the streets, selling a variety of chestnut-based delicacies... There's even a chestnut beer!\"\n\n- *A variety of chestnut-based delicacies* is the equivalent to *a range of traditional food and beverages made from chestnuts*, and beer is a beverage, so the statement is true\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{0.5em}\\textrm{False}$ [1 mark]\n\n- Justification:$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One of the highlights is the chestnut harvest contest, where locals and visitors compete to gather the most chestnuts in a limited time”\n\n- The contest is about the speed of chestnut picking, not singing and dancing.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{0.5em}\\textrm{True}$ [1 mark]\n\n- Justification:$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...combined with the stunning backdrop of Madeira's mountains, makes it an unforgettable experience.\"\n\n- *Stunning backdrop* is the equivalent to *picturesque setting*,  so the statement is true.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{0.5em}\\textrm{C}$ [1 mark]\n\n- The main focus of the text is to highlight the Chestnut Festival's various activities, attractions, and overall atmosphere in an engaging way that encourages readers to participate. \n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D\n\n- **A**: The text mainly focuses on the present-day celebration and atmosphere of the festival rather than delving into the historical significance of chestnuts.\n\n- **B**: The text mentions the variety of chestnut-based products available but doesn't provide any recipes.\n\n- **D**: The text doesn't compare the Chestnut Festival to other festivals but rather focuses on encouraging participation in this specific festival."},{"ref":"EB0017","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0017\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nAnna's fingers brushed against something small and smooth in the damp grass. She picked it up, surprised to find a flat, palm-sized rock painted with a bright red heart surrounded by tiny yellow stars. Beneath the design, a faint blue stamp read $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Kendal,” a town she hadn't thought about in years.\n\nShe turned the rock over in her hands, feeling its cool weight. It was silly, maybe, a painted stone in the middle of the park, yet it stirred a quiet ache inside her. Kendal had been her childhood home, a place she'd left behind long ago when life pulled her away to bigger cities and busier days. She imagined someone, perhaps a child, painting the rock and leaving it here with hopes it would find its way into someone's day.\n\nAnna smiled, tucking the rock gently into her pocket, and let her gaze drift to the distant skyline. The moment felt like a gift, small yet perfectly timed. She decided she'd pass the kindness along—perhaps paint her own rock with colors and leave it somewhere for someone else to find, like a thread connecting her to strangers through tiny, unexpected acts of wonder.\n\n**Choose an appropriate ending from the list that completes each sentence**.\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The painted stone was a reminder of...[1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Anna's discovery in the park sparked...[1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The simple act of finding the rock led Anna to...[1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The unexpected encounter with the painted rock triggered...[1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$...a decision to move back to her hometown.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$...a forgotten memory from her childhood.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$...a feeling of loneliness and isolation.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$...a plan to become a professional artist.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$...a sense of nostalgia and longing for the past.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$...a new appreciation for the small joys in life.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$...a desire to share kindness with others.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$...a commitment to living in the present moment.\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using words as they appear in the last paragraph of the text**.\n\nTucking the rock in her pocket, Anna felt this moment was a **[-5-]** [1 mark]\n\nShe would paint her rock and leave it somewhere, this would be her own act of **[-6-]** [1 mark]","markScheme":"1.4.*Answers 1 to 3 can be written in any order.*\n\n$\\textrm{B, E, F, G}$    [4 marks]\n\n- **B.** The quote $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Kendal had been her childhood home, a place she'd left behind long ago\" directly supports the idea that the stone was a reminder of a forgotten memory from her childhood.\n\n- **E.** The quote $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$yet it stirred a quiet ache inside her\" indicates a sense of nostalgia and longing for the past.\n\n- **F.** The sentence $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The moment felt like a gift, small yet perfectly timed\" - suggests a new appreciation for the small joys in life.\n\n- **G.** The sentence $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$She decided she'd pass the kindness along\" directly supports the idea of wanting to share kindness with others.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D, H\n\n- **A.** The quote $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a decision to move back to her hometown\" implies the stone might evoke nostalgia, but there's no indication in the text that Anna plans to move back.\n\n- **C.** The quote $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a feeling of loneliness and isolation\" suggests the opposite - a sense of connection and warmth.\n\n- **D.** As the quote $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a plan to become a professional artist\" reads, while the stone might inspire creativity, there's no explicit mention of Anna pursuing a career in art.\n\n- **H.** The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a commitment to living in the present moment\" might be understood as a positive takeaway, yet the text primarily focuses on nostalgia and the desire to share kindness, rather than a specific commitment to mindfulness.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{gift}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “The moment felt like a gift, small yet perfectly timed”\\\n\nThe expression *(a moment) is a gift* is a metaphorical way of saying that a moment is precious and will not come back, so we should make the most of it.\n\nThe text mentions Anna tucked her rock and felt this little act, this moment, was a *gift*.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{kindness}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “She decided she’d pass the kindness along”\\\n\nTo *pass (the) kindness along* means to do something nice for someone else because something nice has happened to you.\n\nIn this case, Anna feels grateful for the rock and decides to be kind to someone else in return."},{"ref":"EB0018","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0018\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nSubject: Quack-tastic News from Duckland! 🦆\n\nHey Sue!\n\nHope you're doing awesome! So, I've been meaning to tell you about my latest obsession: rubber ducks! 🦆✨ I know, I know, it sounds a bit quacky, but hear me out. I've started collecting these little guys from all over the world, and it's turning into a full-blown adventure!\n\nI've got a pirate duck from the Caribbean that's ready to set sail, a sushi chef duck from Japan (complete with a tiny sushi roll!), and even a superhero duck that's convinced $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$ can save the world one bath at a time. Seriously, who knew rubber ducks could have such diverse careers?\n\nI'm on a mission to find a duck from every continent—yes, even Antarctica! Imagine a penguin-duck hybrid! 😂 If you come across any cool $\\underline{\\textrm{ones}}$ during your travels, you HAVE to let me know. \n\nWe should totally have a rubber duck party soon! I'll bring my collection, and we can vote on which $\\underline{\\textrm{one}}$ deserves the title of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Ducky of the Year.” Spoiler alert: it's probably going to be the pirate duck. Arrr!\n\nCan't wait to hear from you!\n\nQuack you later,\n\nBill 🦆💛\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the teenager jokingly suggest about the superhero duck's abilities?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the teenager's goal regarding their rubber duck collection?\n\n[1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is special about the rubber duck from Antarctica? [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What fun event does the teenager suggest for their friends? [1 mark]\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ ...duck that's convinced $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$ can save the world...    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ If you come across any cool $\\underline{\\textrm{ones}}$ during your travels... [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ ...vote on which $\\underline{\\textrm{one}}$ deserves...    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**it can save the world**]\n\n   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: (That) it can save the world (one bath at a time) \n\n- The writer mentions they have a superhero duck $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$that's convinced it can save the world one bath at a time.”\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**To find a rubber duck from every continent**]     [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: Finding a rubber duck from every continent\n\n- The writer mentions they are $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$on a mission”, which means to take on a task that they consider very important. In this context *a mission* works as a synonym for *a goal*.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**It is a penguin-duck hybrid**]    [1 mark]\n\n- The writer mentions they want to find ducks from every continent and jokingly speculates about the possibility of a penguin-duck hybrid.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**A rubber duck party**]      [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$We should totally have a rubber duck party soon!”\n\nThe writer mentions a plan to set up this event to vote for the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Ducky of the Year.” \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**(a/the) superhero duck**]     [1 mark]\n\n- The pronoun *it* refers back to the superhero duck mentioned earlier in the sentence.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**ducks**]    [1 mark]\n\n- The writer mentions they are $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$on a mission to find a duck from every continent” and then asks their friend to let them know if they find any.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$:answer[**duck**]    [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: (which) (rubber) duck (from the collection)\n\n- The writer mentions that they want to celebrate a party to vote for $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Ducky of the Year” in this context, one refers to the one duck that will be selected."},{"ref":"EB0097","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0097\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a student talking to their friend about a new free-time activity.**\n\n:audio{#03519a53-7507-4b16-85f4-6c2720753fd8 aid=\"03519a53-7507-4b16-85f4-6c2720753fd8\"}\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the text that completes each gap in the following sentences.**\n\nThe Youth Book Club meeting is at **[-1-]** on Saturday.    [1 mark]\n\nThe speaker joined the book club **[-2-]**. [1 mark]\n\nThe previous book the group read was a **[-3-]**.    [1 mark]\n\nThe book club is a great way to meet people who share the love of **[-4-]**. [1 mark]\n\nThe book club gives the speaker a break from **[-5-]**. [1 mark]\n\n**Answer the following question.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker tells their friend the book club is not just about reading, and gives three reasons for the friend to join.  Give **two** reasons.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{three p.m.}$   [1 mark]\n\n-   “Youth Book Club meeting at three p.m”\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{last month}$   [1 mark]\n\n-   “I joined the club last month”\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{mystery story}$   [1 mark]\n\n-   “and we read a great mystery story.”\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{reading}$ [1 mark]\n\n- “to meet people who also love reading.”\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{school stress}$ [1 mark]\n\n- “it’s a little escape from school stress, too.”\n\n$6$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order. Any two of the following three:*\n\n-   $\\textrm{meet people}$\n-   $\\textrm{make new friends}$\n-   $\\textrm{escape (from) school stress}$\n\n- The speaker says, “It’s not just about reading, either, it’s a great way to *meet people* - especially those who also love reading, and maybe *make new friends*, and it’s a little *escape from school stress*, too.[2 marks]"},{"ref":"EB0098","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0098\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a student reading a brochure about outdoor\nactivities, and talking to a friend about it.**\n\n:audio{#38016d08-a7a6-42f2-99a5-c1de782f7f86 aid=\"38016d08-a7a6-42f2-99a5-c1de782f7f86\"}\n\n**Complete the gaps with words from the text.**\n\nArchery dates back over **[-1-]**    [1 mark]\n\nIn sports archery, people use a **[-2-]** to hit targets. [1 mark]\n\nArchery requires a calm mind, steady breathing, and **[-3-]**.    [1 mark]\n\nThe speaker decides to **[-4-]** archery. [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is an important part of archery?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Strength and speed\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Accuracy and timing\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Using the right bow size\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What decision does the speaker make at the end?     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To learn more about archery’s history\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To take the archery course\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To watch the next Olympics","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{10,000 years}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Archery is one of the oldest sports in the world, dating back over *10,000 years*.”\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Bow and arrow}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Sports archers use a bow and arrow to shoot at targets…”\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Perfect timing}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “...archers must focus their minds, steady their breathing, and release the arrow with *perfect timing*.”\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Sign up for}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I’m going to sign up for archery!”\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text states:$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A key part of the sport is *precision*,... and release the arrow with perfect *timing*.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: *Strength and speed* are not mentioned in the text.\n- **C**: The size of bow each archer needs to use is not discussed in the text.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “I’m going to sign up for archery!”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The text begins with a very short history of archery, but the speaker does not make any decisions until the end of the text.\n- **C**: The text states that archery is an Olympic sport, but there is no mention of the speaker watching the Olympics."},{"ref":"EB0099","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0099\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a teen offering suggestions to their parents for a\nfamily activity.**\n\n:audio{#41746127-8fe5-42df-a0b3-f60507aa555b aid=\"41746127-8fe5-42df-a0b3-f60507aa555b\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the first activity the speaker suggests?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Playing board games\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Going hiking\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Sitting in the park\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the speaker suggest going to the new park?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It has amazing views and is good for exercise\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It is close to their house\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To eat a meal together\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker suggest they eat when playing board games?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Pizza\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Snacks\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Burgers\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the speaker’s main reason for suggesting activities?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To spend time outdoors\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To spend time as a family\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To avoid being bored\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the speaker's attitude toward the activities?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They prefer board games over hiking\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They are open to any activity as long as the family is together\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They want the family to choose hiking because of the new park","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker starts the conversation with,”I have a few ideas to share…   we could go hiking this weekend…”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: Playing board games is the second option the speaker makes.\n- **C**: The speaker suggests going to the park to hike, not to sit in the park.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The park is supposed to have *wonderful views*. And the speaker also says, “ It would be a nice way to get outside and burn some calories, too.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **B**: The speaker says that the part is *new*, but there is no mention of where the park is.\n- **C**: The suggestion to eat a meal together is made in the second suggestion, to have a board games night.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- The speaker mentions *snacks* first, then suggests *burgers*, but their final decision is, *“Homemade pizzas!”*\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “We could make homemade pizzas,... and then we can get the board set up to play.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The speaker suggests hiking outdoors as a way for the family to do something together.  Being outdoors is not the speaker’s main goal.\n- **C**: There is no mention of being bored.  The speaker states that,”... it feels like we haven’t done anything fun together as a family in a long time…” However, this does not mean that the speaker has been feeling bored.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I just want to spend some time with you,” is what the speaker says at the end of the text.  They begin with,”  it feels like we haven’t done anything fun together as a family in a long time,” demonstrating that the speaker wants to spend time with their family.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The speaker states,”Honestly, I don’t mind what we pick—I just want to spend some time with you.  We do not know if the speaker prefers one activity over the other: the goal is to spend time together as a family.\n- **C**: The speaker suggests going to the park because of the lovely view and the opportunity for the family to be outdoors, doing some exercise, together. There is no information that the speaker prefers to hike rather than play games (see option A above)."},{"ref":"EB0333","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0333\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a young adult talking about their unusual hobby.**\n\n:audio{#83aaa11f-59e4-4e19-a41f-050f45e1563b aid=\"83aaa11f-59e4-4e19-a41f-050f45e1563b\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker describe moving through the supermarket?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name **two** things the speaker does with the products they see.    [2 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Give one example of a “little challenge” the speaker creates.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the speaker like going to the supermarket alone? Name **one** reason.    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker feel about going to the supermarket alone?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Bored\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Excited\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Nervous","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{like a tourist}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I wander aisles like a tourist, checking unusual products.”\\\n\nTo *wander* is to walk around, in the exercise the words *moving through* replace wander. The speaker uses the phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$like a tourist” to explain how they feel while exploring.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any two of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{read labels}$\n\n- $\\textrm{imagine tasting}$\n\n- $\\textrm{(figure out) who might buy them}$        [2 marks]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I read the labels, imagine tasting them, and try to figure out who might buy them.”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions these as some of the activities he engages in with the products.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{finding the cheapest chocolate}$\n\n- $\\textrm{finding the weirdest spice}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Sometimes I create little challenges, like finding the cheapest chocolate or the weirdest spice.”\\\n\nThe speaker adds playful challenges to make the supermarket experience more engaging\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{no one interrupts (me)}$\n\n- $\\textrm{(I) can take as long as I want}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$No one interrupts me, and I can take as long as I want”\\\n\nThe speaker frames solo supermarket shopping as a relaxing and quiet activity, without pressure to do anything quickly.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... It feels strangely exciting for such a normal place.”\\\n\nThe supermarket is ordinary, but the speaker finds it exciting to explore alone.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The speaker is clearly engaged and curious, not bored.\n\n- **C**: The speaker does not feel nervous; the experience is described as relaxing and enjoyable."},{"ref":"EB0334","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0334\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a university student describing a leisure activity they enjoy.**\n\n:audio{#666fb6aa-9657-4eda-8adb-095013ee360b aid=\"666fb6aa-9657-4eda-8adb-095013ee360b\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.**[5 marks]\n\nThe speaker practises accents in their **[-1-]**.\\\n\nThey copy voices they hear in a **[-2-]** or a podcast.\\\n\nTo improve pronunciation, they repeat the same **[-3-]** many times.\\\n\nThey never practise accents in front of **[-4-]**.\\\n\nPractising accents helps them notice new words and identify the way people **[-5-]**.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the speaker say they mix accents or invent their own?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It is strange\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It is fun\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$It is like acting","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{free time}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One thing I really enjoy doing in my free time is practising accents.”\\\n\nThe speaker clearly states that this activity is done during their free time.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{film}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I hear a voice in a film or a podcast and try to copy it.”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions using films and podcasts as sources to practice accents.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{sentence}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I repeat the same sentence again and again, just to get the sound right.”\\\n\nRepeating the same sentence helps the speaker focus on pronunciation and/or sound.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{other people}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I never do it in front of other people.”\\\n\nThe speaker prefers to practise accents privately and not in front of others.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{really sound}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I notice words I never heard before and how people really sound”\\\n\nThe activity increases the speaker's awareness of vocabulary and natural speech.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... it helps me switch off and have fun with language.”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$practising accents” is a way to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$have fun with language”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A** is confusing. The speaker mentions practicing accents is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$strangely relaxing,” but not strange.\n\n- **Option C** is incorrect. The speaker mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It's not about acting”"},{"ref":"EB0335","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0335\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a young adult talking about a leisure activity they enjoy in their free time.**\n\n:audio{#244a34d9-5b1f-477b-81c4-a33fd972db64 aid=\"244a34d9-5b1f-477b-81c4-a33fd972db64\"}\n\n**Find the four true statements.**  [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker explores his area on his bike. \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker plans their destination before leaving home.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker gets on whatever public transport arrives first. \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker gets off in the middle of the ride. \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker sometimes visits parts of the city they do not know. \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker takes photos during these trips. \n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker usually travels very far from home. \n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker enjoys travelling alone.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker do after arriving at the final stop? Name **one**.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker describe this activity, even though they are not travelling far?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1 - 4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C, E, F, H }$        [4 marks]\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$get on the first train or bus that arrives” \\\n\nThe speaker does not plan ahead and chooses the first available form of transport.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Sometimes I end up in areas I've never seen before.” \\\n\nThe activity leads the speaker to discover parts of the city they $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$do not know” or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$have never seen before”.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I walk around, take a few photos, and then go home.” \\\n\nTaking photos is part of the speaker's routine during these trips.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I like being alone and traveling at my own pace” \\\n\nThe speaker clearly states that they enjoy travelling alone.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B, D, G \n\n- **A**: The speaker mentions using *public transportation*, not a bike.\n\n- **B**: The speaker says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I don't choose a place first.” This means they do not $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$plan their destination”\n\n- **D**: The speaker mentions waiting $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$until the final stop,” not getting off in the middle of the ride.\n\n- **G**: The speaker says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I know I'm not travelling far.”\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{walk around}$\n\n- $\\textrm{take photos}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I walk around, take a few photos, and then go home.”\\\n\nAfter arriving, the speaker explores the area briefly before returning home.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a mini adventure}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... it still feels like a mini adventure.”\\\n\nThe speaker compares the experience to a small adventure despite staying local."},{"ref":"EB0397","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0397\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**The Queue**\\\n\n*Thursday evening*\n\nPeople call me a professional queuer. I prefer *line consultant* - it looks more distinguished on a business card. The work is simple: demanding clients pay me to wait so they don't have to. They are busy Londoners with money to spare. I stand in line. They get the prize.\n\nThis week: three jobs.\n\nThe O2, Monday. A sold-out concert. A client needed the wristbands for her two front-row tickets. I joined the queue at dawn, got the bands by mid-morning and was home by lunch. She messaged yesterday to say the show was unforgettable. I was glad for her.\n\nWembley, Tuesday. Champions League. I held a client's place in line for twelve hours and handed over my spot 30 minutes before the opening ceremony. He messaged at midnight - apparently the match was incredible. I was happy for him.\n\nCarnaby Street, Wednesday. A limited-edition trainer release. I queued for four hours, entered the store, and secured the last pair in my client's size, narrowly ahead of a very determined sixteen-year-old. My client was delighted. The teenager was not.\n\nMy phone just buzzed.  Another job tomorrow. Crufts tickets. Great - I love dogs.\n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraph 1 which means the following:** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$impressive    [1 mark] \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$difficult    [1 mark] \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$burn    [1 mark] \n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker waited all day to get the concert wristbands.    [1 mark] \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker's client contacted them after the match.    [1 mark] \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker was unable to get the trainers in the client's size.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{distinguished}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it looks more distinguished on a business card\"\\\n\n*Distinguished* describes something that appears impressive or of high social status. The speaker prefers a title that sounds more professional and elevated to clients.\n\n \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{demanding}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$demanding clients pay me to wait\"\\\n\n*Demanding* describes people who have high expectations and are not easy to please. This tells us that the speaker's clients are rich and used to getting exactly what they want.\n\n \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{spare}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$busy Londoners with money to spare\"\\\n\n*Burn* works because it fits the same idiomatic structure (“money to + verb”) and conveys excess wealth. *Burn* is used metaphorically, meaning they have extra money. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$was home by lunch\"\\\n\nThe speaker joined the queue at dawn but had completed the job by midday, which is significantly less than a full day.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He messaged at midnight\"\\\n\nThe client sent a message after the match had ended to share their happiness reaction with the speaker.\n\n \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$secured the last pair in my client's size\"\\\n\nThe speaker DID successfully obtain the correct size by beating another customer (a teenager) to the last pair."},{"ref":"EB0398","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0398\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Cutting Edge**\\\n\n*By our motorsport correspondent*\n\nDonna Merkley does not look like a typical racing driver. The 47-year-old from Saskatoon, Saskatchewan wears jeans and an old leather jacket, a bicycle helmet, and swimming goggles. For the last eleven years, 50 weeks a year, Donna has ruled the world of competitive lawnmower racing. She currently holds three regional titles and is described by both admirers and rivals as $`$ $\\underline{\\textrm{relentless}}$'.\n\n**[-1-]**\\\n\nThe machine she races - called Gerald - is a modified ride-on mower with the engine tuned for maximum speed. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$People assume these things crawl,” she says. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Mine does not.” Gerald, a 1987 Husqvarna she has rebuilt entirely on her own, can reach speeds of around 60 kilometres per hour.\n\n**[-2-]**\\\n\nCompetition in Donna's league is $\\underline{\\textrm{fierce}}$. Her closest competitor, a retired schoolteacher from Alberta, has finished ahead of her just twice in four seasons. Donna's comment about him: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He's good. I'm better.” Regional events draw drivers from across Canada and the US, and audiences are surprisingly large.\n\n**[-3-]**\\\n\nRacing is more than a hobby for Donna - it's a $\\underline{\\textrm{lifelong}}$ commitment. She is currently designing a training programme for junior competitors and has plans to enter her first international event next spring. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gerald,” she says with a smile, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$is already being prepared.”\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$A Life on the Track\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The Competition\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Friends on the Circuit\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The Machine\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The Need for Speed\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$More Than a Sport\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|4.$\\hspace{1em}$relentless |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$ambitious |\n\n|5.$\\hspace{1em}$fierce |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$intense |\n\n|6.$\\hspace{1em}$lifelong |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$lasting |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$permanent |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$persistent |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$powerful |","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Her current model, a 1987 Husqvarna she has rebuilt entirely on her own, can reach speeds of around 60 kilometres per hour.\"\\\n\nThis paragraph focuses on the technical details of Donna's racing mower - its modifications, speed, and history.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** sounds relevant but does not describe the paragraph's specific focus on the machine itself. \n\n \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Her closest competitor, a retired schoolteacher from Alberta, has finished ahead of her just twice in four seasons.\"\\\n\nThis paragraph describes Donna's rivals - the people she races against, race results, and the competitive atmosphere of regional events.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is incorrect. The paragraph mentions large audiences and a competitive atmosphere, which could suggest friendship, but the focus is clearly on rivalry. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$    [1 mark]\n\n- \"Racing is more than a hobby for Donna - it's a lifelong commitment.\"\\\n\nThis paragraph moves beyond competition to explore what the sport means to Donna personally, including her plans for its future.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option E** is incorrect. The word $`$speed' appears earlier in the text, making this heading plausible, but this paragraph focuses on Donna's commitment to the sport.\n\n \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$is described by both admirers and rivals as $`$relentless'\"\\\n\n*Relentless* describes someone who never gives up or stops trying. *Persistent* has the same meaning - continuing without stopping despite meeting difficulties.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is incorrect. *Ambitious* means wanting to achieve success, which is related but does not capture the meaning of never stopping or giving up.\n\n \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Competition in Donna's league is fierce.\"\\\n\n*Fierce* describes something very strong or forceful in this context. *Intense* has a similar meaning.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option F** is not correct here. *Powerful* could describe physical strength but does not reflect the meaning of fierce competition in this context.\n\n \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it's a lifelong commitment\"\\\n\n*Lifelong* describes something that continues for the whole of a person's life. *Lasting* is a very close synonym meaning $`$continuing for a long time.'\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is incorrect. *Permanent* suggests something that will never end or change, which is stronger than lifelong and does not fit naturally in this context."},{"ref":"EB0399","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0399\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**Night Shift at the Bounce Palace**\n\nI told you I just started working nights at an indoor trampoline park. It's a simple job: guard the building and make sure nobody breaks in. During the day it is loud and full of customers of all ages. When I'm there, it is completely different.\n\n \n\nMy shift starts at midnight. The whole building is quiet and the lights are low. There are about forty trampolines in the main hall, and they just sit there in the dark, waiting. I do my rounds. I drink my coffee. I try not to look at them too much.\n\n \n\nI lasted three weeks before I tried one.\n\nI told myself it was just a quick test to make sure the equipment was safe. I jumped. And bounced. And then I spent the next forty minutes doing something that I can only describe as the best fun I have had since I was ten years old.\n\n \n\nI am a 34-year-old man. I am responsible for the security of this building. I bounced off the walls. I laughed so hard my sides hurt. Now I bounce every night, and I have never told anyone about it before - except you.\n\n**Choose the three true statements.**[3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The guard has worked at the trampoline park for a long time.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Keeping the building safe is the main responsibility.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Only children are allowed to use the park during opening hours.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The guard begins work in the middle of the night.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The main hall contains a large number of trampolines.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The narrator finds it easy to ignore the trampolines during his shift.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.** [4 marks]\n\n \n\n**Marco** 🏀\\\n\n*00:47*\\\n\nok confession time 😬\n\n \n\nso you know that new job? I **[-4-]** it out for exactly three weeks before I gave in and tried one of the trampolines.\n\n \n\ntold myself it was a safety check. it wasn't.\n\n \n\nI **[-5-]** onto one and just kept going. **[-6-]** nearly forty minutes just bouncing around. honestly the most fun I've had in years.\n\n \n\n**[-7-]** so hard I could barely stand up. now I do it every single night. don't tell anyone ok 😂\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$bounced \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$climbed \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$endured \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$jumped \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$lasted \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$laughed \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$spent  \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$worked","markScheme":"$1 - 3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, D, E}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Guard the building and make sure nobody breaks in\"\\\n\nThe guard describes his job as keeping the building safe, which tells us that security is his main responsibility.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$My shift starts at midnight\"\\\n\nMidnight is the middle of the night, confirming that the guard begins work at this time.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There are about forty trampolines in the main hall\"\\\n\nForty trampolines is a large number, telling the reader that the hall contains many trampolines.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C, F\n\n- **A**: The guard says he $`$just' started working a month ago, which is a short time, not a long time. The word $`$just' also implies something that has happened recently.\n\n- **C**: The text mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$customers of all ages” using the park, not only children.\n\n- **F**: The guard says he tries not to look at the trampolines too much, which suggests he finds it difficult to ignore them.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I lasted it out three weeks before I tried one\" \\\n\n*Lasted it out* means to continue for a period of time without giving up. The guard resisted trying the trampolines for three weeks before giving in.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option H** is not correct. Although $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$worked it out\" is a common phrase in English, it means to find a solution to a problem, which does not fit the meaning here.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I jumped. And bounced.\" \\\n\nThe guard describes getting onto the trampoline energetically. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jumped onto one\" fits the action described in the original text.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is not correct. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Climbed onto one\" is grammatically correct but the text describes a sudden, energetic action, not a careful or difficult movement.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I spent the next forty minutes doing something that I can only describe as the best fun I have had since I was ten years old\" \\\n\n*Spent* describes passing a period of time doing an activity. The guard bounced around for nearly forty minutes.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is not correct. *Endured* suggests suffering through something unpleasant, which contradicts the joyful description of this moment.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I laughed so hard my sides hurt\" \\\n\nThe guard was laughing with such intensity that it caused physical discomfort - but in this case, it shows how much fun he was having.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is not correct. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Bounced so hard\" makes sense in a trampoline context, but the text makes clear it is laughing, not bouncing, that caused the pain."}]},{"name":"Life Stories","questions":[{"ref":"EB0022","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0022\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\LARGE{\\textrm{SOLO IN THE ANTARCTIC!}}$\n\nInterviewer: **[-0-]**  So, Mr. Thompson, after two years alone in the Antarctic, what was the toughest part for you?\n\nMr. Thompson: Honestly, it was a mixed bag. Some days were a walk in the park, while others felt like I was climbing a mountain. The isolation was the real kicker. It's like being in the middle of nowhere, with no one but yourself to talk to.\n\nInterviewer: **[-1-]** \n\nMr. Thompson: I had to dig deep, you know? Sometimes, I'd get caught up in my own thoughts, and it was a slippery slope. But I kept a diary, which was a lifesaver. Writing down my feelings was like venting to a friend. I also kept busy with research; it kept my mind from wandering.\n\nInterviewer: **[-2-]** \n\nMr. Thompson: Oh, there were a few hair-raising moments, for sure. Once, I nearly ran out of fuel for the generator. That would have been a nightmare. But I managed to scrape by, patching things up as I went along.\n\nInterviewer: **[-3-]** \n\nMr. Thompson: In a heartbeat. Despite the ups and downs, it was a once-in-a-lifetime experience. I came out stronger and with a fresh perspective on life.\n\nown text\n\n**Choose one of the questions (A-F) from the list below that best complete the gaps 1-3.**  [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Were you ever afraid for your life?\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Looking back, would you do it again?\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$What was the best moment of the experience?\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$How did you keep your spirits up?\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Did you have any close calls?\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$What did you do to keep yourself busy?\n\n**Find the word of phrase in paragraph 0 which means the following:**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a piece of cake\"    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$remote location    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The answer below gap 1 describes the ways Mr Thompson used to cheer himself up (*keep his spirits up*), for example, keeping a diary or keeping himself busy.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The paragraph below gap 2 talks about difficult and potentially dangerous moments (*close calls*).\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Mr. Thompson's answer *In a heartbeat* confirms his eagerness to try such an adventure again.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, F\n\n- **A:** This question is a distractor for gap 2, which indeed describes the dangerous situations, but none of them threatens Mr Thompson's life.\n\n- **C:** None of the answers is focused on one positive experience during the stay in Antarctic\n\n- **F:** This question is a distractor for gap 1, as this paragraph mentions Mr Thompson $`$busy with research', yet it is not the essence of this fragment- not breaking down is. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A walk in the park}$   [1 mark] \n\n- *A walk in the park* is a very easy task/activity, which is a synonym of *a piece of cake.*\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$  $\\textrm{in the middle of nowhere}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: Being in the middle of nowhere \n\n- *Being in the middle of nowhere* means being far and cut off from other people and places, in a remote location."},{"ref":"EB0023","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0023\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nGood morning everyone,\n\nToday, I want to shed light on a lesser-seen side of *voluntourism* *—the $`$dark side.' While volunteering abroad is often pursued with the best intentions, it can sometimes do more harm than good. In many cases, short-term volunteers without specialized skills are placed in roles that unintentionally disrupt local communities. Projects may be designed more to attract tourists than to meet real needs, providing a temporary $`$feel-good' experience for volunteers but little lasting benefit for those they aim to help.\n\nWorse, voluntourism can create dependency on outside aid rather than empowering communities to be self-sustaining. In some cases, it even exploits vulnerable populations, especially children, to appeal to tourists. This is a call to rethink how we help- by partnering with local organizations, investing in skills training, and supporting sustainable development that truly benefits communities.\n\nLet's remember that real change requires commitment and humility, not just adventure.\n\nThank you for your attention.\n\n\\*  *Voluntourism* is a portmanteau (a word combining two words and their meaning into a new meaning) of voluntarism and tourism\n\n1 - 4: **Choose the 4 true statements**   [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker believes voluntourism mostly results in long-term positive impacts.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Volunteers without relevant skills may unintentionally disrupt local communities.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker suggests ending all types of volunteer work abroad.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Some projects are organized more for tourists’ benefit than for the communities.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker claims that voluntourism rarely has any negative consequences.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Voluntourism can foster dependency rather than self-sufficiency in communities.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker recommends partnering with local organizations to create real. change.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$According to the speaker, voluntourism generally avoids exploiting vulnerable populations.\n\n \n\n**Choose the correct answer**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the main purpose of the speech?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To encourage people to stop volunteering abroad altogether\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To highlight the negative aspects of voluntourism and promote more responsible approaches\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To promote voluntourism as a way to experience adventure while helping communities\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$To explain the benefits of short-term volunteer work in all communities","markScheme":"$1$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers 1 to 4 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, D, F, G}$ [4 marks]\n\n- **H:** The speaker mentions that unskilled volunteers may end up disrupting local communities unintentionally.\n\n“In many cases, short-term volunteers without specialized skills are placed in roles that unintentionally disrupt local communities\"\n\n- **D:** The speaker explains that some projects are created more to appeal to tourists than to meet real needs in the community.\n\n“Projects may be designed more to attract tourists than to meet real need\"\n\n- **F:** The speaker notes that voluntourism can sometimes create dependency on external aid, hindering self-sufficiency.\n\n“Voluntourism can create dependency on outside aid rather than empowering communities to be self-sustaining\"\n\n- **G:** The speaker encourages partnering with local organizations as a way to support sustainable, positive change.\n\n“by partnering with local organizations, investing in skills training, and supporting sustainable development\"\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, E, H\n\n- **A:** The speaker warns that voluntourism can have negative effects and may not lead to long-term positive change if it's not done thoughtfully.\n\n“While volunteering abroad is often pursued with the best intentions, it can sometimes do more harm than good\"\n\n- **C:** The speaker does not suggest ending all volunteer work but advocates for more thoughtful approaches to ensure lasting benefits.\n\n“The answer isn't to stop helping, but to rethink how we help\"\n\n- **E:** The speaker specifically addresses the negative consequences that voluntourism can bring, such as disruption and dependency.\n\n“It can sometimes do more harm than good\"\n\n- **H:** The speaker warns that voluntourism can exploit vulnerable populations, like children, to appeal to tourists, highlighting the ethical issues involved.\n\n“In some cases, it even exploits vulnerable populations, especially children, to appeal to tourists\"\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker acknowledges the problems with voluntourism, such as dependency and exploitation, and advocates for more sustainable, thoughtful methods of helping communities. This is the main purpose of the speech.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **A:** The speaker does not suggest abandoning all forms of volunteering but instead emphasizes finding responsible ways to make a positive impact.\n\n- **C:** The speaker actually challenges this idea, suggesting that some voluntourism projects are designed more for the tourists' benefit rather than genuinely helping communities.\n\n- **D:** The speech points out potential harms of short-term volunteering, especially when volunteers lack the necessary skills, rather than promoting its benefits universally."},{"ref":"EB0024","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0024\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n$\\LARGE{\\textrm{Singapore's Increasing TCK Population and Educational Challenges}}$\n\n*J. Smith for The Global School News, 10/11/24*\n\nSingapore, a global financial hub, is witnessing a rise in the number of Third Culture Kids (TCKs), children who spend a significant part of their formative years living outside their parents' native culture. This trend, fueled by increased globalization and corporate relocations, presents unique challenges for the city-state's education system.\n\nTCKs often exhibit exceptional adaptability, cultural sensitivity, and multilingualism. However, their constant transitions between cultures can lead to identity crises, academic disruptions, and social adjustment issues. Singapore's education system, renowned for its academic rigor, may not always cater to the diverse needs of TCKs.\n\nTo address these challenges, some schools in Singapore are implementing programs that foster intercultural understanding, provide counseling services for TCKs, and offer flexible curriculum options. Additionally, there's a growing need for teacher training in multicultural education to better support the unique learning styles and emotional needs of TCKs.\n\nWhile Singapore embraces its position as a global city, it's crucial to ensure that its education system remains inclusive and responsive to the evolving needs of its diverse student population, including the growing number of TCKs.\n\n**Answer the following questions:**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What $\\underline{\\textrm{two}}$ factors contribute to the rise in the number of TCKs in Singapore?[2 marks]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name $\\underline{\\textrm{two}}$ negative consequences the lifestyle of TCKs can lead to.[2 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How do schools in Singapore try to support TCKs?[2 marks]\n\n**Choose the correct answer**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the most suitable heading for the last paragraph?    \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The Future of Singapore's Education System\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$A Global City, A Global Education\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Addressing the Needs of TCKs\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$ Challenges and Solutions","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Increased globalization}$ [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Corporate relocations}$  [1 mark]\n\n- The last sentence of the 1st paragraph reads: “This trend, fueled by increased globalization andcorporate relocations, presents unique challenges for the city-state's education system.\"\n\n-  *Fueled by* (increased globalization and corporate relocations) means contributing to the rise of the number of TCKs in Singapore described at the beginning of the paragraph.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Any two of the following (in any order)\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{identity crises}$  [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{academic disruptions}$  [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{social adjustment issues}$ [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n- The 2nd sentence of the second paragraph “However, their constant transitions between cultures can lead to identity crises, academic disruptions, and social adjustment issues.\" enumerates 3 possible negative consequences of TCKs transitions.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Any two of the following (in any order)\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{...fostering intercultural understanding}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{providing counseling services}$ [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{offering flexible curriculum options}$ [1 mark]\n\n[2 marks]\n\n- The first sentence of the third paragraph reads “To address these challenges, some schools in Singapore are implementing programs that foster intercultural understanding, provide counseling services for TCKs, and offer flexible curriculum options\", mentions *these challenges*, which are the problems such as identity crises etc. that TCKs' constant transitions between cultures can lead to, as described in the paragraph before.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Addressing the Needs of TCKs\" is the most accurate option because it directly reflects the main point of the paragraph, which discusses the strategies and initiatives being implemented to support TCKs in Singapore's education system.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D \n\n- **A:**  While this is a broad topic, the paragraph specifically focuses on a subset of the future, namely the needs of TCKs.\n\n- **B:** This heading is too broad and doesn't directly address the specific issue of TCKs.\n\n- **D:** This is a potential heading, but it's more general and doesn't highlight the specific focus on TCKs."},{"ref":"EB0127","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0127\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a former professional athlete talk about how an\ninjury changed his life forever.**\n\n:audio{#c8873dcb-4a59-4a28-871b-64936b1ea953 aid=\"c8873dcb-4a59-4a28-871b-64936b1ea953\"}\n\n1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the 3 true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$As a professional athlete, he wanted to be superior to any other competitor.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$When he competed professionally, he was not interested in being famous.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The athlete's injury had no emotional impact on him.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker's previous athletic career made his recovery more difficult.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Regaining his self-esteem was key in finding a new direction in life.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker finds coaching other athletes truly rewarding.\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.** [3 marks]\n\nThe speaker thinks his success as an athlete was the result of **[-4-]** and of challenging himself to the fullest. He believes that being **[-5-]** helped him go through the injury with strength and courage and that what happened to him taught him a valuable **[-6-]**.","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, E, F}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **A**. The speaker says, “When I was a professional athlete, my life basically revolved around perseverance and pushing my limits to excel in races…” The word excel means to surpass others or be superior in some respect. In this case, the speaker expresses he wanted to be superior to others in races.\n\n- **E**. The speaker says, “Slowly, I began to rebuild my confidence and to find a new purpose in life,” which means that the athlete’s self-esteem had decreased after the injury, but regaining it was vital to discover he could train young athletes; that was a new purpose for his life.\n\n- **F**. The speaker says, “Now, I train young athletes, and I have a renewed sense of fulfilment.” The phrase *renewed sense of fulfilment* suggests he found training young athletes *satisfying*, which is a synonym of the word *rewarding*.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: B, C, D\n\n- **B**.  The speaker says, “...my life basically revolved around (...) and be recognised worldwide for this,” which means the speaker wanted to be famous worldwide.\n\n- **C**. The former athlete says, “However, an injury drastically altered my life, causing my entire world to crumble.”  *To crumble* refers to how his world of success collapsed due to the injury. Then he adds that being told he couldn't compete anymore was *devastating*, which adds to the idea that what happened to him had an emotional impact on him.\n\n- **D**. The speaker says, “All those years of hard training and overcoming obstacles (...) helped me navigate my recovery smoothly…” The phrase “navigate my recovery smoothly”  refers to the speaker going through the recovery  without difficulty.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{perseverance}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “When I was a professional athlete, my life basically revolved around perseverance…” which means he never gave up and continued towards his goal.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{resilient}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “All those years of hard training and overcoming obstacles had made me really resilient and strong, which helped me navigate my recovery smoothly…” *Resilient* refers to someone who is strong and quick to recover after a hard time in life.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{lesson}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Lesson learnt? Facing adversity can lead to significant personal growth.”"},{"ref":"EB0128","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0128\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a woman describe her childhood in a rural area.**\n\n:audio{#35e7bb1b-62d0-4bc8-b7be-d0aa8224dc31 aid=\"35e7bb1b-62d0-4bc8-b7be-d0aa8224dc31\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker couldn't attend school because…    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$she had to take care of the farm animals.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$her house was in a remote location.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$she helped her mother mend clothes.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the family mostly manage their food supply?     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Her mother worked extra hours.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The family raised their own animals.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The family had a vegetable garden.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$One of the problems during the winter was that…     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$the cold inside the house was unbearable.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$the water supply was too limited.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$they could not go outside the house.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$During the winter, the father…     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$repaired the water pipes.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$worked more hours than usual.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$used his creativity to feed the family.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$As a child, the speaker learnt to value…     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$a hot bath.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$her father’s hard work.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$the simple things in life.\n\n**Answer the following question.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the speaker, what did their mother teach them to do because they had little money?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The woman says, “Our house was too far from the town, so my brother and I were homeschooled.”\n- The word *remote* suggests the house was far away from the school, and the speaker and her brother didn’t go to school. That's why they were *homeschooled*, which means they were taught at home. thing.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect as the speaker says, “We didn't’ have cows or chickens…”\n\n- **Option C**: is an incorrect answer since the speaker says it was her mother who mended their clothes; she doesn't suggest she helped her.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “...we grew most of our food,” from which we infer they had a garden where they cultivated vegetables.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect because the speaker mentions it was the father who had to work “extra shifts”; she doesn't mention that the mother works.\n- **Option B**: is a wrong option since she says, “We didn't’ have cows or chickens…”\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The woman says, “In the winter, the bitter cold would freeze the garden and the water pipes, so we had less food and little water.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: the word *unbearable* refers to something that is impossible to tolerate. The speaker says, “Our circumstances made us appreciate (...) a hot meal or a cosy blanket on a cold night,” which suggests they felt comfortable inside the house during winter.\n- **Option C**: is not a correct option since she doesn't mention they had to stay inside the house.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “In the winter, the bitter cold would freeze the garden (...) we had less food and little water. But my father worked extra shifts to cover our basic needs.” This shows that due to the shortage of water and food during winter, the father had to do extra hours at his job to get more money to buy the food they couldn’t grow.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect as she doesn’t mention the father repairing or fixing the water pipes.\n- **Option C**: is not correct, as she explains that it was the mother who taught them to be creative to compensate for what they didn't have or couldn’t buy.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “Our circumstances made us appreciate the small joys in life, like a hot meal or a cosy blanket on a cold night.” These are simple things in life that bring people joy.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: is not correct, as the speaker never mentions a hot bath; she actually talks about the shortage of water in winter.\n- **Option B**: the speaker mentions her father worked hard, but she doesn't specifically say that was something she valued, making option B incorrect.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{to rely on our creativity}$  *Also accept other words with similar meaning.*  [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “My mother taught us to rely on our creativity to make do with their limited resources.” The speaker mentions that their mother taught them to be creative to be able to “make do with their limited resources”. *To have limited resources* is, for example, to have little money."},{"ref":"EB0129","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0129\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a pianist talk about the impact that her piano teacher had on her career.**\n\n:audio{#965b5ea6-1025-4bab-96dc-7016cf0c2704 aid=\"965b5ea6-1025-4bab-96dc-7016cf0c2704\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ Why did the speaker’s mother think she might be interested in music? [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ How did the speaker’s mother know Mr. Vernuchi?  [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ How did Mr. Vernuchi describe the speaker’s natural talent at the piano? [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ At first, what was the obstacle to the speaker having a piano at her house? [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$At the pianist’s first solo concert, Mr Vernuchi… [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$gave her technical instructions during the performance.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$looked distressed.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$gave the speaker emotional support and encouragement.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{She found her playing a simple tune by ear }$  [1 mark]\n\n- *Also accept*: She found her playing a simple tune by ear at the piano (at his grandmother's).\n- *Accept other wording with the same meaning*. The correct answer must include the words “by ear.”\n\n- “I was eight when my mother was surprised to find me playing a simple tune by ear at the piano at my grandmother's.”\n\n- To play a tune *by ear* means to play the piano without reading any sheet music. It is not common to find a child playing a simple melody by ear at the piano without prior knowledge, which suggests the speaker as a child showed she had a musical ear.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{her childhood piano teacher}$  [1 mark]\n\n- *Also accept*: Mr. Vernuchi had been her childhood piano teacher.\n- *Accept other wording with the same meaning*.\n\n- “She asked Mr. Vernuchi, who had been her childhood piano teacher…”\n- Since she used to take piano lessons with Mr. Vernuchi when she was a child, she sent the speaker to take lessons with him.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A real prodigy.}$  [1 mark]\n\n- “Soon, he said to my parents I was “a real prodigy” at the piano…”\n- The word *prodigy* refers to a person, especially a child, who has an extraordinary talent; in the speaker’s case, she was a musical prodigy.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Her parents couldn't afford it.}$  [1 mark]\n\n- *Accept other wording with the same meaning*. The correct answer should include the idea of money as a problem.\n\n- The speaker says, “...he said to my parents (...) and that I should have one to play at home. But they couldn't afford it.” *To afford something* means to have the right amount of money to pay for something.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says, “At my first solo piano concert, he was sitting in the first row, constantly giving me a reassuring nod.” The word *reassuring* means the speaker’s mentor was trying to make her feel confident by smiling at the speaker during the performance; this is a sign of support and encouragement.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect because the speaker never mentions that the mentor was talking to her during the concert; he was sitting in the audience during the performance.\n- **Option B**: is not correct because the word *distressed* means worried or anxious. According to the speaker, the piano teacher was smiling during the performance."},{"ref":"EB0286","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0286 \n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nDear Sharon,\n\nHow are you? Sorry I've taken so long to write, but I've been swamped with exams! Algebra was a tough one - you know I don't get on well with figures, lol! Anyway, I thought I'd drop you a line to tell you about my new life at university. Classes are interesting and thought-provoking, and teachers always encourage us to give our best. The beginning of the term, however, was not fun. I had to fill in and sign so many forms and documents…#boring! So many administrative tasks and bureaucracy.\n\nI also have a new roommate named Emily Chen. She is from the UK. However, her parents were born in China. The other day, they dropped by for a couple of days, and they were so nice that they invited me to eat out at a traditional restaurant with them! It was an awesome experience.\n\nIn other news, I truly miss you, but I guess I will see you soon!  Can't wait to hear from you!\n\nLots of love\n\nJo\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer of the letter is overwhelmed with assessments.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Jo is writing to tell her friend about her university life.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Lectures are sometimes boring.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Teachers do not give their best.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$When the term began, Jo felt happy and relaxed.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The school required the writer to complete a lot of paperwork.\n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraph 2 which means the following:** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$visited    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$authentic    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1 - 3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*Las respuestas pueden estar en cualquier orden.*  \n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, F}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I've been swamped with exams”\n\nThe phrase *to be swamped* means to be *overwhelmed* by having too much of something, in this case, exams.\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I thought I'd *drop you a line* to tell you about my new life at university”\\\n\nThe phrase drop a line means to contact someone by sending a message or email. Jo is telling her friend about her $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$new life at university.”\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I had to fill in and sign so many forms and documents”\\\n\nThe word *paperwork* refers to *signing forms and documents*, which are the requirements of the University at the beginning of the year. \n\n- *Opciones Incorrectas:*  C, D, E\n\n- **Option C** is incorrect. The word *lecture* makes reference to classes at a University and the writer states that the *classes are thought-provoking*, which means they are really interesting. The writer does say that filling in and signing documents was boring.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. The text mentions that *teachers encourage students*, which implies that they do their best and try to motivate the students.\n\n- **Option E** is incorrect. The writer mentions  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The beginning of the term, however, was not fun.” The phrase *not fun* means that the writer did not enjoy the beginning of the school year, which is the opposite of *happy and relaxed*.   \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{dropped by}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The phrasal verb *drop* by means to visit someone spontaneously. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{traditional}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The words *traditional and authentic* can be considered synonyms in this context. The text makes reference to a restaurant where you can taste *authentic* Chinese food."},{"ref":"EB0287","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0287\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nOctober 2nd            \n\n**Overcoming adversity one movie at a time**\\\n\nBy Susan Stone\n\nHi movie freaks! Long time no see! I'm writing today to tell you about a movie that really blew my mind! It's called $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Blind Side”, and let me tell you, it is a must-see. One of the most astounding facts is that it is based on the true story of an American football player who went on to become an NFL superstar.\n\nIt stars Sandra Bullock as Leigh Anne Tuohy and Quinton Aaron as Michael Oher. He plays an African-American homeless boy who comes from an impoverished neighborhood and overcomes hardships thanks to the help of Tuohy's family. Leigh Ann is moved by compassion and decides to invite him to stay with them, and the whole family stands by Michael by providing tutors and study help and cheering him on when he plays football.\n\nThe movie is full of highs and lows, since Michael's life becomes overwhelming from time to time. The story is deeply heartfelt, and it is a clear example of how a person can overcome adversity with compassion and generosity.\n\nI recommend having a packet of tissues while watching this masterpiece!\n\nI'd love to read your comments about $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Blind Side”!\n\n**Answer the following questions.**  \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the writer describe the impact the movie had on them?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What word or phrase in paragraph 1 means this movie is not to be missed?    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What kind of story is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The Blind Side”?    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.**[3 marks]\n\nSandra Bullock plays Leigh Anne Tuohy, **[-4-]** Quinton Aaron stars as Michael Oher, a homeless African-American teenager from an impoverished neighborhood. **[-5-]** Leigh Anne is moved by compassion, she decides to invite him to stay with her family. Michael receives support from the Tuohys, including tutoring, academic assistance, and encouragement during his football games. **[-6-]** their help, he begins to overcome the many hardships in his life.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$although\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$meanwhile\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$because\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$while\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$thanks to\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$without\n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the writer, how will the movie make you feel?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Moved \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Entertained\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Relaxed\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Curious","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{blew my mind}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a movie that really blew my mind.”\\\n\nThe phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to blow somebody's mind” means to impress or to amaze someone. This means the reviewer was highly impressed by the movie.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{must-see}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it is a must-see.”\\\n\nThe compound word *must-see* means that the movie is essential to watch; therefore, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$movie-freaks” are advised not to miss it.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{true story}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it is based on the true story $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\\\n\nThe review mentions the movie is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$based on a true story.” When something is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$based on a true story,” it means it has derived from a true story. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The word *while* is used to pair the two roles in the story. \\\n\nOption **B** could be confusing in this space. *Meanwhile* introduces an action that occurs simultaneously with another, as described in the text. However, it would suggest that Sandra Bullock shifts to Quinton Aaron's role.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The word *because* is used to show cause and effect. In the text, the word *because* anticipates the reason why Anne decides to invite Michael to her house. \\\n\nOption **A**, *although*, could be confusing in this space, however, it indicates contrast, not cause and effect.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The phrase *thanks to* is used to show that something happened *because of* something else. Michael could overcome his problems *thanks to* the help he received.\\\n\nOption **C**, *because* could be confusing in the space. However, the preposition *of* is missing for the sentence to be grammatically correct.\\\n\nOption **F** could be confusing in this space. *Without* indicates the absence of something, but the sentence indicates Michael succeeded because of the help he received.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I recommend having a packet of tissues while watching this masterpiece!”\\\n\nThe writer recommends *a packet of tissues*, suggesting that you may cry during the movie. This implies that you may feel *moved* by the film. \n\n- *Opciones incorrectas:* B, C, D\n\n- **Option B**, *entertained*, is confusing. The review talks about the movie as it might be entertaining but the closing refers to the emotional impact of the movie.\n\n- **Option C**, *relaxed*, is incorrect. The movie is described as an emotional story, which suggests it is intense, not relaxing.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. The review does not focus on how *curious* you might feel about the story."},{"ref":"EB0288","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0288\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**Inspiring People: Lizzie Velazquez**\n\nLizzie Velasquez is a motivational speaker and inspiring person. She was born with a rare condition (only two other people in the world have it!) that prevents her body from storing fat or building muscle, yet her organs and brain developed normally. Despite facing health challenges and bullying from a young age, Lizzie has become a powerful voice for kindness and resilience.\n\nOne of the key moments in her life was when she was 17. A video labeling her negatively went viral and gathered a ton of terrible comments. Instead of allowing this to define her or affect her, she used the experience to work harder on her mission to inspire and be an example to others. \n\nToday, Lizzie is a successful motivational speaker, author, and YouTuber. Her TEDx talks and her documentary *A Brave Heart* have reached millions. In her book *Dare to Be Kind*, she shares how compassion and self-acceptance can change lives. Lizzie promotes the idea of being $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$differently-abled” rather than $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$disabled,” encouraging people to see beauty in uniqueness.\n\nHer story is a reminder that everyone deserves respect and the chance to shine.\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Two more people in the world have the same disease as Lizzie.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Lizzie Velazquez suffered bullying, and this made her depressed.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$At 17, she went viral and this was a negative experience.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$After the viral video, she decided to become a source of hope and inspiration for others.    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.** [3 marks]\n\n**[-5-]**, Lizzie Velasquez is a well-known speaker, writer, and YouTuber. Her TEDx presentations and the film *A Brave Heart* have been watched by millions of people. **[-6-]**, her book *Dare to Be Kind* describes how showing care and accepting yourself can make a difference. Lizzie thinks that people should be seen as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$differently-abled” **[-7-]** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$disabled,” helping others to appreciate the value in being unique.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$however\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$instead of\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$in addition\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$because\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$today\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$actually","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$only two other people in the world have it!”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe text mentions that Lizzie has a *rare condition* or *disease*. And specifies that only two other people in the world have it, too.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$has become a powerful voice for kindness and resilience”\\\n\n\\\n\nLizzie did not get depressed after being bullied, instead she became an advocate for kindness and resilience.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$gathered a ton of terrible comments”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe viral video was a bad experience for Lizzie, as it had nasty (*terrible*) comments about her. *Gathered* is a synonym of *collected*.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$to inspire and be an example to others”\\\n\n\\\n\nTo *be an example* means *to inspire others*. The text mentions that the negative experience from the viral video made her work harder to be an inspiration.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$    [1 mark]\n\n- *Today* refers to her current activities, to what she is doing.\\\n\n\\\n\nOption **F**, *actually*, is incorrect. *Actually* means *really* and it is used to emphasize that something someone said or did is surprising.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- *In addition* is a connector used to add more information, in this case, about Lizzie's achievements.\\\n\n\\\n\nOption **D**, *because*, could be confusing in this space. However,  the word *because* would introduce a reason for something, not add information.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- *Instead of* shows contrast between two ideas *differently-abled* and *disabled*)\\\n\n\\\n\nOption **A**, *however*, is incorrect as it is used to show contrast between two sentences, not words or phrases."}]},{"name":"Migration","questions":[{"ref":"EB0031","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0031\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\nImmigrant farmworkers are vital to U.S. agriculture, making up an estimated 73% of the agricultural workforce. $\\underline{\\textrm{This}}$ labor-intensive work is essential to the economy, with a total value of over \\$1 trillion, yet it faces an ongoing labor shortage that was made worse by the COVID-19 pandemic. Farmworkers often work long hours in all types of weather, and $\\underline{\\textrm{some}}$ face dangerous conditions - like exposure to chemicals and pesticides - without access to medical care, which means fewer Americans are applying for these jobs.\n\nIn California, 56\\% of farmers reported struggling to find enough labor over the last five years. This shortage leads to increased imports, food waste, and economic losses. The dairy industry, for example, could see doubled retail milk prices if these workers were not permitted to work in the U.S.  The inability to find reliable sources of agricultural labor leads to increased imports of fruit and vegetables, raising consumer prices. Without immigrant farmworkers, crops are not gathered or harvested, which contributes to food waste. \n\n**To whom or to what do the following words refer?  Answer using words as they appear in the text.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{This}}$ labor-intensive work...    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$and $\\underline{\\textrm{some}}$ face dangerous conditions...    [1 mark]\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why are fewer Americans applying for jobs as farmworkers?  Give **two** examples.    [2 marks]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What economic impact does the lack of agricultural workers have on consumers? Give **two** examples.    [2 marks]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Agricultural work}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The paragraph begins by identifying the subject of the article ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$immigrant farmworkers\"), and then states two facts about them. The work they do is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$vital\" to American agriculture, and it is this work which is referred to in the second sentence.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Farmworkers}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The sentence begins with the subject $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Farmworkers\", followed by a list of the challenges they may face while doing their work. Information is given about a group (or sub-set) of these farmworkers who may face dangerous exposure to chemicals and pesticides, indicated by the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$some”.\n\n$3$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\textrm{often work long hours in all types of weather}$ \n\n- $\\textrm{Some face dangerous conditions}$\n\n- The third sentence in paragraph one lists several of the challenges farmworkers face. The list includes unpleasant and even dangerous conditions, which make the work unattractive to people seeking work. Few Americans wish to do this work, so immigrant farmworkers make up the majority of agricultural workers.[2 marks]\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\textrm{doubled (retail) milk prices}$\n\n- $\\textrm{raising consumer prices}$\n\n- Paragraph two details the impact that the lack of farmworkers can have. One of the details highlights increased imports ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$inability to find reliable sources of agricultural labor leads to increased imports”), one specifically mentions food waste ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$crops are not gathered or harvested”), and two mention direct impacts on consumers. [2 marks]"},{"ref":"EB0032","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0032\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nMany professionals face difficult choices when conflicts $\\underline{\\textrm{arise}}$ in their countries.  The choice of staying or trying to find a new country to live in - to emigrate -  is extremely tough to make, but many people choose to leave, believing that their families will be safer and that there will be new opportunities - for themselves and their children - in a conflict-free country.\n\nMany countries welcome professionals and their families, assisting them in finding new homes and work, providing medical care, and $\\underline{\\textrm{ensuring}}$ that children go to school.  But for some, the process of moving and finding good work is not that simple.  Doctors often find that there are licensing $\\underline{\\textrm{barriers}}$ for them: despite their skills and years of experience, they find that they are not qualified to practice medicine in their new country.  Many must make another painful choice: to take a lower-paying job in a $\\underline{\\textrm{field}}$ outside of medicine to support their family immediately or go through the sometimes very difficult and expensive process of requalifying as a doctor. \n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [4 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$arise |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$grassland|\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$ensuring|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$guaranteeing|\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$barriers|$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$get up|\n\n|4.$\\hspace{1em}$field|$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$obstacles|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$insuring|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$area|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$G.$\\hspace{1em}$occur|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$H.$\\hspace{1em}$fences|\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text.  Both parts are required for [1 mark].**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Families of new immigrants are well received in many countries.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Professionals who choose to move to another country to escape conflict in their own country can immediately find appropriate work.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- While **Option C**, *get up*, is another meaning of the word *arise*, it does not fit in the context of the text (conflicts happening in a country). **Option G**, *Occur*, means *to happen*, which is the exact meaning in the text.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Option B.** To *guarantee*, means to be sure that something happens, making it the correct choice.  The word *ensure* may be easily confused with the word *insure*, which means to make safe.   **Option E** is, therefore, incorrect.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The word *barriers* has several meanings, one of which is *fences* (**Option H**). However, in the context of this text, the meaning of the word barrier is implied to be negative ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...$\\underline{\\textrm{despite}}$ their skills and years of experience they find they are not qualified to practice medicine…”).  Therefore, the correct choice is *obstacles*.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- A *field* of expertise is the *area* in which a person has a lot of experience or skills, which makes it the correct choice. An alternative meaning for *field* is a large open area, usually covered with grass or some other plants - a field of corn, for example - however, **Option A**, *grassland*, does not fit in the context of the text.  \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:**  Many countries welcome professionals and their families\n\n- The text states that being accepted into a country as an immigrant can be a very positive experience, as shown in the sentence above.   \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- **Justification:** But for some, the process of moving and finding good work is not that simple.\n\n- The main theme of the text is that although immigrants may be welcomed into a new country, there are some professions - specifically related to medicine - in which even highly qualified and experienced doctors may not be able to work in their field."},{"ref":"EB0033","marks":"8","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0033\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\nI don't like cold. I have lived in Toronto for two years and am still not used to the cold weather. It's so different from Manaus, my hometown in Brazil, where the weather is always hot and rainy. I did not want to move, but my parents believed that studying abroad was better than the education we could get at home, so they worked very hard and saved up enough money for us to move to Canada.  \n\nWhen I arrived in Canada, I did not speak any English. I was 13 years old and in school, I had to take special English classes for kids like me. At first, I felt quite isolated and overwhelmed by how hard it was to learn a new language. But soon I realized that in that class there were so many kids whose parents had made the same decision as mine, and we all had the same goal: to learn English as quickly as we could. We wanted to get into mainstream classes - the classes for students who spoke English - so we could $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fit in\". So we studied hard and learned to enjoy life in this great city as we got better and better in our English. I made my first friends in that class - kids like me who were homesick and couldn't communicate well, but were kind and funny and, most importantly, determined to please our parents.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**  \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What emotions did the writer feel when starting school in Toronto? Give **two** answers.    [2 marks]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What were the main motivators for the students to study hard and learn English?  Give **two** examples.    [2 marks]\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentence. Answer using the words as they appear in the text.**\n\nThe writer still missed the climate in Manaus, where it is **[-3-]**    [1 mark]\n\nThe decision to emigrate was based on parents' understanding that **[-4-]**    [1 mark]\n\nThe immigrant students wanted to learn quickly and join the local students so they **[-5-]**    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ The immigrant students had several common traits, which included being **[-6-]**    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$  [2 marks]\n\n- $\\textrm{isolated}$\n\n- $\\textrm{overwhelmed}$\n\n- The text clearly states that the writer did not speak any English and felt “quite isolated and overwhelmed”.\n\n$2$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$  [2 marks]\n\n- $\\textrm{get into mainstream classrooms}$\n\n- The writer mentions all kids in the wanted to learn English “as quickly as they (we) could” and then mentions they all wanted to “fit in”\n\n- $\\textrm{please their parents}$\n\n- At the end of the paragraph, the writer mentions that it was very important for them to “please their parents” making this a cleare reason to learn English quickly.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{hot and rainy}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “It’s so different from Manaus, my hometown in Brazil, where the weather is always hot and rainy\"\n\n- The writer mentions they have lived in Toronto for two years but they are “still not used to the cold weather” which implies they still miss the weather in Manaus, their hometown.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{studying abroad was better}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “my parents believed that studying abroad was better than the education we could get at home”\n\n- The meaning of the phrase *my parents believed* is similar to the meaning of *the parents’ understanding* and they both imply the parents’ thought education in another country would be better than the education in their home country.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{could “fit in”}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “We wanted to get into mainstream classes – the classes for students who spoke English - so we could ‘fit in’.”\n\n- The writer mentions immigrant students made an effort to join their “local” peers. The phrase *fit in* means to belong or to be accepted.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{determined to please (our parents)}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “kids like me … determined to please our parents.”\n\n- The writer mentions immigrant kids were similar and they all were determined to please their parents which means to make them happy."},{"ref":"EB0182","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0182\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a cultural historian explaining how migration enriches societies.**\n\n:audio{#b3458111-3d28-4464-bf47-67451c1f1631 aid=\"b3458111-3d28-4464-bf47-67451c1f1631\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do migrants bring with them when they move?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What are **two** of the most commonly seen areas of cultural influence?    [2 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did pizza, sushi, and tacos become popular worldwide?    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What happens to local culture when migrants arrive?    [1 mark]\n\n    \n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$By blending different traditions, jazz and hip-hop appeared as new musical **[-5-]**.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Migration does replace local cultures, it makes them **[-6-]**.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{their traditions}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When people move, they bring their traditions with them”\\\n\nImmigrants bring their cultural traditions when they move to a new place, and over time, some of these traditions blend (become part of) into the local culture. The word *their* is key in this answer, as immigrants bring traditions that belong to their countries.\n\n2.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{food}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{music}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One of the most visible examples is food” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$music is another area where cultures blend”\\\n\nThese lines indicate two areas in which cultural influence is most visible  or seen.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{migration}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$many of the world's most popular dishes, like pizza, sushi, and tacos, spread due to migration”\\\n\nAlthough these foods may now be found all over the world, they began in a few specific locations or countries (Italy, Japan, and Mexico), and were taken to new places by immigrants.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(it) expands}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rather than replacing local culture, migration expands it”\\\n\nThe text tells us that the traditions that immigrants bring to their new home expands the local culture and does not replace it. For example, eating a pizza outside of Italy, for example in London, does not negatively impact the culture of the U.K. - their traditional foods still exist and people still love them.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{genres}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Genres like jazz, reggae, and hip-hop emerged from the mixing of different traditions.”\\\n\nA genre is a type or style of something, usually related to art, music, and literature.  When multiple cultures come into contact, these arts can blend (mix), creating something entirely new. Emerge means to come out of, so these new genres come from combining different elements of multiple cultures. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{richer}$\n\n- $\\textrm{more diverse}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rather than replacing local culture, migration expands it, creating something richer and more diverse.”\\\n\nThe text indicates that migration enriches - makes them richer -  local cultures and makes them more varied or diverse."},{"ref":"EB0183","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0183\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**  \n\n**You are going to hear an immigration center administrator explaining the role of volunteers.**\n\n:audio{#1aafd10f-a65c-4f85-bbfa-edc8fecbbe26 aid=\"1aafd10f-a65c-4f85-bbfa-edc8fecbbe26\"}\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The center provides housing for all new arrivals.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Volunteers help new arrivals adapt to their new environment.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The coordinator knows that simply communicating with immigrants can positively impact their perspective.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The immigration center helps immigrants find jobs.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Many immigrants feel insecure when they first arrive.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Volunteers only assist adults, not children.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Acts of kindness help immigrants feel less overwhelmed.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Volunteers are paid for their work at the immigration center.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the main purpose of the immigration center's volunteer program?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To ensure immigrants complete all required paperwork.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To support new arrivals in multiple aspects of their adjustment.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To help immigrants return to their home countries\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the text mention a smile, a shared meal, or a listening ear?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To highlight the differences between the volunteers and the   immigrants.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To emphasize the importance of learning a new language.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To show that small gestures can make a big difference.","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, C, E, G}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **B.** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They help new arrivals adjust to life in a foreign country”\n\n\\\n\nThe volunteers play an important role in helping newly arrived immigrants *adjust* to their new home. *Adapt* is a close synonym of *adjust*, which means to make changes to fit in a new situation or environment.\n\n- **C.** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I see firsthand how a simple conversation can change someone's outlook.”\n\n\\\n\nThe coordinator can see how conversations between volunteers and immigrants can help improve their *outlook* or *perspective*. To $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$see something firsthand\" is to experience it personally.\n\n- **E.** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Many immigrants arrive feeling lost, unsure of where to start.”  \\\n\nImmigrants face many challenges when they arrive in a new location and the number of things they need to do can be overwhelming.  But with the support and guidance of the *volunteers, they feel less *insecure and are able to begin to adapt. *Insecure* is similar in meaning to *unsure* or *lost*.\n\n- **G.** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$they provide kindness. A smile, a shared meal, or even a listening ear can make an overwhelming situation feel manageable”\\\n\nAn $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$act of kindness\" is something done for someone simply out of a feeling of care, support, or friendship. An example is opening the door for someone, especially if you don't know them. Kindness helps new immigrants begin to feel safe and secure in their new environment and allows them to begin to take the steps they need to settle in.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, D, F, H\n\n- **A.** The immigration center does not provide housing. They do provide other services as the volunteers $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$assist with paperwork, explain transportation systems, and even help children with school assignments”.  Providing housing - a place to live - is not mentioned in the text.\n\n- **D.** The center does not specifically help new immigrants find jobs.  The text states that volunteers $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$assist with paperwork”, but is not specific about what kind.\n\n- **F.** The volunteers assist immigrants of all ages, helping adults with paperwork and understanding how to get around ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$explain transportation systems”), and some even help children with homework assignments.\n\n- **H.** Volunteers are people who work without receiving payment for their services. Many people volunteer to contribute to their community or to be active in an organization they share values with.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They help new arrivals adjust to life in a foreign country, offering language support, cultural guidance, and emotional encouragement.”\\\n\nIn addition to the actions given above, volunteers provide support in other ways: they $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$assist with paperwork, explain transportation systems, and even help children with school assignments”.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They help new arrivals adjust to life in a foreign country.“ Although assisting with paperwork is one of the ways volunteers help immigrants, it is not the main purpose for them being present.\n\n- **C**: The immigration center helps newly arrived immigrants.  They are assisting them in getting settled into their new location, not in assisting them in getting back to their home countries.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$More than anything, they provide kindness. A smile, a shared meal, or even a listening ear can make an overwhelming situation feel manageable.”\\\n\nThe small *gestures* (non-spoken movements or actions that show a feeling or emotion) mentioned show the immigrants that the volunteers care about them. The volunteers are happy to share time ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a listening ear means\" that you are ready to listen and pay attention to a person), food (a *meal*) or just show that they care with a gentle smile.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B\n\n- **A**: All of the small gestures or acts of kindness shown by the volunteers are intended to *highlight* (emphasize or show the importance of something) the similarities between people, not the differences.  Everyone likes to be cared for, treated kindly, and encouraged when facing challenges, which is what the volunteers do for the newly arrived immigrants.\n\n- **B**: None of the gestures mentioned requires the people involved to share a language.  While learning the language of the new location will be needed, it is not being emphasized (*highlighted*) by the gestures or acts of kindness mentioned."},{"ref":"EB0184","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0184\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a professional football player share their migration experience.**\n\n:audio{#1eb84668-11a0-4146-863f-4bba23f014a9 aid=\"1eb84668-11a0-4146-863f-4bba23f014a9\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What was the biggest challenge for the speaker after moving abroad?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The intense training sessions.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The difficulty of adapting to life outside of training.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The lack of proper training facilities.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why was forming friendships a challenge for the speaker?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They didn't speak the language well.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Their teammates were unfriendly.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They had no free time.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why did the speaker almost give up?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They weren't enjoying the training sessions.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They were injured during the trip abroad.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They felt isolated and missed home.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ What motivated the speaker to change their mindset?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$A coach's words of encouragement.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$A visit from their family.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$A new teammate who spoke their language.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker mean by $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Slowly, things got better\"?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They decided to return home.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Their training sessions became easier.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They gradually adapted to their new life.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the speaker feel when looking back on their migration experience?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They regretted the decision to move.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They felt it was a positive experience.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They wished they had moved to a different country.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$but the real challenge was off the field”\\\n\nThe footballer didn't speak the language of their new country very well, and he felt lonely. The phrase off the field means that the challenges the footballer faced were not related to football but to their everyday life outside the field.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the training was intense”, which should be expected if you are training to be a professional player. But this was not the biggest problem for the player.\n\n- **C**: There is no mention of what kind of facilities the new team had, but the training was intense, so there must have been some good-quality facilities (buildings, equipment or services needed for a particular purpose) for the players to train in.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I didn't speak the language well, and making friends was difficult.”\\\n\nThis was one of the biggest challenges for the player.  Because he couldn't communicate well, he felt *isolated* (alone and lonely).\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C\n\n- **B**: The speaker does not mention his new teammates, so we do not know if they were friendly or not.\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the training was intense\" shows that the player was busy, but it does not mean that there was no free time for making friends.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Making friends was difficult. I missed home, my family, and my old teammates.”\\\n\nThe player was having trouble adapting to the new environment.  He was lonely and isolated, which made him consider giving up and returning home.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B\n\n- **A**: The player calls the training intense, but that does not mean that they weren't enjoying the experience and instead wanted to go home.\n\n- **B**: There is no mention of any kind of injury, so **option B** is incorrect.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$One day, a coach pulled me aside and said, $`$Struggles make champions.' That changed my mindset.”\\\n\nThe player was thinking about leaving because he was having a hard time adapting to the new environment, but his coach told him that when one *struggles* (tries very hard to overcome a problem or difficulty), one becomes stronger. The player stopped thinking about his situation as a problem and began to see it as an opportunity to grow.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C\n\n- **B**: The player says: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I missed home, my family”, but there is no mention that anyone from their family came to visit.\n\n- **C**: There is no mention that any of their teammates spoke their language, therefore **option C** is incorrect.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I started working harder, both on and off the field. Slowly, things got better.”\\\n\nWith the words of encouragement from their coach, the player began to work harder. The extra effort improved everything, and eventually the player began to feel more at home.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B\n\n- **A**: The player had considered returning home but in the end, decided to stay and work even harder. Therefore, **option A** is incorrect.\n\n- **B**: The player says “I started working harder” but there is no mention that the training became easier.  Therefore, **option B** is incorrect.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Looking back, migration tested me in ways I never imagined—but it also made me stronger.\\\n\nThe player felt that overcoming the challenges of migration had strengthened them as a person.  They were able to overcome loneliness and homesickness as well as the challenges that learning a language and forming new relationships presented, alongside becoming a better football player.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C\n\n- **A**: Although there were some hard times, the player does not regret (wish something had not happened) making the decision to move abroad.  With time, they were able to see the challenges they overcame as a positive aspect of the move.\n\n- **C**:  The player does not mention wishing they had moved somewhere else.  Therefore, **option C** is incorrect."}]},{"name":"Rites of Passage","questions":[{"ref":"EB0025","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0025\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Rites of passage}}$\n\n**[-1-]**\n\nA rite of passage is a particular type of ritual, conducted to mark an important transition in somebody’s life. These rituals most commonly follow people from the cradle to the grave. An initiation ritual transforms a person from their current state to a new one.\n\n**[-2-]**\n\nRites of passage have multi-layered meanings. The purpose and intent of the ritual can be social or psychological as well as spiritual or religious. Certain rites of passage represent first and foremost transformations in the religious status or circumstances of the initiated. Religious initiation rituals primarily have a spiritual meaning.\n\n**[-3-]**\n\nInitiation rituals are not limited to religions and form part of every society around the globe. Within our great cultural variety, we find many commonalities in the way $\\underline{\\textrm{these events}}$ are structured. French anthropologist Arnold van Gennep (1873-1957) is famous for   $\\underline{\\textrm{his}}$ analysis of Rites of Passage. In his book *Les Rites de Passage* (1909), he shows that these rites include three phases: \n\n- Separation\n\n- Transition\n\n- Reincorporation.\n\n**[-4-]**\n\nEach initiation ritual has three stages: the pre-liminal, liminal, and post-liminal. The Latin word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$limen” means a threshold, and the liminal world forms the threshold between the old and the new.\n\n[Adapted from  The social importance of rites of passage and initiations by The Horizon of Reason used under CC BY-SA 4.0. \n\nPrevos, P. (2001). The social importance of rites of passage and initiations. The Horizon of Reason. Available at https://horizonofreason.com/culture/rites-of-passage/ Accessed 9th November 2024. CC BY-SA 4.0.]\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading for each paragraph of the text** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The structure of rituals\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The importance of rituals\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Understanding rites of passage\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Latin rituals\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The importance of religious rituals\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The multifaceted significance of rituals\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Phases of initiation rites\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Initiation rituals\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ … in the way $\\underline{\\textrm{these events}}$ are structured.[1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ In  $\\underline{\\textrm{his}}$ *Les Rites de Passage*    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This paragraph summarizes the main aspects to understand what rites of passage are and how long they last.\n\n- **Option H** is a distractor for this space. Paragraph 1 mentions initiation rituals; however, it provides an overview of the rites of passage and only mentions initiation rituals without providing an in-depth explanation.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Multifaceted* refers to the multiple meanings of the rites mentioned in paragraph 2, which are social, psychological, and religious.\n\n- **Option E**: Although paragraph 2 mentions religious rituals as examples of rites of passage, the focus is on the multiple meanings of these rites: psychological, social, and religious.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Paragraph 3 explains how initiation rituals are structured following Genepp's theory of Rites of Passage (separation, transition, reincorporation).\n\n- **Option B** is incorrect as the paragrpah does not discuss the imporance of rituals but the typical phases of rituals.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This paragraph explains the 3 phases of initiation rites: pre-liminal, liminal, post post-liminal.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect as the Latin reference in paragraph 4 refers to the word limen (threshold between the old and the new), not to the rituals themselves.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{initiation rituals}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The reference to *initiation rituals* can be found at the beginning of the paragraph. In this particular case, just mentioning *rituals* without the word *initiation* would be incorrect, as the section clearly refers to these particular rituals and not others.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(French anthropologist) Arnold van Gennep.}$   [1 mark]\n\n- His is a possessive adjective that is used to refer to a male person. This person is mentioned in the previous statement."},{"ref":"EB0026","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0026\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Rituals}}$\n\nMost people associate this term with preindustrial societies and the initiation rites of boys or girls entering their adult counterpart. However, postindustrial societies have many examples of rites of passage that we may not think about because they are not usually enforced by an authority. Getting a driver's or marriage license, becoming eligible to vote, or buying alcohol are all commonly celebrated rites of passage that are sanctioned by our government. Less institutionalized rites of passage in our culture would be a baby shower, a Sweet 16 or Quinceañera celebration, or a high school graduation ceremony. For each of these events, the individual knows that, after the ceremony, they have a new status and therefore a new set of expectations. For example, after graduating high school, individuals are expected to either get a job or go to college.\n\nRites of passage are helpful for the individual in that they include the individual in a social group, which will ideally support that individual in many ways. Rites of passage are also functional for society because they publicly acknowledge the individual's transition and new status, highlighting society's expectations for them in an effort to maintain cultural norms.\n\nAdapted from Social Structure: The Building Blocks of Social Lifes by Christina Miller-Bellor and Donna Giuliani, used under CC BY-SA 4.0 \n\nMiller-Bellor, C., & Giuliani, D. (n.d.). Chapter 5: Social structure and social interaction. In Local to Global: The Sociological Journey. University of Minnesota Libraries. https://open.lib.umn.edu/sociology/chapter/5-1-social-structure-the-building-blocks-of-social-life/ (Accessed November 14, 2024). CC BY-SA 4.0.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.** [4 marks]\n\nRites of passage **[-1-]** an important transition in a person's life, signifying a major step or phase in a person's life. **[-2-]** the names and details of life stages may vary from culture to culture, all cultures recognize rites of passage to mark life course **[-3-**].  Events like obtaining a driver's license, marriage license, or voting eligibility are government-sanctioned rites of passage. Other ceremonies, like baby showers, Sweet 16 or Quinceañera celebrations, and high school graduations, also signify personal transitions. Rites of passage **[-4-]** the individual with group support and society by confirming the cultural expectations and transitions.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$however\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$development\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$help\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$mark\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$serve\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$although\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$represent\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$movement\n\n**Answer the following question.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ How do rites of passage benefit both individuals and societies? Provide   $\\underline{\\textrm{two}}$ answers.[2 marks]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *mark* (a transition) is the correct verb for this gap as it is used to refer to a specific point or moment in a person's life.\n\n- **Option G**, *represent*, could fit gap 1. However, as the text refers to the transitions in a person's life, *mark* is a more appropriate word to show a point in this changing state.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *although* is used in this gap to express contrast between the 2 sentences.\n\n- **Option A**, *however*, could fit gap 2, because it is a connector, but it needs a follow-up comma, so punctuation indicates that the correct option is *although*.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *development* in this gap is used to refer to the evolution of a life's course.\n\n- **Option H**, *movement*, could fit gap 3, however, this word implies relocation, rather than the progression through stages in a person's life\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *serve*  in this gap means help with the individuals with support.\n\n- **Option C**, *help*, could fit gap 4, because is a verb. However, the verb *help* means to aid someone or make something simpler. It does not convey the active role of rituals in *serving* or guiding a person through the process.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1.5em}$ :answer[**they include the individual in a social group**] [1 mark]\n\n- $\\hspace{0.5em}$ :answer[**they publicly acknowledge the individual’s transition (and new status)**]  [1 mark]\n\n- *Accept other wording with similar meaning*\n\n- The last paragraph highlights and explains the benefits of rituals for the individual and society.\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rites of passage are helpful for the individual” indicates the following words will provide this information. Similarly, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Rites of passage are also functional for society” will introduce their relevance for society as a whole."},{"ref":"EB0027","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0027\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{La Quinceañera: A Rite of Passage among Latina Adolescents}}$\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hurry, *mamá*!” Laura begged her mom. She was hurriedly trying to unlace the glittery, bedazzled gown to put on her Jalisco dress to dance to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$El Son de la Negra” with her *corte de chambelanes y damas*, her court of young men and women who would accompany her. Amy, Laura's mom, clumsily helped Laura unlace the bodice of the dress, then helped Laura step into her Jalisco dress, careful not to get any of Laura's curls stuck in the zipper. The scene felt like a fiasco because the guests were waiting to see the *baile sorpresa* (surprise dance). It was Laura's quinceañera, the elaborate party and celebration of Laura turning 15 a few months ago, a ritual that many other Latina girls her age had gone through or were already planning for their birthdays. Laura had practiced the dance for months with her court members, and it was almost the moment to debut the choreographed and stylized dance for the party guests, a mixture of Laura's extended family members, friends, Amy's coworkers, and even guests that had flown to Chicago for the big event.\n\nAdapted from Pressbooks.pub. 5.3.3 In Focus: La Quinceañera: A Rite of Passage among Latina Adolescents, used under CC BY-SA 4.0 \\\n\nPearce, E. B. (n.d.). In focus: La Quinceañera: A rite of passage among Latina adolescents. In Contemporary Families: An Equity Lens (2nd ed.), Chapter 5.3: Traditions and rituals. Open Oregon Educational Resources. https://openoregon.pressbooks.pub/families2nd2e/chapter/5-3-traditions-and-rituals/ (Accessed November 14, 2024)\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in the text.** \n\nLaura urgently asked her mom to **[-1-]** her dress.    [1 mark]\n\nThe scene felt chaotic, as guests were waiting for the surprise dance Laura had been rehearsing with her **[-2-]**.    [1 mark]\n\nLaura’s mother struggled with the bodice before helping Laura into her Jalisco dress, ensuring her **[-3-]** didn’t get caught in the zipper.    [1 mark]\n\nThe celebration was Laura’s quinceañera, a tradition many Latin girls celebrate when **[-4-]** 15.    [1 mark]\n\nLaura’s party had a variety of guests, including her friends, **[-5-]**, Amy’s colleagues, and even guests from other cities.    [1 mark]\n\n**The following sentences are either True or False. Write True or False then justify them using words as they appear in the text.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, Laura’s quinceañera party was a simple celebration.     [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Laura’s quinceañera party took place in a Latin country.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{unlace}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unlace the glittery, bedazzled gown”\n\n\\\n\n*To unlace* a dress, means to untie a dress. Laura asked her mom for help as she was in a hurry and needed to change her dress for the dance.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{court}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$her court of young men and women”\n\n\\\n\n*Court* refers to the young men and women who would accompany Laura in her dance.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{curls}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not to get any of Laura's curls stuck in the zipper”\\\n\n  A *curl* is a lock of hair in the form of a spiral. Laura's mother, who was in a hurry, made sure to prevent Laura's hair from being caught by the zipper. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{turning}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$celebration of Laura turning 15”\\\n\nTo *turn* a particular age means reaching that age, so *turn 15* means reaching the age of 15.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{extended family members}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a mixture of Laura's extended family members”\\\n\n According to the text, the guests at the party were not only Laura's friends but also her distant relatives.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$guests that had flown to Chicago for the big event.”\\\n\nThe party was held in Chicago, USA. The text mentions some guests had to fly to get there.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Laura's quinceañera, the elaborate party and celebration”\\\n\nThe word *elaborate* indicates the party was carefully planned and had many details."},{"ref":"EB0152","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0152\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a person’s experience of living alone for the first time.**\n\n:audio{#128e8b89-9bb4-4193-aee2-78cca6469522 aid=\"128e8b89-9bb4-4193-aee2-78cca6469522\"}\n\n1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Living alone is easy from the start.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Paying rent and doing chores are part of living alone.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Independence comes with no responsibilities.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Most young adults never feel lonely after moving out.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Over time, people learn to manage independence.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Moving out of the family home is a stepping stone in becoming an adult.\n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What are some responsibilities of living independently? Give **two** examples. [2 marks]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is one of the emotional challenges of living alone?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, E, F}$   [3 marks]\n\n- **B**. The speaker tells us: “I soon learned that being independent also came with responsibilities: paying rent, doing laundry, and fixing problems”\n\n- **E**. “...eventually, I learned to manage these challenges and my independence grew” The speaker recognized the benefits they received from being able to handle the responsibility of living alone, and felt that they were prepared for life as an adult.\n\n- **F**. “Moving out of my family’s home was a major step into adulthood.” The speaker recognizes the importance of this move and the role it played in his development.  A *stepping stone* is a metaphorical expression that means an action that helps to make progress towards a goal. In this context, it is similar in meaning to a *major step*.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: A, C, D\n\n- **A**.  The speaker tells us that “...eventually, I learned to manage these challenges…”, showing that living alone was not easy from the start, but it did get better with time.\n\n- **C**. The speaker tells us the opposite: “I soon learned that being independent also came with responsibilities.”\n\n- **D**. We learn that the speaker did feel lonely: “I felt a bit lonely…” but we do not have any information on how other young people feel about their experience of living alone.\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$*Any two, in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{paying rent}$\n-   $\\textrm{doing laundry}$\n-   $\\textrm{fixing problems}$\n\n- “being independent also came with responsibilities: paying rent, doing laundry, and fixing problems (like a leaky sink).  The speaker learned that it wasn’t always fun being on their own, and that chores needed to be done.  [2 marks]\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{feeling lonely}$\n\n- “I also discovered that I felt a bit lonely…” The text tells us about the challenges the speaker felt: paying the rent (economic challenge), doing laundry (physical challenge), fixing problems (problem-solving challenge), and feeling lonely (emotional challenge). [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0153","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0153\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on coming-of-age ceremonies.**\n\n:audio{#d9c8ffad-e351-40e8-a264-39da45467de0 aid=\"d9c8ffad-e351-40e8-a264-39da45467de0\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is a common feature of coming-of-age ceremonies? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They celebrate the transition to adulthood\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They happen only at age 16\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They are always private events\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How do families participate in these traditions?[1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$By ignoring the celebration\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$By taking part in or observing the ceremony\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$By deciding when adulthood begins\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$At what age do young people in Japan become legal adults? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$15\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$16\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$20\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is a quincea$\\tilde{\\ce{n}}$era? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$A legal ceremony in Japan.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$A 15th birthday celebration.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$A party for 18-year-olds.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Which of the following is a key element of coming-of-age ceremonies? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Learning new skills for adulthood\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Wearing special clothesRecognition from family and society\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Recognition from family and society","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Although they differ from culture to culture,...these ‘coming of age’ events … celebrate the end of childhood and the beginning …of adulthood.” \\\nThe end of childhood is a special time and in many cultures the celebration of it is an important symbol of the child’s readiness to accept more responsibilities.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **Option B**: “The age at which one is considered an adult varies.”\\\n In the text, three different examples are given for the age children transition into adulthood in different cultures. 16 years old  is the example given for the United States.\n\n- **Option C**: “...usually the family and community of the young person participate in or observe…”\\\n  The text tells us that in some cultures the whole community is part of the celebration, which means that it is not private.  *Private* means personal and not to be shared, or something that is restricted to a specific group of people.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “... the family and community of the young person participate in or observe these ‘coming of age’ events…”\\\n  The text states that families are involved in these ceremonies, but without giving specific examples.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: The participation of family is important in marking this transition in a young person’s life.  The text does not mention that the family ignores the celebration.\n\n- **Option C**: “The age at which one is considered an adult varies.”  The age is not decided by the parents, but by the community.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “In Japan, turning 20 is the moment a young person becomes a legal adult…”\\\n  The text provides this information.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: “...in Central America and Mexico, girls are 15 when the festive quinceanera is held.” \\\n15 is the age of adulthood in these cultures.\n\n- **Option B**: “...many families celebrate with a ‘Sweet 16’ party…”\\\n 16 is often considered the age of adulthood for girls in the United States.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “...in Central America and Mexico, 15 is the age at which the festive quinceanera is held.”\\\n15 is the age at which many girls in Spanish-speaking Central America, Mexico and the United States are considered to be adults.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: The text mentions that “In Japan, turning 20 is the moment a young person becomes a legal adult…”, but there is no mention of any name given to celebrations held there.\n\n- **Option C**: There are no celebrations for 18 year-olds mentioned in the text.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “...the family and community of the young person participate in or observe these ‘coming of age’ events…”\\\n These celebrations are important markers of the\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: The text states that the purpose of the ceremony is to celebrate “the beginning of the privileges and responsibilities of adulthood.”\\\n  There is no mention of specific skills that needed to be learned as part of or before the ceremony.\n\n- **Option B**: Although some celebrations do include special clothing, they are not mentioned in the text."},{"ref":"EB0154","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0154\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about weddings.**\n\n:audio{#4f1cfd0c-88fd-4951-a531-2d07d3c1ad67 aid=\"4f1cfd0c-88fd-4951-a531-2d07d3c1ad67\"}\n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Different cultures have unique **[-1-]** that connect wedding participants to each other. [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Honoring **[-2-]** is important in Chinese weddings.  [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$To represent eternity, **[-3-]**  is part of many weddings in Canada. [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Prosperity is represented in Greek weddings when **[-4-]**. [1 mark]\n\n**Answer the following question.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do weddings represent? [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{traditions and rituals}$\n\n- “...every culture has traditions and rituals which connect couples to their heritage, their families and each other.”  Weddings are important parts of every culture and serve as times of connection for people who may see each other only during occasional but important ceremonies. [1 mark]\n\n$2$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{honoring elders}$\n\n- “In China, a tea ceremony honors the couple’s elders.” The text gives three examples of important elements of wedding traditions, beginning with this example from China. [1 mark]\n\n$3$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{exchanging rings}$\n\n- “Canadian weddings often feature couples exchanging rings, representing eternity…” The symbolism within weddings highlights the importance of physical elements (rings), as well as their culturally significant meaning (together for eternity). [1 mark]\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{guests throw rice}$\n\n- “in Greece, guests throw rice to symbolize prosperity.” In a similar way to question 3, the physical and symbolic elements of wedding traditions are explained. [1 mark]\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{love, unity and new beginnings}$\n\n- “... ceremonies that symbolize love, unity and new beginnings.” Weddings are highly important ceremonies which signify the end of one chapter of life  - as unmarried people - and the beginning of a new chapter lived as a couple.  [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0384","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0384\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a description of a fishing village tradition.**\n\n:audio{#56956855-8cee-4123-86fe-4b7913197f78 aid=\"56956855-8cee-4123-86fe-4b7913197f78\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.** [6 marks]\n\n**Travel Article**\n\nPorto Vela is a small fishing community with a **[-1-]** tradition that young people have followed for many generations. At the age of **[-2-]**, each teenager must take the family vessel out onto the water alone for the first time.\n\nThis year, Marco carried on the tradition. Although he had accompanied his father on fishing trips from **[-3-]**, this was Marco's first voyage as **[-4-]**. He set out from the harbour at **[-5-]**, watched by his proud and anxious family.\n\nMarco came back safely after **[-6-]**, and the entire community welcomed him home, celebrating his successful transition into adulthood.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{coming-of-age}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$there is a coming-of-age tradition that has been passed down for generations\"\\\n\nThe text identifies the tradition specifically as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$coming-of-age” ritual, meaning it marks the transition from childhood to adulthood.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{thirteen}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When a teenager turns thirteen\"\\\n\nThe tradition begins at a specific age in this community - thirteen - which marks the beginning of adulthood.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{very young}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He had been fishing with his father since he was very young\"\\\n\nMarco was not new to fishing - he had years of experience working on his father's boat. This makes his first solo trip a meaningful step forward, not a first experience of the sea.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{captain}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$this was his first trip as the captain\"\\\n\nDespite his experience, Marco had always been a passenger and learner. Taking the role of captain for the first time placed all responsibility on him alone.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{five in the morning}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He left at five in the morning\"\\\n\nThe early departure time reflects the reality of fishing, but also the significance of the moment. His family was watching from the dock as he left before sunrise.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{four hours}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Four hours later, Marco returned with a small catch\"\\\n\nThe length of the trip is important - four hours alone at sea, responsible for the boat and his own safety, shows his ability to enter the community of adults in the village."},{"ref":"EB0385","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0385\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a farmer talking about their sheepdog's first solo trial.**\n\n:audio{#0554861d-dbe2-4b69-b711-c47f2b3dea08 aid=\"0554861d-dbe2-4b69-b711-c47f2b3dea08\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What breed of dog is Moss?    [1 mark] \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What must a herding dog do before it can work with sheep?    [1 mark] \n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the final task in the trial?    [1 mark] \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Moving the sheep through a series of gates.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Gathering the sheep from the far end of the field.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Separating two sheep from the rest of the group.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How long does it usually take most dogs to reach trial level?    [1 mark] \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Two years\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Several years\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Eight weeks\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is most impressive about Moss's performance?     [1 mark] \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$He completed the course without making any mistakes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$He corrected his only mistake without being told to.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$He completed the course in under five minutes.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(Border) Collie}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$He's a two-year-old Border Collie”\\\n\nThe text clearly identifies Moss's breed. Border Collies are well known as working sheepdogs.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{pass a trial}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Before a herding dog can be trusted to work with sheep, he has to pass a trial”\\\n\nThe trial is a formal assessment, described in the text as a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$dog exam”, that tests that the dog has the knowledge needed to safely work with sheep.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$separate two from the group at the end”\\\n\nSeparating two sheep is the final task in the trial. It comes after gathering and moving the flock through the gates.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **A**: Moving through the gates is the second task in the trial, not the final one.\n\n- **B**: Gathering the sheep from the far end of the field is the first task, not the last.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It takes most dogs years to compete at this level”\\\n\nThe text uses the word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$years” - plural - indicating it usually takes a long time.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: Two years is Moss's age, not the typical training period for most dogs.\n\n- **C**: Eight weeks is the age at which Moss began his training, not the time it takes to reach trial level.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$he corrected himself without any command from me”\\\n\nThe most impressive detail is not the time or the mistake itself, but that Moss recognised and fixed his own error independently, showing true readiness to work alone.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The text states Moss made one mistake, so he did not complete the course without errors.\n\n- **C**: While completing the course in four minutes twelve seconds is fast, the text does not present the time as the most significant achievement."},{"ref":"EB0432","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0432\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nHey Sis,\n\nI know I promised to call, but it has been super busy and stressful here. Jordan signed the papers yesterday. She is officially the owner of the bee farm! Hives, equipment, supplier contracts, and all. Forty-three years after Dad started it, Jordan is taking over Honey Hill Farms.\n\nI watched her do hive inspections on Saturday morning. She was calm and methodical, exactly the way Dad taught me, and exactly the way I taught her. She didn't need me standing next to her, and we both knew it. I felt so proud. I also felt completely unnecessary, which I wasn't expecting.\n\nYou know better than anyone how much she struggled in her first year of college. Beekeeping gave her the structure she needed. Weekends at home, gradually taking on the responsibility that came with the family business. I think the bees saved her from giving up on college, in a way.\n\nShe's ready. I know she's ready. The question I'm asking now is: am I ready to let go? I spent most of my life running this business and then teaching it to her. I wonder what comes next for me?\n\nI'll figure it out. Probably.\n\nI'll call you Sunday.\n\nDiane x\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Jordan has recently become the legal owner of the farm.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Diane taught Jordan the same techniques she had learned from their father.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Diane expected to feel unnecessary when Jordan took over.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Jordan found beekeeping helpful during a difficult period at college.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Diane is confident about what she will do next.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: Jordan signed the papers yesterday. She is officially the owner of the bee farm.\\\n\n\\\n\nThe word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$yesterday\" confirms this happened very recently, and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$officially\" confirms the transfer of ownership is now legal and complete.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: exactly the way Dad taught me, and exactly the way I taught her\\\n\nDiane passed on to Jordan the same knowledge and techniques she had received from their father, which emphasizes the fact that the business has been in the family for generations.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: I also felt completely unnecessary, which I wasn't expecting.\\\n\nDiane was surprised by this feeling. The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$which I wasn't expecting\" makes clear that she had not anticipated feeling unnecessary when the moment finally arrived.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: Beekeeping gave her the structure she needed\\\n\nDuring her first difficult year at college, Jordan struggled. The routine and responsibility of beekeeping provided exactly the stability she needed to continue.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: I'll figure it out. Probably.\\\n\nThe word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$probably\" shows that Diane is NOT confident about her future.  She does not know what comes next and is honest about her uncertainty."},{"ref":"EB0433","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0433\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Walk a Mile in My Shoes**\n\n**[-1-]**\n\nSelin and I had been talking about doing a long-distance hike for years. Last spring, we finally committed to joining a group in Italy: eight strangers, 12 days, 300 kilometers. I signed up without hesitation. I train regularly, I eat well, and at 54, I still think of myself as someone who can do these kinds of things.\n\n**[-2-]**\n\nBy day three, my left knee had opinions I hadn't asked for. By day five, I was taking the low road while the others scampered up hills. On day seven, it rained heavily and I stood at a fork in the trail watching the group move away, their voices echoing down the valley. Selin was with them, her red jacket fading into the mist. I turned around and started limping down to the next village.\n\n**[-3-]**\n\nSelin completed every kilometer. I completed most of them. I'm not sure what I expected to feel at the end of the hike… but it wasn't this. It isn't failure exactly. But clearly, I am no longer who I thought I was.  That's not a bad thing, I guess it is just a new road to walk down.\n\n**Choose an appropriate question from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Was it worth it?\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Now what?\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$What could go wrong?\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$What was I thinking?\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Where was Selin?\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$How hard can it be?\n\n**Find a word in the text which means the following.** [3 marks]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$agreed    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$dashed    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$disappearing    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I signed up without hesitation. I train regularly, I eat well, and at 54, I still think of myself as someone who can do these kinds of things.\"\\\n\nThis section opens with confidence and enthusiasm: The writer has no doubts at this point, making $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$What could go wrong?\" the perfect humorous header for a tale that does not, actually, go very well.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option F** is not correct. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$How hard can it be?\" expresses a similar overconfidence, but it focuses specifically on physical difficulty. The paragraph is not only about the challenge ahead; it is about the writer's overall self-image. \n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$By day three, my left knee had opinions I hadn't asked for...\"\\\n\nThis section describes the physical and emotional reality of the hike, which was a sharp contrast with the confidence of the opening. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$What was I thinking?\" reflects the writer's self-doubt as problems begin to arise.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option E** is the distractor. Selin is mentioned in this section, but she is a detail within a larger picture of struggle and isolation. This section is about the writer's experience, not Selin's.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'm not sure what I expected to feel at the end of the hike... but it wasn't this.\"\\\n\nThis section is reflective, and there is no clear ending. The writer has discovered that they are not quite the person they used to be and must now transition into a new, unknown phase of life, making $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Now what?\" the only heading that captures that open, uncertain mood.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** does not fit well here. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Was it worth it?\" implies a conclusion or an ending has been reached. The writer managed to finish most of the hike, but this section deals with their internal struggles, not the hike itself.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{committed}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Last spring we finally committed to joining a group in Italy\"\\\n\nCommitted means to firmly agree or dedicate yourself to something. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{scampered}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I was taking the low road while the others scampered up hills\"\\\n\nScampered describes moving quickly and lightly. Dashed is a synonym that also captures the ease and speed with which the other hikers climbed.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fading}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$her red jacket fading into the mist\"\\\n\nFading describes something gradually disappearing from view. Here, it captures the moment Selin and the group move beyond the writer's reach."},{"ref":"EB0434","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0434\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n*Thursday*\n\nDanny's getting married. The invitation arrived this morning. Spring wedding, plus one. I put it on the counter and went to check on the dogs.\n\nThere are eleven greyhounds on the farm now. They come from the track when their racing days are over, when someone decides they're too slow, too old. Sometimes they arrive injured. Most have never lived outside a cage. There's one I've named Ghost. It arrived so withdrawn that I wasn't sure he'd find his way back. He's fine now. More than fine.\n\nMy life doesn't look like much from the outside, I know. No wife, no children, no particular achievement or success to show. But I love this farm and the work I do on $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$, and being able to give hope and comfort to these throw-away dogs gives me purpose.\n\nI go back to the kitchen and respond to the invitation: just one person attending. Danny will be happy in his marriage, I hope so, anyway. I have 11 dogs who need feeding and $\\underline{\\textrm{who}}$ need me.  And $\\underline{\\textrm{that's}}$ enough for a Thursday.\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].** [4]\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$An invitation to an event arrived recently.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The dogs on the farm were previously used for sport.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Dogs always arrive at the farm ready to be rehomed.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Ghost found it easy to settle into his new life.    [1 mark]\n\n**What do the following words refer to in the text?** [2]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... the work I do on $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$...”    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... $\\underline{\\textrm{who}}$ need feeding...”    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: The invitation arrived this morning\\\n\nThe writer received the wedding invitation on the same day they were writing, confirming it arrived very recently.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: They come from the track when their racing days are over\\\n\nThe greyhounds on the farm are all former racing dogs who have been retired from competition.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: Sometimes they arrive injured\\\n\nNot all dogs arrive healthy: the text makes clear that some animals end their racing lives due to injuries.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: arrived so withdrawn I wasn't sure he'd find his way back\\\n\nGhost's adjustment was slow and uncertain, the opposite of easy. Withdrawn means unable or unwilling to communicate or interact with others; it took the writer a long time to earn Ghost's trust so that he could live like a normal dog.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the farm}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It\" refers directly to the farm, which the writer mentions in the same sentence.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the eleven dogs}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Who\" refers to the eleven greyhounds the writer cares for."}]}]},{"name":"Identities","subtopics":[{"name":"Beliefs & Values","questions":[{"ref":"EB0007","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0007\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nOctober 24 \n\nToday was really tough. I had my math test, and I'd studied for days, but when I got to class, my mind went blank. The pressure to get a good grade started to creep in, and I felt panicked. Right then, I saw Paige next to me, with her paper at just the right angle—I could easily see her answers if I leaned just a bit. \n\nFor a split second, it felt like my only option. Everyone always says how important grades are for our future, and I didn't want to mess up. But then I remembered what my parents and teachers always say about honesty and integrity. Would copying actually help me learn anything? And could I even feel proud if I did well that way? \n\nSo I decided to stick with what I knew, even if it meant my grade wasn't perfect. It wasn't easy, and I felt disappointed leaving some answers blank. But when I handed in my paper, I felt surprisingly relieved. I guess I proved something to myself today. I want my grades to reflect my effort—not someone else's. Still nervous to see the result, but at least I can be proud of how I handled it.\n\n1 - 4: **Choose the 4 true statements.**   [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer had prepared for the math test by studying for several days.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$During the test, the writer copied answers from a classmate due to the pressure of getting a good grade.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer's classmate, Paige, had her paper angled in a way that made it possible for the writer to see her answers.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer reflected on advice about honesty and integrity from both parents and teachers before deciding not to cheat.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer felt only disappointment and regret after deciding not to copy answers from Paige.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$In the end, the writer felt relieved after handing in the test, even though some questions were left blank.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer hopes to receive a perfect grade despite not feeling entirely confident about the answers.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$TThe writer is proud of their result.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What seems to be the main purpose of this diary entry? [1 mark]\n\n - A.$\\hspace{1em}$To explore the impact of academic pressure on students’ mental health\n\n - B.$\\hspace{1em}$To describe a difficult test experience and the writer's study habits\n\n - C.$\\hspace{1em}$To reflect on a personal choice about honesty and integrity\n\n - D.$\\hspace{1em}$To express frustration with the importance placed on grades in school","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers 1 to 4 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, D, F}$ [4 marks]\n\n- **A:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I had my math test, and I'd studied for days...\", so a few days/ several days.\n\n- **C:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I saw Paige next to me, with her paper at just the right angle—I could easily see her answers if I leaned just a bit.\" The angle enabled the narrator to see the answers.\n\n- **D:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But then I remembered what my parents and teachers always say about honesty and integrity.\"\n\n- **F:** $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It wasn't easy, and I felt disappointed leaving some answers blank. But when I handed in my paper, I felt surprisingly relieved.\"\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, E, G, H\n\n- **B**: The writer did not copy answers from Paige. While the writer did consider it briefly, they ultimately decided against cheating to stay true to their values: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$So I decided to stick with what I knew, even if it meant my grade wasn't perfect.\"\n\n- **E**: The writer felt both disappointment and relief, not just disappointment. After submitting the paper, the writer reflects: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But when I handed in my paper, I felt surprisingly relieved.\"\n\n- **G**: The writer does not expect a perfect grade. Instead, they acknowledge leaving answers blank, which made them $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$nervous to see the result,\" and focus more on pride in their integrity than on expecting a top score.\n\n- **H**: The writer does not express being proud of their work. They mention being proud of the way they handled to whole situation. “I can be proud of how I handled it.\"\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Each option touches on elements related to the text (eg. **Option A** mentions the impact of academic pressure on mental health, which is not the main topic or purpose of the text; **Option B** is wrong, as the text does not focus on the authors study habits; **Option D** concentrates on frustration, which again isn't the main point of the text), but the primary purpose remains the writer's internal reflection on maintaining personal integrity despite external pressures."},{"ref":"EB0008","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0008\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{MEANINGFUL WAYS TO CELEBRATE YOUR PATRIOTISM}}$\n\nThe 4th of July is a day to wave the American flag and celebrate our nation's independence, which was achieved over 200 years ago in 1776. Most people like to celebrate this holiday with backyard BBQs amongst friends and family. If you're looking for a few other ideas to show your patriotism, though, then you've come to the right place. \n\n- **[-1-]** \n\n- Invite friends, family, and neighbors to come together for a 4th of July celebration with plenty of food, drinks, and patriotic red, white, and blue decorations. To elevate the party's patriotic feel, include celebratory items like themed tableware, streamers, banners, flags, and several fresh floral arrangements in red, white, and blue to create a vibrant atmosphere.\n\n- **[-2-]**\n\n- Cities nationwide often host dazzling Independence Day parades honoring veterans and active service members. Bring your family to enjoy the display of patriotic floats and people, join in a lively and inspiring experience.\n\n- **[-3-]**\n\n- Offer your time or assistance to veterans in your area, or show appreciation with a patriotic flower arrangement or gift. If you don't know any veterans personally, reach out to a VA facility to learn ways to support veterans and their families.\n\n- **[-4-]**\n\n- Display the American flag as a symbol of patriotism. Raise it with care, ensuring it flies above other flags, never touches the ground, and is illuminated if displayed at night.\n\n- **[-5-]**\n\n- America's National Parks showcase some of the country's most incredible landscapes. Historic sites and monuments also offer rich experiences and a sense of pride. Many operate with limited funds, so consider visiting, joining a support group, or donating to help preserve them.\n\n- **[-6-]**\n\n- Strengthen your community by choosing locally-owned shops and American-made products when possible. Putting money back into the local economy is a meaningful way to show patriotism.\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [6 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Wave the Stars and Stripes Properly\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Promote Community Gatherings\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Support Those Who Fought for You\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Shop to Help Your Homies\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Take to the Streets: A Show of National Pride\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Be a Proud Citizen and Show it\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Host a Festive Gathering\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Volunteer at a Veteran Shelter\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1em}$Appreciate the Country's Natural Beauty\n\n- J.$\\hspace{1em}$Celebrate Historical Events by Attending Parades\n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraphs 1-2 which means the following:** [2 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$improve\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$impressive","markScheme":"1 - 6.$\\hspace{1em}$ [6 marks]\n\n- **[-1-]** $\\textrm{G}$\n\n- The paragraph suggests organizing gatherings to foster community and celebrate patriotism.\n\n- **[-2-]** $\\textrm{E}$\n\n- The paragraph highlights participation in community events that celebrate national pride\n\n- **[-3-]** $\\textrm{C}$\n\n- This paragraph emphasizes the importance of recognizing and helping veterans.\n\n- **[-4-]** $\\textrm{A}$\n\n- The paragarph discusses the proper display of the flag as a patriotic act.\n\n- **[-5-]** $\\textrm{I}$\n\n-The text recommends exploring national parks as a way to appreciate the country’s nature.\n\n- **[-6-]** $\\textrm{D}$\n\n- The paragraph encourages purchasing local products to strengthen the economy.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, F, H, J\n\n- **B**. none of the paragraphs talks about promoting Independence Day parties and parades, just organizing or attending them.\n\n- **F**. It is too general to be a good subheading for any of the paragraphs, actually, it could serve as the title or it could be used for each and any of the paragraphs.\n\n- **H**. the paragraph about veterans mentions a VA facility only as one of the ways of supporting the veterans, it is not the main topic of this. fragment\n\n- **J**. the second paragraph does talk about attending parades, but not necessarily ones meant to celebrate historical events other than the Independence Day.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{elevate }$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Improve* is a good replacement word for *elevate* as they both mean to enhance, to make better.\n\n$8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{dazzling }$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Impressive* is a synonym of *dazzling*"},{"ref":"EB0009","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0009\n\n**Maximum mark: 10**\n\nDear Editor,\n\nI am deeply troubled by the behavior of many young people today, who seem to have little regard for the rules and values that keep our society functioning. I see it everywhere, on the streets, in schools, even in public spaces where simple courtesies are ignored. Young people nowadays seem to think they're above the law, whether it's speeding, ignoring traffic rules, or vandalizing property. It's as if rules have become suggestions rather than expectations.\n\nWhat happened to the days when respecting authority and abiding by the law were instilled as basic values? We've gone from raising responsible citizens to nurturing a generation that tests boundaries without fear of consequences. And let's be clear: without respect for the law, chaos isn't far behind.\n\nParents, educators, and society as a whole need to **[-5-]** young people accountable. This lack of respect doesn’t just **[-6-]** poorly on today’s youth; it undermines the very fabric of our community. We can’t let our values erode at the hands of a generation that refuses to take responsibility. It’s time to **[-7-]** them that rules exist for a reason.\n\nSincerely,\\\n\nA Concerned Citizen\n\n**Choose an appropriate ending from the list that completes each sentence.** [4 marks]\n\n1.$  \\hspace{1em}$ Young people today… \\\n\n2.$  \\hspace{1em}$ Politeness and good manners…\\\n\n3.$  \\hspace{1em}$ The writer wonders why people…\\\n\n4.$  \\hspace{1em}$ Without respect for the law, society…\n\n- A.$  \\hspace{1em}$ are seen only as suggestions. \n\n- B.$  \\hspace{1em}$ are not paid attention to.\n\n- C.$  \\hspace{1em}$ will be in shambles.\n\n- D.$  \\hspace{1em}$ are not afraid of punishment. \n\n- E.$  \\hspace{1em}$ cannot survive for very long. \n\n- F.$  \\hspace{1em}$ do not respect shared values.\n\n- G.$  \\hspace{1em}$ have no respect for authority.\n\n- H.$  \\hspace{1em}$ lost respect for traditional values.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.** [3 marks]\n\nParents, educators, and society as a whole need to **[-5-]** young people accountable. This lack of respect doesn’t just **[-6-]** poorly on today’s youth; it undermines the very fabric of our community. We can’t let our values erode at the hands of a generation that refuses to take responsibility. It’s time to **[-7-]** them that rules exist for a reason.\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$|A. hold|\n\n||B. reflect|\n\n||C. remind|\n\n||D. warn|\n\n||E. affect|\n\n||F. keep|","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “have little regard for the rules and values that keep our society functioning\" \n\n- The text mentions young people today do not respect the shared values that hold society together. \n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. The text mentions young people testing boundaries “without fear of consequences,\" but this sentence is about respect for values, not about fear of punishment.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “public spaces where simple courtesies are ignored\" \n\n- To *ignore* something means not to pay attention to it; politeness and good manners are being overlooked. \n\n- **Option A** is incorrect. The writer says rules have “become suggestions,\" not politeness and good manners; this option matches a different idea in the text.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “What happened to the days when respecting authority and abiding by the law were instilled as basic values?\" \n\n- The writer questions why people no longer respect authority, as they once did. \n\n- **Option H** is incorrect. The text refers specifically to respect for authority and the law, not to “traditional values\" in general.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Chaos* means complete disorder, which is what “in shambles\" describes. \n\n- **Option E** is incorrect. The text warns of chaos, not that society will disappear or fail to survive.s, making it less suitable in this context.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Hold accountable\" is a fixed expression meaning to make someone take responsibility for their actions.\n\n- **Option F** is incorrect. Keep does not collocate with “accountable\"; the standard expression is “hold someone accountable,\" not “keep someone accountable.\"\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Reflect poorly on\" is a fixed expression meaning to give a bad impression of someone or make them look bad. \n\n- **Option E** is incorrect. *Affect* means to influence or have an effect on something, but it does not collocate with the adverb \"poorly\" in this structure; we say \"reflect poorly on,\" not \"affect poorly on.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Remind* means to make someone remember something they already know or have forgotten, which matches the writer's wish to “bring rules back.”\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect. *Warn* means to tell someone about a possible danger or bad consequence, which is too strong here; the writer wants young people to be *reminded* of why rules exist, not threatened with what will happen if they break them."},{"ref":"EB0143","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0143\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a person reflecting on time spent with their grandmother.**\n\n:audio{#36e42810-f6ba-4026-a9bb-3c68ab93926a aid=\"36e42810-f6ba-4026-a9bb-3c68ab93926a\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker identifies several activities done during the day by their grandmother.  Give **two** answers.     [2 marks]\n\n2 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker’s parents worked from home every day.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker was regularly with their grandmother when he was younger.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker often felt bored when staying with their grandmother.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker learned about their family from their grandmother’s stories.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The grandmother taught the speaker important life values.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The grandmother enjoyed reading and did not like other activities.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker feel about their grandmother’s influence?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$grateful\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$angry\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$regretful","markScheme":"$1$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order. Any two of the following:*\n\n-   $\\textrm{cooking (an evening meal)}$\n-   $\\textrm{cleaning}$\n-   $\\textrm{helping a neighbor}$\n-   $\\textrm{working in the garden}$\n-   $\\textrm{reading}$\n\n- All options come from the following sentence: “She was tireless and would joyfully spend all day doing something: cooking a delicious evening meal for the family, cleaning, helping a neighbor, working in the garden, and, when she had time, she would sit in her favorite chair and read.” [2 marks]\n\n2 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, D, E}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **B**. “When I was young, I spent a lot of time with my grandmother”\n\n- The speaker mentions spending a lot of time with their  grandmother. The phrases *to spend a lot of time* and *to be regularly with someone* convey the same idea.\n\n- **D**. “I learned about my relatives and the troubles and triumphs of their lives.”\n\n- *Relatives* is a synonym for family.  *Troubles and triumphs* is another way of saying that the stories included the problems, *troubles*, that their relatives experienced, and the way they overcame them, *triumphs*.\n\n- **E**. The speaker lists the personal values learned from their grandmother: “perseverance and kindness…the importance of family, and keeping a sense of humor…”.  The speaker recognizes how important the time spent with their grandmother was in shaping the values the adult speaker has.  *Perseverance* is the quality of not giving up when things become difficult.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: A, C, F\n\n- **A**.  The text states that the speaker’s parents “were at work.”  The text does not say where the parents were working, but it implies that the speaker and grandmother were the only ones at home during the day.\n\n- **C**. The text shows us that the young speaker enjoyed the time spent with their grandmother: “As she worked and I followed her around, copying what she did…”  The speaker was as active as their grandmother, and was engaged in different activities during the day. There is no indication that the speaker remembers being bored.\n\n- **F**. Although the grandmother did enjoy reading “when she had time, she would sit in her favorite chair and read”, the text clearly shows that she also did many other activities “joyfully”.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker says “. I am who I am… thanks to grandmother’s stories”, showing that they appreciated what they learned from her.  *Grateful* means being thankful for something done for you.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **Option B**: There is no sign that the speaker was angry about spending time with their grandmother. The speaker uses positive images to describe the time they spent together: “joyfully,” “told me stories,” and “thanks to grandmother’s stories.”\n\n- **Option C**: *Regret* means feeling sad or disappointed about something.  The speaker does not show regret for spending time with their grandmother and learning about values.  Instead, the speaker says they are grateful: “thanks to grandmother’s stories…”"},{"ref":"EB0144","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0144\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a person explaining why they chose to become a vegetarian.**\n\n:audio{#19324caf-8bc9-404d-abae-c2e85bb43c47 aid=\"19324caf-8bc9-404d-abae-c2e85bb43c47\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What event at school led the speaker to first question their diet?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What **three** resources does the speaker mention that meat production requires?    [3 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the speaker, what kind of choices have meaning?    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What made becoming a vegetarian difficult at first?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker didn’t like vegetarian food\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Their family wasn’t vegetarian\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They didn’t believe it would make a difference\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker define making a difference?   [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Changing the entire world\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Stopping environmental harm alone\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Making a small positive change","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Reading about meat production}$  [1 mark]\n\n- “Then, in school one day we read about how meat production harms the environment.”  The speaker had not considered how eating meat impacted the planet before this, and the knowledge made them reconsider what they chose to eat.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{water, land, and energy (in any order)}$ [1 mark]\n\n- “I was shocked by how much water, land, and energy it uses.” The quantity of resources needed to produce meat surprised (“shocked”) the speaker.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{individual}$ [1 mark]\n\n- “I believe individual choices matter…”\n- *Matter* and *have meaning* are synonyms which show that something is of importance or has value.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “My family wasn’t vegetarian...”  The speaker had to overcome these challenges when they started a vegetarian diet.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: the speaker tells us “...there were foods I really missed”, indicating that they missed eating meat.  However the text does not tell us that the speaker did not like vegetarian food.\n\n- **Option C**: “Even if I can’t change the world, I can change my actions.” The speaker did believe their choice could make a difference.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I can change my actions and make a small positive contribution to the health of our planet.”  The speaker feels that it is possible for one person’s choice to make a difference in making the planet a little healthier.  *Contribute* means to give something to help achieve a goal or objective.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**:  The speaker knows that one person cannot change or influence the whole world: “…even if I can’t save the world alone, I can change my actions.” However, the speaker believes that “every small choice matters.”\n\n- **Option B**: The speaker believes that they can “...make a small positive contribution to the health of our planet.”  The speaker believes that “...everyone plays a role in protecting the Earth” and they want to do their part to help."},{"ref":"EB0145","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0145\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a person reflecting on overcoming self-doubt.**\n\n:audio{#9765f2b9-9e44-4095-9ed7-f99bf54d6640 aid=\"9765f2b9-9e44-4095-9ed7-f99bf54d6640\"}\n\n1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the :u[three] true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker never struggled with self-belief.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Perseverance was important in helping the speaker build confidence.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker now sees failure as part of learning.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker immediately gained confidence after one success.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker believes only perfection leads to confidence.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Lack of confidence was the biggest problem the speaker faced.\n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.** [3 marks]\n\nThe speaker used to question whether they were **[-4-]** to handle problems.\\\nThe speaker **[-5-]** new things because they were scared of failing.\\\nSelf-belief is not about perfection but about **[-6-]** that growth is possible.","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, C, F}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **B**. The speaker tells us: “setting goals and sticking to them…made me stronger.”  *Perseverance* is the quality of never giving up and, in this case,  it it has a similar meaning to *sticking to* something.\n\n- **C**. The speaker believes that “and even failure is just a step forward.” A *step forward* is another way of saying something can be learned if a mistake or failure happens.\n\n- **F**. “self-doubt had been my biggest obstacle.”  *Obstacle* is a synonym for *problem* or *challenge*, something which must be overcome or solved.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: A, D, E\n\n- **A**.  The text begins with the statement: “For years, I doubted myself.”  This tells us that the speaker had struggled with self-belief.\n\n- **D**. The text indicates that the process of becoming more confident was not immediate. “Each small success made me stronger” and “over time I realized…” show that it took a series of successes to help build the speaker’s self-belief\n\n- **E**. “I didn’t need to be perfect…”  The text tells us that perfection is not necessary to have confidence, and making mistakes is part of the process of learning.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{good enough}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “When I faced a challenge, I wondered if I was good enough to overcome it.”  *Wondered* and *questioned* are closely related in meaning and show that the speaker was worried about their ability to handle the problem.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{hesitated to try}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I hesitated to try new things, fearing I would fail.” *Hesitate* means to pause before doing something, usually because of fear or nervousness.  The speaker lacked self-confidence and was worried about failing if they tried something new.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{trusting}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker came to understand that “...I didn’t need to be perfect; it’s about trusting that I can learn and grow.”  Overcoming the fear of failing at something gave the speaker confidence and allowed them to try new things, even if they weren’t always successful (“...even failure teaches something important”)."},{"ref":"EB0339","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0339\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Instant opinions - not always for me!**\n\nHey guys - I want to talk about something that's been bothering me lately. It feels like having no opinion is almost worse than having the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$wrong” one. Teachers always say they want discussion, and I get it. But if I admit in class that I'm not sure how I feel - or what I think about the subject - someone laughs and goes, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Classic you. Always thinking.”\n\nLast week, we discussed banning phones in school. Everyone had an instant opinion, like it was obvious. I didn't. Phones are distracting, yes, but they're an important part of our life. So when people act like it's a simple problem with a simple solution, I just don't buy it. When I said, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I need more time to think,” my teacher smiled, but I could tell some classmates were annoyed.\n\nOnline it's even worse. Chats and posts move fast, and people expect immediate reactions to every headline or social issue. If I don't reply quickly, someone will message, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$So you're not saying anything?” as if silence automatically means I don't care. But I do care. I just don't want to fake certainty about something. Am I the only one experiencing this?\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the writer mainly concerned about?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Teachers never allow students to speak in class.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$People expect instant opinions and judge uncertainty.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Phones make it impossible to have real discussions.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Students do not care about social issues anymore.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the classmate's comment $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Classic you. Always thinking.” suggest?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The classmate respects the writer's careful thinking.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The classmate is surprised the writer has an opinion.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The classmate is making fun of the writer for not choosing quickly.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The classmate is confused about the topic.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the writer mention the phone-ban discussion?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To show that teachers ignore students' opinions.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To give an example of an issue too complex for a quick answer.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To argue that phones should never be banned.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$To prove that classmates are addicted to phones.\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words that appear in the text. Both parts are required for 1 mark.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer thinks banning phones in school is an easy decision.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer also feels pressure in online spaces.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer believes silence online means a person does not care.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$having no opinion is almost worse than having the $`$wrong' one” \\\n\nThe writer explains that people expect immediate responses both in class and online. This shows the main concern is pressure to have instant opinions and being judged for uncertainty.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C, D\n\n- **A**: The writer does not say teachers stop students from speaking. In fact, the writer says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Teachers always say they want discussion.”\n\n- **C**: Phones are only used as an example in one discussion, not the main concern of the text.\n\n- **D**: The writer explicitly says they care: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But I do care.” The issue is not a lack of interest in social issues.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The comment is disrespectful and impatient, showing that the classmate is making fun of the writer. The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$classic you” indicates this is the typical response of the writer. \n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D\n\n- **A**: The mocking response, indicating the attitude of the writer is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$classic” or expected,  does not show respect.\n\n- **B**: The writer does not share a clear opinion at that moment - they say they are unsure and need more time.\n\n- **D**: The classmate is not confused about the topic; they react to the writer's hesitation.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Everyone had an instant opinion... I didn't”\\\n\nThe writer explains that the issue is not simple: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I just don't buy it.” This shows the phone-ban discussion is used as an example of a topic that needs time and thought.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **A**: The teacher does not ignore the writer. The writer says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$my teacher smiled.”\n\n- **C**: The writer does not argue phones should never be banned; they say phones are distracting but important.\n\n- **D**: The writer does not claim classmates are addicted to phones. Addiction is never mentioned.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$when people act like it's a simple problem with a simple solution, I just don't buy it.”\\\n\nThis shows the writer believes the issue is complex. The phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I just don't buy it” means the writer is not convinced that this issue has a simple solution.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Online it's even worse.”\\\n\nThe writer mentions that online spaces are more difficult than classes. They then continue to explain how online discussions are fast and expect $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$immediate” reactions.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But I do care”\n\nThe writer rejects the idea that silence means a lack of care. Instead, they explain how silence means careful consideration of the topic."},{"ref":"EB0340","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0340\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**The after-game group chat**\n\n*Coach*: Good win today. I'm proud of the team's effort, but I'm going to say it again: we can't play politely and expect results. When the other team starts to lose focus, you press harder. That's not being cruel - that is competing. \n\n*Maya*: I agree with the pressure part, but some of the celebrating was embarrassing. When their striker missed that easy shot, half our bench laughed. I know it wasn't meant to be personal, but it was mean. If someone did that to us, we'd be furious. \n\n*Aiden*: That's bothered me, too, Coach. I want to win, and I'll work for it. But I don't want us to become the team that enjoys someone else failing. There's a difference between taking advantage of a mistake and cheering because a person made it. I didn't say anything because I didn't want to sound like I was judging everyone, but it's been stuck in my head since the game. \n\n*Coach*: I didn't mean to make any individual player feel bad about a mistake - mistakes are part of the game, too. But I hear what you're saying, and I will try to encourage the team in better ways from now on.\n\n**Complete the sentences using words or phrases from the text. Use no more than three words for each gap.**[4 marks]\n\nWhen the opposing team begins to lose focus, the players are expected to **[-1-]**. \n\nDuring the match, half the bench **[-2-]** when the striker missed an easy chance. \n\nThere is a clear difference between using an opponent's mistake to your advantage and **[-3-]** because of it. \n\n**[-4-]**, the concern about the team's reaction remained in one player's mind after the game. \n\n**Choose the correct answer.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the coach decide at the end of the conversation?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$To defend the team's behavior and ignore criticism\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$To blame one player for overreacting\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$To adjust how he motivates the team\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$To reduce the team's competitiveness","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{press harder}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When the other team starts to lose focus, you press harder.”\\\n\nThe coach clearly states that the response is to increase pressure.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{laughed}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Half our bench laughed.”\\\n\nThe sentence refers to the team's reaction when the player missed the shot. The text specifies laughter as the reaction.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{cheering}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$There's a difference between taking advantage of a mistake and cheering because a person made it.”\\\n\nThe question contrasts using a mistake strategically with celebrating the person's failure. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Cheering” reflects the behavior Aiden criticises.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{since the game}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... it's been stuck in my head since the game.”\n\nThe sentence focuses on the fact that the concern continued after the match. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I will try to encourage the team in better ways from now on.”\\\n\nThe coach acknowledges the concern and states that he will change how he encourages the team.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B, D\n\n- **A**: The coach does not defend the behavior; he says he hears the concern and will try to change.\n\n- **B**: He does not blame anyone in his final message.\n\n- **C**: He does not suggest reducing competitiveness, only changing the way he encourages the team."},{"ref":"EB0341","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0341\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Disagreement does not equal disrespect**\n\n**[-1-]**\\\n\nIn many families, disagreements between generations are common, especially when it comes to social issues. When she was young, Leila used to avoid these conversations with her grandparents because she believed conflict could damage their relationship. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I didn't want to say the wrong thing,” she explains. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I thought disagreement automatically meant disrespect.”\n\n**[-2-]**\\\n\nHowever, as she grew older, Leila learned that in her home, respect was not defined by silence or automatically agreeing with an adult's idea. Instead, her parents had gently insisted on one rule: you can disagree with someone, but you must speak and listen with care. Her grandparents followed the same principle. Even when discussions became tense, they listened closely and responded kindly and without sarcasm. They challenged her ideas, but they also took her seriously. Leila learned to listen to their points of view and, even if she didn't change her position on the issue, she learned why her grandparents held their ideas.\n\n**[-3-]**\\\n\nLeila has learned that respect is not the same as agreement. The lesson her parents taught her - to respectfully discuss even difficult issues - now shapes the way she handles any type of disagreement inside or outside her home.\n\n**Choose an appropriate heading from the list that matches each paragraph.**[3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Respect as a family value\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The first step: avoiding conflict at all costs\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Fear of saying the wrong thing\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$A rule that guided three generations\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$When agreement becomes the only goal\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Respect beyond home\n\n**Find the word or phrase in the text which means the following:** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$harm    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$irony    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$affects    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- The text tells us that Leila avoided conversations due to fear of conflict damaging the relationship.\n\n- **Option C** is incorrect here. Although Leila says, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I didn't want to say the wrong thing,” the paragraph is not mainly about fear of speaking. It is about avoiding disagreement altogether because she thought it meant disrespect.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$    [1 mark]\n\n- This is the correct option because this paragraph introduces the family rule and shows that the whole family follows it.\n\n- **Option A** does not fit here. Respect is clearly important in this paragraph, but the focus is not on respect as a general family value. Instead, it is specifically about the rule that guides how they disagree.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$    [1 mark]\n\n- This paragraph explains how the lesson shapes Leila's approach to disagreements, no matter where they happen.\n\n- **Option E**  contradicts the main idea of the paragraph, which clearly states that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$respect is not the same as agreement,”\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{damage}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$conflict could damage their relationship.” \\\n\n*Damage* and *harm* are synonyms.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{sarcasm}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$responded kindly and without sarcasm.” \\\n\nBoth *sarcasm* and *irony* refer to saying something not meant literally. They could also imply mockery.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{shapes}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$now shapes the way she handles...”   \\\n\n*Affects* and *shapes* are very close synonyms in this context."},{"ref":"EB0380","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0380\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a recorded phone message left by a school counselor for a student's parents.**\n\n:audio{#be4f4d99-556c-484b-b2b4-38ee29a4317d aid=\"be4f4d99-556c-484b-b2b4-38ee29a4317d\"}\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The caller works at Jordan's school.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The incident took place during a history examination.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The incident occurred during an afternoon examination.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Ms. Hartley personally witnessed the incident during the exam.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$A mobile device had been concealed during the exam.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Jordan was found to be copying from written materials during the exam.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Jordan was uncooperative when asked about the incident.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Jordan acknowledged responsibility for their actions. \n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What grade will Jordan receive for the history exam?    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ When is the formal meeting scheduled?    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name **one** possible consequence discussed at the meeting.     [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1 - 4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, E, H}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I am the student counselor at Redfield High School”\\\n\nMs. Hartley identifies herself at the beginning of the message, confirming her role at the school.\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jordan was involved in a serious incident during this morning's history exam”\\\n\nThe text specifies that the exam subject was history, confirming that this is where the incident took place.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$...Jordan using a hidden phone”\\\n\nThe text states the phone was concealed, meaning it had been hidden on purpose during the exam.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jordan was cooperative and honest when we spoke and took full responsibility for what happened”\\\n\nTaking full responsibility means Jordan openly admitted to what they had done without attempting to deny it.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  C, D, F, G\n\n- **C**: The incident took place during a morning exam, not an afternoon one. The text states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$this morning's history exam.”\n\n- **D**: The text states, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a teacher observed Jordan” - Ms. Hartley was not present in the exam room. She spoke with Jordan afterward.\n\n- **F**: Jordan used a phone to photograph other students' answer sheets, not written materials such as notes or a textbook.\n\n- **G**: The text states Jordan $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$was cooperative and honest”, which is the opposite of uncooperative.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a zero}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Jordan will receive a zero for this exam”\\\n\nUnder the school's academic honesty policy, receiving a zero is the automatic consequence for cheating in an exam.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{next Tuesday at nine a.m./ 9:00 am}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a formal meeting next Tuesday at nine a.m.”\\\n\nThe text gives both the day and the time of the meeting, and both are required for a complete answer.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{a short suspension}$\n\n- $\\textrm{an honesty awareness workshop}$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$which may include a short suspension and an honesty awareness workshop”\\\n\nThe text mentions two possible outcomes of the meeting. In addition to the poor mark, Jordan will have to face further consequences as a result of the cheating incident."},{"ref":"EB0381","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0381\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a news reporter outside a school prom.**\n\n:audio{#4f676ce9-e82b-4a73-bbf2-c57d089a3b0d aid=\"4f676ce9-e82b-4a73-bbf2-c57d089a3b0d\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.**[4 marks]\n\n**School Newsletter**\n\nEach year, students in their final year of our school must fulfil a **[-1-]** to be admitted to the prom by finishing **[-2-]** of community service. Now running for **[-3-]**, the program was set up by the school board to strengthen community bonds. Seniors give their time in local organizations such as elderly care facilities and **[-4-]**.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did students feel after completing the hours?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$stressed \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$satisfied\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$comforted","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{requirement}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$every student had to complete a requirement”\\\n\nA requirement is something you have to do. Students had to complete their volunteer hours to go allowed to go to prom.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{one hundred hours}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: 100 hours\\\n\n\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$one hundred hours of community service”\\\n\nThe text gives the exact number of hours required to be allowed to attend the prom.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{third year}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$now in its third year”\\\n\nThe policy was introduced 3 years ago.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{local primary schools}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$local primary schools”\\\n\nAlong with food banks and care facilities, primary schools are one of the named volunteering locations in the text.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the experience was fulfilling”\\\n\nMost students felt that the experience of serving the community was *fulfilling*. After, they felt *satisfied*. Remember, an experience can be fulfilling or satisfying, and a person feels fulfilled or satisfied.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: The students felt stressed while doing the hours $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$alongside their studies,” but not after they had finished.\n\n- **C**: The students did not feel *comforted* after doing the hours. *Comforted* means to make someone feel better when they are sad or worried."},{"ref":"EB0382","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0382\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a teenager reading a letter of apology.**\n\n:audio{#5818791f-e6db-4ba5-b64b-3f82f93a31cf aid=\"5818791f-e6db-4ba5-b64b-3f82f93a31cf\"}\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Sam came to school in traditional clothing.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Asher made the comment after school.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The class stayed quiet after the comment.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Asher's family wore traditional dress on the weekend.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The grandmother's visit happened before Asher made the comment.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Asher had never found traditional clothing unusual at home.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Asher understood what they had done wrong after talking with Sam.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Asher's comment made Sam feel embarrassed about their culture.\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What made the writer finally understand the impact of their actions?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Remembering that Sam was upset.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Wondering if Sam had spoken to other students about it.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Imagining a family member being made fun of.","markScheme":"$1 - 4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, D, F, H}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you wore your traditional dress to school last week\"\\\n\nSam was wearing traditional clothing on the day the incident happened at school.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$She and my mom wore their traditional clothes...\"\\\n\nBoth Asher's grandmother and mother wore traditional dress - women wearing their cultural clothing was something Asher saw in his own home.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$never once thought it was strange or old-fashioned\"\\\n\nTraditional clothing had always been a completely normal part of life at home for Asher's family.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I made you feel that who you are - your culture - was something to be ashamed of\"\\\n\nAsher realized the comment had gone beyond the clothes themselves — it had attacked Sam's cultural identity directly.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  \n\n- **B**: The text states the comment was made $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in front of the class\" - not after school.\n\n- **C**: The text says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$people laughed\", which means that the class was not quiet.\n\n- **E**: The school incident happened $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$last week,\" and the grandmother's visit was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$yesterday.\" The visit came after the incident, not before.\n\n- **G**: There was no conversation with Sam after the comment was made.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I imagined someone laughing at my grandmother in her dress\"\\\n\nPicturing a family member being made fun of in the same way made Asher understand the real impact of what they had done to Sam.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, C, D\n\n- **A**: Sam's sadness is mentioned in paragraph 2, but this was not what led to Asher's realization - that came later, during the grandmother's visit.\n\n- **B**: There is no mention in the text of Sam speaking to other students about the incident."},{"ref":"EB0383","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0383\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a nurse talking to a friend about their first solo night shift.**\n\n:audio{#b7f46048-e474-4409-8b14-990a5af65d71 aid=\"b7f46048-e474-4409-8b14-990a5af65d71\"}\n\n**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It was the nurse's first time working alone.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The nurse was in charge of many patients.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The nurse felt relieved but still nervous.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Mrs. Banda was in bed six.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The nurse asked for advice before changing the medicine.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Mrs. Banda felt better in less than an hour.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What was the intern learning to do?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the nurse describe themselves professionally at the end of the shift?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1 - 3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{ A, C, F}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$No supervisor — just me and twelve patients”\\\n\nThe nurse was completely alone for the first time - there was no senior member of staff available to ask for help or rely on.\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I was happy to have been assigned to the small ward. But I was still nervous”\\\n\nA *ward* is a section of a hospital or clinic that serves one type of patient. Although the nurse was happy to be working in a smaller setting, the responsibility of being in charge on their own was still causing anxiety.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$twenty minutes later she was comfortable”\\\n\nTwenty minutes is less than an hour, so this statement is true. The nurse acted independently and Mrs. Banda’s condition improved quickly as a result.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, D, E\n\n- **B**: The text states the nurse was assigned to a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$small ward”, and we are told there were 12 patients, which is not a large number.\n\n- **D**: The text clearly states Mrs. Banda was in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$bed four,” not bed six.\n\n- **E**: There is no mention of the nurse consulting anyone. The text makes clear there was no supervisor present, and the nurse acted independently.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{enter data}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$how to enter data into the system”\\\n\nThe nurse took time during a quiet moment to guide the intern through the data entry process, already beginning to take on a mentoring role.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a true professional}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Now I feel like a true professional”\\\n\nHaving successfully managed the ward alone for the first time, the nurse felt they had experienced an important moment in their career."}]},{"name":"Health & Well-Being","questions":[{"ref":"EB0004","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0004\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{What Is Homeopathy?}}$\n\nHomeopathy is a medical system based on the belief that the body can cure itself. This method, developed in the late 1700s in Germany, follows the principle $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$like cures the like”. This idea suggests that a substance that triggers a certain disease in a healthy person can be used, in very small doses, to treat that disease. Unlike conventional medicine, which often focuses on directly targeting ailments, homeopathy aims to stimulate the body's self-healing mechanisms. The treatments are prepared from natural substances, including plants, minerals, and metals, diluted to ensure safety and minimize toxicity.\n\nHomeopathy offers a holistic approach to health by treating both physical symptoms and emotional or mental conditions. One of its key advantages is that it can be used by a wide range of people, including infants, children, the elderly, and even pregnant women, without causing serious side effects.  Furthermore, many supporters appreciate how quickly it can work in some cases, offering fast relief.\n\nAlthough homeopathy has been widely criticized by supporters of conventional medicine, it has gained global recognition. Many people choose it for its benign, non-invasive treatment methods and holistic approach to well-being.\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, what is the principle of homeopathy?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Using conventional medicine to treat diseases.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Using only herbal medicine.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Causing similar symptoms to attack the original symptom.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Treating only physical symptoms.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Which of the following is NOT considered a benefit of homeopathy?     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It causes negative effects.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It cures in a short period of time.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$It can be applied to a wide age range.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$It is supported worldwide.\n\n**Find the word of phrase which means the following:**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$diseases    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$trigger    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$adverse reactions    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This idea suggests that a substance that triggers a certain disease in a healthy person can be used, in very small doses, to treat that disease.\"\n\n- The text explains the basis of homeopathy as curing diseases by producing the same symptoms these diseases cause: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$like cures the like”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D\n\n- **A**: The preposition $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$unlike” suggests that it is different from conventional medicine.\n\n- **B**: The preparation of homeopathic medicine includes not only plants but also some other elements such as metals, minerals, and others.\n\n- **D**: Homeopathy is a holistic method; it treats not only physical but also emotional and mental illnesses.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions several benefits of homeopathy, one of which is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$no side effects.” Theherefore the appearance of negative effects can not be considered a benefit.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C, D\n\n- **B**: The text mentions that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$many supporters appreciate how quickly it can work” implying it acts in a short period of time.\n\n- **C**: The text lists the benefits of homeopathy covering all age ranges  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$infants, children, the elderly, and even pregnant women\"\n\n- **D**: The last benefit refers to the support and recognition homeopathy has gained at a global scale, which aligns with worldwide support.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{ailments}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *ailments* are *illnesses*, especially those which are not very serious. \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{stimulate}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Trigger* means incite, cause something to happen or to start, making this a good replacement word for *stimulate*.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{side effects}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *side effects* align with adverse reactions meaning an undesired effect caused by a drug or treatment."},{"ref":"EB0005","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0005\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Addressing Extreme Thinness in Media and Fashion}}$\n\nIn recent years, concerns have risen over the portrayal of extreme thinness in media and the fashion industry. Specialists are not only worried about the health implications for models but also about the broader societal effects on body image. In response, Israel has passed a law prohibiting models with a body mass index (BMI) below 18.5 from appearing in advertisements and shows. This law aims to promote the acceptance of all body types and encourage healthier standards in the fashion industry. This is part of an effort to stop glorifying the ultra-thin.\n\nReducing the number of images in the media portraying super-skinny models is expected to have a positive impact on women, especially in reducing the pressure to conform to unrealistic beauty standards. Claire Mysko, an advocate for body diversity, supports this view, stating that media exposure to a broader range of body types would contribute to healthier perceptions of beauty.\n\nWhile the law has sparked hope for change, opinions are divided. Some believe it could be the beginning of the end for the dominance of thin bodies in the media, while others are skeptical of its long-term effects. Despite the controversy, there is a clear movement toward celebrating body diversity and reducing harmful ideals.\n\n1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Experts' main concerns are related to health problems caused by extreme thinness.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The law passed in Israel prohibits models with low BMI.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The aim of this law is to support the acceptance of women of all sizes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The law discourages the acceptance of body diversities in the fashion industry.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Social media supports extreme thinness.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The fewer images portraying extremely thin models, the less negative impact these will have on women.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Claire Mysko thinks a solution to this problem will be to show more varied bodies.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, the law passed in Israel is the catalyst needed to make a change.\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in the text.**\n\nThe aim of the law is to visualize all types of bodies and stop praising the **[-5-]**.   [1 mark]\n\nThe portrayal of only one image of beauty can be **[-6-]**.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers 1 to 4 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, C, F, G}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **B**. “Israel has passed a law prohibiting models with a body mass index (BMI) below 18.5 from appearing in advertisements and shows.”\n\n- The law passed supports the banning of models from advertisements or shows whose BMI is less than 18.5.\n\n- **C**. According to the text, the passing of this law is part of an effort “to promote the acceptance of all body types”. “All body types” aligns with “women of all sizes”.\n\n- **F**. “Reducing the number of images in the media portraying super-skinny models is expected to have a positive impact on women”\n\n- The text suggests that reducing the number of images portraying extremely thin models in the media will be beneficial for them as they will not be pressured to conform to “unrealistic beauty standards”.\n\n- **G**. “Claire Mysko, an advocate for body diversity, supports this view” Claire Myrko states that the media could help spread information about different types of bodies, which would benefit women.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: A, D, E, H\n\n- **A**.  “not only worried about the health implications for models” The use of the structure “not only… but also… implies that the concerns are twofold. According to the text, experts are worried about health problems as well as possible broader societal effects.\n\n- **D**. T The text states this law “aims to promote the acceptance of all body types and encourage healthier standards.” so the word *discourages* makes the statement false.\n\n- **E**. The statement is incorrect as the text does not mention that social media supports extreme thinness.\n\n- **H**. “Some believe it could be the beginning of the end for the dominance of thin bodies in the media, while others are skeptical” This quote implies that though some people expect the law to be the beginning of change, others are not sure (skeptical) As there are divided opinions, the answer is incorrect.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{ultra-thin/super skinny}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Both adjectives are mentioned in the text and both can be appropriate in this sentence\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{harmful}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This is the only possible word as the text mentions “there is a clear movement toward celebrating body diversity and reducing harmful ideals\""},{"ref":"EB0006","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0006\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Mindfulness: A Pathway to a Calmer Mind and Well-being}}$\n\nHave you ever felt like your mind is so full of thoughts that it's hard to concentrate? It's as though your brain is always going back to past events or preparing for future ones, not allowing you to fully enjoy the present. Our minds are constantly creating stories or imagining different scenarios, which can make it hard to stay focused on what we're actually doing. \n\nPracticing mindfulness is like finding a peaceful spot in your mind where you can notice what is happening in the moment without judgement. Although it may seem challenging at first, it's a skill that grows with regular practice.\n\nIf your goal is to add calm to your daily life and truly appreciate each moment, this is a great place to begin.\n\nMindfulness helps clear the mind of overwhelming thoughts, promoting an inner sense of calm. It aids in managing emotions, reducing stress responses, and strengthens focus and attention, which is essential in today's busy world. Practicing mindfulness enhances listening and  $\\underline{\\textrm{empathy}}$, fostering deeper connections with others. Overall, it provides effective tools for building mental $\\underline{\\textrm{resilience}}$ and supporting psychological $\\underline{\\textrm{well-being}}$.\n\n**What do the following words mean in the context of the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$empathy|$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$health|\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$resilience|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$responsibility|\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$well-being|$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$comfort|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$understanding|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$resistance|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$strength|\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What are **two** of the main purposes of mindfulness mentioned in the article?    [2 marks]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does mindfulness contribute to developing better relationships?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Both *empathy* and *understanding* are synonyms in the text as they refer to awareness of other people’s feelings.\n\n- **Option C** could be confusing in this space, its meaning focuses on providing care, or relief, not understanding of someone’s feelings.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- In this context both *resilience* and *strength* are used as synonyms to mean “toughness to face difficulties.”\n\n- *Resistance* **(option E)** could be considered here as *resistance* and *resilience* could be related. However, *resistance* focuses on opposing forces, not to adaptation.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Health* is a good replacement word for the phrase *well-being* in the context of this text.\n\n- **Option C**, *comfort*, cannot be used in any available space.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Any **two** of the following:\n\n - Clearing the mind[1 mark]\n\n - Creating a sense of inner calm [1 mark]\n\n - Managing emotions [1 mark]\n\n - Practicing focus [1 mark]\n\n - Improving listening and empathy [1 mark]\n\n - Providing tools for improving [1 mark]\n\n - Mental health [1 mark]\n\n  [2 marks]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{it improves listening and empathy}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Practicing mindfulness enhances listening and empathy, fostering deeper connections with others.\" \n\nThe text mentions that through better listening and empathy, addressing how mindfulness helps people build better relationships."},{"ref":"EB0134","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0134\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to listen to part of a lecture by a professor of psychology on ambiverts.**\n\n:audio{#bae868fd-a7bf-437f-a508-04c322f67859 aid=\"bae868fd-a7bf-437f-a508-04c322f67859\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the primary characteristic of being an ambivert?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They excel at communication only.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Their ability to adapt to different social situations.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They can build strong relationships.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How do ambiverts manage their social interactions?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They avoid all social situations.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They balance social and quiet time effectively.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They prefer constant social engagement.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, what skill makes ambiverts good at interpersonal communication?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Always being talkative.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Always being quiet.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Knowing when to listen and when to speak.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the professor say about ambiverts at work?  [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They naturally manage work-life balance better.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They only work well alone.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They prefer working with large groups.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker's tone can best be described as:   [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Critical and judgemental\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Informative and balanced\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Overly enthusiastic","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text states ambiverts, “adapt easily to both social and quiet settings\" as their primary characteristic.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect because the text mentions communication as one of several skills, not the primary one.\n\n- **Option C**: is incorrect, because building relationships is presented as a result of their communication skills, not the primary characteristic, which is the ability to adapt.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions they \"avoid overstimulation or loneliness\" by balancing their social energy. The word *overstimulation* can mean *hyperactive* or *overwhelmed*. Which means ambiverts avoid feeling like this in social interactions.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect, because the text specifically states they can adapt to social settings, so they don’t avoid them.\n- **Option C**: is also incorrect, because the text emphasizes balance, rather than constant social engagement.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text directly states they excel at \"knowing when to listen and when to assert themselves.\" The expression *assert themselve* means to behave in a way that expresses your confidence, importance or power in a way that earns you respect from others.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect because the text emphasizes balance in communication, not constant talking.\n- **Option B**:  is also incorrect, because the text emphasizes the importance of both listening and speaking.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text explicitly states, \"Ambiverts are successful at managing personal and professional dynamics, often striking a healthy work-life balance.\" *Work-life balance* is a good replacement for *personal and professional dynamics*, as they both imply that ambiverts can effectively balance the public and private parts of their lives.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **Option B**: is incorrect because the text mentions they can adapt to both social and quiet settings.\n- **Option C**: is also  incorrect because the text doesn't mention preference for group size.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text presents balanced, factual information without strong opinions.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: is incorrect because there are no critical or negative judgements in the text.\n- **Option C**: is also incorrect because the tone maintains professional objectivity without excessive enthusiasm."},{"ref":"EB0135","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0135\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a radio host introduce the idea of pet-ternity\\* leave as a way of boosting employees' well-being.**\n\n:audio{#e48458a3-a826-413f-bead-4afa5037072d aid=\"e48458a3-a826-413f-bead-4afa5037072d\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How much are you paid on pet-ternity leave? [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Who introduced this policy?  [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Who inspired the creation of this policy?     [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do supporters say the main benefit of this policy is?     [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the text describe the public response to this idea?     [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{full pay}$\n\n- *Accept any other answer with the same meaning* [1 mark]\n\n- The second part of the text starts with this information exactly: “Imagine three weeks off work, full pay…\"\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Derrick Moon (an IT boss)/ (an) IT boss}$\n\n- *Also accept*: Derrick Moon (an IT boss) or An IT boss [1 mark]\n\n- The text says that “Derrick Moon, an IT boss, is making it happen,\" so he is the person who introduced the policy.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{his wife}$ [1 mark]\n\n- Derrick Moon was inspired by his wife, which is stated in the fragment, “After his wife took nine months to care for their dogs…\"\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{great for mental health}$\n\n- *Also accept*: reduces stress\n\n- boosts happiness\n\n- *Accept other words with similar meaning*. [1 mark]\n\n- The text lists the main benefits of pet-ternity in the following sentence: “Advocates say it's great for mental health—caring for pets reduces stress and boosts happiness.\"\n\n- The word *boost* means improve or enhance, in this case happiness.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{sparking plenty of debate}$\n\n- *Accept other words with similar meaning.* [1 mark]\n\n- The text concludes with the sentence describing the public response to the idea of pet-ternity leave , \"Love it or not, it's sparking plenty of debate,” which clearly shows the idea is controversial, as the phrase *sparking debate* implies this topic is the source of arguments and divided opinions."},{"ref":"EB0136","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0136\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a part of a programme on self-care and well-being promoting taking ‘ussies’ instead of selfies.**\n\n:audio{#87d5b63c-86dd-4770-b5dc-d5ac66d58ba3 aid=\"87d5b63c-86dd-4770-b5dc-d5ac66d58ba3\"}\n\n1 - 5.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the 5 correct statements.** [5 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Ussies are photos taken with other people.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The text suggests selfies can promote perfectionism.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Ussies help combat feelings of isolation.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Research shows social connections reduce stress levels.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Ussies create lasting memories with others.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The text claims “ussies” are always better than selfies.\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The text suggests never taking selfies again.\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The conclusion encourages including others in photos.","markScheme":"1 - 5.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, C, D, H}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **A**. “photos taken with others\"\n\n- The text directly defines ussies as pictures with other people.\n\n- **B**. “Unlike selfies, which can sometimes feed into perfectionism\"\n\n- The fragment shows that selfies can promote perfectionism, as the expression *feed into* in this context means *promote* and *strengthen*.\n\n- **C**. “helping to combat feelings of isolation\"\n\n- It is directly stated in the fragment that ussies help overcome such feelings, and the word *combat* means fight, overcome\n\n- **D**. “Research shows that social connections lower stress,\"\n\n- The text states explicitly that social connections have a beneficial influence on high stress levels. The word *lower* is a synonym of *reduce*.\n\n- **H**. “invite someone into the frame\",\n\n- The conclusion explicitly calls for inviting others to join the picture, and the word frame in question is literally the photo frame.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: E, F, G\n\n- **E**.  “It’s a simple way to make memories”\n\n- While the text mentions making memories in the conclusion, it doesn't specifically claim they are “lasting\", that is long-term memories - this would be an overstatement of what the text actually says.\n\n- **F**. “Unlike selfies, which can sometimes feed into perfectionism or self-focus, ussies emphasize togetherness”\n\n- The text doesn't make this absolute claim, only emphasizes the benefits of ‘ussies’,  while the word ‘always’ makes the statement in F sound so.\n\n- **G**. “So, next time you reach for your phone, invite someone into the frame. “\n\n- Text encourages listeners to take more ussies, not *eliminate* selfies (which is the synonym to *never taking selfies* again)."},{"ref":"EB0266","marks":"7","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0266\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**Food noise**\n\nAn interview with Dr. Anya Sharma’s. \n\n**[-1-]**\\\n\nFood noise describes the intrusive thoughts about food that many people experience. It's more than just feeling hungry; it's a constant internal monologue about eating. This can include obsessing over your next meal, regretting something you ate, or intense cravings for certain foods. This “mental chatter” can be very disruptive and make it difficult to focus on other aspects of daily life.\n\n**[-2-]**\\\n\nA combination of our biology and our modern environmental factors, almost anything, really. For example, hormones that regulate hunger can send powerful signals to our brain. When these triggers intensify, the “noise” gets louder. \n\nThe thing is, we are constantly bombarded with food advertisements, social media posts, and the easy availability of mouth-watering processed foods. This, in turn, creates a cycle in which our brains are nonstop triggered to think about eating.\n\n**[-3-]**\\\n\nA key strategy is to focus on balanced nutrition. Eating meals rich in protein and fiber can help regulate hunger hormones and help you feel satisfied, which quiets the noise. Additionally, mindfulness practices can help individuals recognize and manage these thoughts without actually acting on them. Finally, regular exercise helps reduce stress and can be a healthy distraction from “food noise.”\n\n**Choose the correct question to fill the gaps 1-3.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$What problems does “food noise” describe?\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$What can intensify food-related thoughts? \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$How does social media increase “food noise”?\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$How can balanced nutrition help reduce food noise?  \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$What helps manage food noise effectively? \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$How does food noise affect your daily life?\n\n**Find the word or phrase in the text which means the following:** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$continual     [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$desires    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$increase    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$tasty    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mental chatter” can be very disruptive and make it difficult to focus on other aspects of daily life.\"\\\n\nThis answer deals with the effects of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$food noise.” It mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$intrusive thoughts,” obsession, or food cravings, as well as the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mental chatter” suffered by people who experience $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$food noise.”\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is the distractor here. This question asks for a description of the problem of food noise, not about its daily impact, which is the central idea of this paragraph.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A combination of our biology and our modern environmental factors, almost anything, really.”\\\n\nThis answer addresses both internal and external causes of food noise.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is the distractor here. The text does mention social media, but this is not the focus of the answer\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A key strategy is to focus on balanced nutrition”\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ ... mindfulness practices can help ...”\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... regular exercise helps...”\\\n\nThis answer is about all possible methods for food noise management, such as nutrition, mindfulness, and exercise.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is incorrect as it only focuses on balanced nutrition as a way to manage $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$food noise.”\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{constant}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... it's a constant internal monologue about eating.” \\\n\n*Constant* means something that continues over time without stopping, which matches the idea of *continual*. In the text, it describes food-related thoughts that don't go away easily.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{cravings}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... intense cravings for certain foods...”\\\n\n*Cravings* are strong desires or urges to have something; here, *cravings* refer to a strong wish to eat particular foods.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{intensify}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When these triggers intensify, the \"noise\" gets louder.”\\\n\n*Intensify* means make something deeper, stronger, or more intense. In the text, it shows how outside influences increase the strength of food-related thoughts or feelings.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{mouth-watering}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... the easy availability of mouth-watering processed foods...”\\\n\n*Mouth-watering* means pleasant to taste, appetizing. The same idea as *tasty*. In the text, it refers to foods designed to be especially enjoyable."},{"ref":"EB0267","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0267\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Safeism: Are We Too Obsessed with Safety?**\n\nThese days it often feels like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$safety” has become one of the most valuable assets. But is aiming [-1-] total safety always better for us? How is the rise of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$safeism”- the mindset that we must eliminate every possible risk- changing how we live, learn, and interact?\n\nAt school, trigger warnings and safe spaces are everywhere. On social media, it's easy to block [-2-] uncomfortable ideas. We're told to avoid tough conversations, shield ourselves [-3-] distress, and always seek comfort. \n\nOn the surface, it sounds good: who wouldn't want to feel safe? But is this pursuit actually helping our mental health, or is it making us less resilient?\n\nSome psychologists warn that safeism can create a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fragile” mindset. If we never face difficulties, how do we learn to cope with stress or setbacks? Avoiding discomfort doesn't make problems disappear; it may actually leave us unprepared for real life. \n\nOf course, creating supportive environments is important, especially for those who truly need them. But maybe instead of removing all challenges, we should focus more on building real resilience.\n\nLet's encourage open dialogue and healthy risks. Sometimes, the path to well-being runs straight through discomfort- not around it.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the text.** [3 marks]\n\nThese days it often feels like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$safety” has become one of the most valuable assets. But is aiming **[-1-]** total safety always better for us? How is the rise of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$safeism,” the mindset that we must eliminate every possible risk, changing how we live, learn, and interact?\n\nAt school, trigger warnings and safe spaces are everywhere. On social media, it's easy to block **[-2-]** uncomfortable ideas. We're told to avoid tough conversations, shield ourselves **[-3-]** distress, and always seek comfort. \n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$from\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$against\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$for\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$out\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$off\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$at\n\n**Choose an appropriate ending from the list that completes each sentence.** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Mental health professionals caution that...    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Without experiencing challenging situations...     [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$It's necessary to provide environments...     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$... that support people, especially those who need them.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$... following the principles of safeism may result in individuals lacking resilience.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$... people may struggle to develop effective ways to manage pressure or obstacles.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$... strengthening people's ability to face challenges is not easy.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$... you can't solve underlying issues.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$... that can lead to better preparation for everyday realities.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... is aiming for total safety always better for us?”\\\n\nThe phrase *aiming* for means to pursue or strive toward something, which fits logically in this context. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option F** is the distractor here, as you can *aim at* things (literally, for example, with a weapon), but contrary to *aiming for* things, which is trying to achieve something, it doesn't make sense here.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... it's easy to block out uncomfortable ideas.”\\\n\n*Block out* means to prevent something from entering or affecting you, fitting with the idea of avoiding uncomfortable ideas online.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option E** is the distractor here, as although *block something off* sounds logical, it actually refers to closing a physical space (like *blocking off* a road), rather than filtering or ignoring information or ideas, which *block out* stands for.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... shield ourselves from distress... ”\\\n\nThe correct preposition with *shield* is *from*, indicating what you protect yourself against.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is the distractor here, as, although it sounds quite logical to *shield somebody against something*, it is not grammatically correct.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Some psychologists warn that safeism can create a $`$fragile' mindset.”\\\n\nThis matches well with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$adopting the principles of safeism may result in individuals lacking resilience,” which is what the metaphor *fragile mindset* stands for.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** is the distractor, as, although it is related to resilience, the original statement is about the risks of safeism, not the difficulty of building resilience.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$If we never face difficulties, how do we learn to cope with stress or setbacks?”\\\n\nThe phrases *to face difficulties* and *to experience challenges* are similar in meaning. The sentence expresses that without these challenges, there is no way to develop resilience ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$effective ways to manage pressure or obstacles”)\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option E** is the distractor here, as the original sentence is about developing coping skills, not about solving deeper problems.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$creating supportive environments is important, especially for those who truly need it.”\\\n\nThe sentences are similar in meaning. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Provide environments that support people\" and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$create supportive environments\" are similar in meaning in this context. \\\n\n\\\n\n**Option F** is the distractor here, as this phrase relates more to being unprepared for real life (previous sentence), not the need for support."},{"ref":"EB0268","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0268\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nDecember 23rd  \n\nToday was Festivus, and honestly, the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Airing of Grievances” might just be my new favorite wellness ritual. I gathered my friends and family, and instead of bottling up the small irritations, the grievances from the year, we set them free- openly, but with humor. It felt strange at first, putting words to frustrations I usually swallow, but once we started, I realized how refreshing it was.  \n\nThere's something deeply freeing in voicing one's complaints this way. Saying, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I was upset when you canceled on me last minute,” in a safe, structured setting eased the tension I didn't even know was there. We laughed at the petty complaints, like my brother never responding to texts, but we also acknowledged deeper feelings that needed communicating. My chest felt lighter, my jaw less tight, as if my body was releasing stress. \n\nWhen it was my turn to listen, I practiced not taking things personally. Instead, I heard them as invitations to reflect and improve. By the end, the room felt lighter too, there was less resentment and more connection. Who knew that Festivus could be therapeutic? I'm convinced: releasing grievances, instead of burying them, is a true medicine for the mind and body.  \n\n**Choose the three correct statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The author spent Festivus with his loved ones. \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It felt natural to talk about the frustrations. \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Telling people about their complaints made the author tense.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The event included both playful and serious complaints.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Most complaints were about the writer's brother.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer felt emotionally better after talking about year-long irritations. \n\n**What do the following words mean in paragraph 3? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|4.$\\hspace{1em}$invitations |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$complaints |\n\n|5.$\\hspace{1em}$resentment |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$solution |\n\n|6.$\\hspace{1em}$medicine |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$anger |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$tension |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$remedy |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$possibilities |","markScheme":"1- 3.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, D, F}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I gathered my friends and family...\"\\\n\nThe writer states they were together with friends and family, so his loved ones, for Festivus.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$We laughed at the petty complaints, but we also acknowledged deeper feelings that needed airing.\"\\\n\nThe author notes that both minor (*petty* - small, unimportant) complaints and more serious issues were discussed.\n\n- **F**:  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$My chest felt lighter, my jaw less tight, as if my body was releasing stress.\"\\\n\nThe author felt physical and emotional relief following the airing of grievances.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C, E\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It felt strange at first”\\\n\nThe author says it felt strange to air grievances at first, not natural. \n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Saying... eased the tension”\\\n\nThe author mentions that telling people what had made them angry *eased the tension*, which means it made them feel less tense.\n\n- **E**: The writer mentions one of the complaints was about their brother but they were not only about him.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Instead, I heard them as invitations to reflect and improve.”\\\n\nThough *invitations* and *possibilities* are not synonyms, they both express that the writer felt what was said to them offered a chance to change and improve their attitude or behavior.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A**, *complaints*, does not express the idea that change is possible; instead, it implies that something is wrong and needs to be fixed. This will make the sentence negative and critical.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$there was less resentment and more connection”\\\n\n*Tension* is a good replacement word for *resentment* in this context, as it expresses that, after communicating their grievances, the room felt less tense.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** is the distractor here, as *anger* is a stronger, more violent emotion, whereas *resentment* implies a subtler feeling of bitterness.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'm convinced: releasing grievances, instead of burying them, is a true medicine for the mind and body.” \\\n\n*Remedy* and *medicine* are similar in meaning. They both convey that this process works, metaphorically, heals $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the mind and body.”\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B**, *solution*, could sound possible. However, a solution does not imply healing, which is a process."},{"ref":"EB0330","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0330\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a teenager talking about health advice they receive from two sources.**\n\n:audio{#7a2f5bac-6618-409b-81f4-d80903e7d64d aid=\"7a2f5bac-6618-409b-81f4-d80903e7d64d\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps using no more than three words for each answer.**\n\nThe speaker feels caught between **[-1-]** because the advice they receive is different.    [1 mark]\n\nFamily members suggest simple habits, such as eating when you are **[-2-]** and sleeping enough.    [1 mark]\n\nIn contrast, online advice encourages strict control and waking up very **[-3-]**.    [1 mark]\n\nThe speaker decides to trust their **[-4-]** more than online trends.    [1 mark]\n\nAs a result, the speaker says they are already feeling **[-5-]**.    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What best describes the speaker's attitude toward online health advice now?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They follow all online advice carefully.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They completely stop using social media.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They look at online tips but do not follow everything.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{two worlds}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I often feel caught between two worlds”\\\n\nThe student uses this phrase to describe the conflict between family advice and social media advice.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{hungry}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$My grandmother says to eat when you're hungry.”\\\n\nThis shows that the family's advice focuses on listening to natural signals rather than strict rules.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{early}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Online friends say wake up at 5 a.m. and exercise.”\\\n\nWaking up at 5 a.m. is an example of waking up very early, which reflects the pressure of online wellness culture.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{family's ideas / family}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I finally decided to trust my family's ideas and ignore most online trends.”\\\n\nThe student states that they choose to follow their family's advice instead of online trends.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{healthier}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I'm already feeling healthier from listening to my family!”\\\n\nFollowing their family's advice has had a positive effect on their health.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$still look at online tips, of course, but I don't try to do everything”\\\n\nThis shows that the student does not completely ignore online advice but is selective about what they follow.\n\n- *Incorrect options:** A, B\n\n- **A**: The speaker does not follow all online advice; they choose selectively.\n\n- **B**: The speaker continues to use social media; they do not stop completely."},{"ref":"EB0331","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0331\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a university student reflecting on their experience of tracking health habits.**\n\n:audio{#489edc2e-d907-4313-a12e-32dd25afb84b aid=\"489edc2e-d907-4313-a12e-32dd25afb84b\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What did the speaker install on their phone?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name **two** things the speaker tracked.    [2 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How often did the speaker check the app later on?    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What did the speaker compare themselves to?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the speaker feel?    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What did the speaker finally do with the app?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a habit tracker}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Last year, I installed a habit tracker on my phone.”\\\n\nAlso accept: app\n\n- The speaker tells us that the app they installed was a habit tracker used to monitor health-related activities.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any two of the following answers:*  \n\n- $\\textrm{steps}$\n\n- $\\textrm{sleep}$\n\n- $\\textrm{water}$\n\n- $\\textrm{vegetables}$        [2 marks]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I counted steps, sleep, water, even how many vegetables I ate.”\\\n\nThe speaker lists several habits they tracked. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{dozens of times (a day)}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I found myself checking the app dozens of times a day.”\\\n\nThis shows that the speaker became overly focused on the app, checking it very frequently.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{“ideal” numbers}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I started comparing myself to $`$ideal' numbers.”\\\n\nThe speaker explains that they judged their health by comparing their results to target numbers set by the app.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{anxious}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Instead of feeling healthy, I felt anxious.”\\\n\nThe speaker directly contrasts how they expected to feel (happy) with how the habit tracking actually made them feel (anxious).\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{deleted the app}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I finally deleted the app.”\\\n\nThe speaker explains that they stopped using the habit tracker completely by removing it from their phone."},{"ref":"EB0332","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0332\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a secondary school student talking about how they use music in their daily life.**\n\n:audio{#958f0dd9-9ba6-45fc-879e-88ed40088a1c aid=\"958f0dd9-9ba6-45fc-879e-88ed40088a1c\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What kind of songs help the student wake up in the morning?    [1 mark]\n\n**Find the four true statements.**[4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The student listens to different playlists depending on the time of day.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Morning music is relaxed and quiet.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The student changes their school playlist every morning. \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Music helps the student feel calmer before school. \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The student uses a playlist to celebrate good feelings.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Music affects the student's thoughts and feelings. \n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$When they are stressed, the student does not listen to music. \n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The student does not spend much time choosing music.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fast beats}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Morning songs wake me up with fast beats and lots of energy.”\\\n\nThe student explains that energetic music helps them wake up in the morning.\n\n$2 - 5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, D, E, F}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I have a playlist for everything. Morning songs wake me up...”\\\n\nThe student explains that they have different playlists for different situations, including mornings and the journey to school.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$On the way to school, I play the same quiet tracks every day because they calm me...” \\\n\nThe student states that listening to the same music daily before school helps them feel calm and ready to focus.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I have a $`$happy' playlist that I use to celebrate when I'm feeling good” \\\n\nThe student describes using a specific playlist to celebrate positive emotions.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Music changes how I move, how I think and how I feel” \\\n\nThe speaker explains that music affects their emotions, thoughts, and physical well-being.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C, G, H \n\n- **B**: The student mentions that their morning playlist has $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fast beats and lots of energy,” which is the opposite of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$relaxed and quiet”.\n\n- **C**: The student says they play $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the same tracks every day” on the way to school, not that they change the playlist daily.\n\n- **G**: The student says he used music $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$last week I used it to help me feel better when I was feeling stressed,” so they do listen to music when stressed.\n\n- **H**: The student says they $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$take time to make playlists,” which shows they do spend time choosing music."}]},{"name":"Language & Identity","questions":[{"ref":"EB0013","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0013\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n$\\LARGE{\\textrm{The Power of Language in Shaping Identity}}$\n\nLanguage is more than a means of communication; it is a powerful tool that shapes who we are and how we connect with the world. For many, speaking a native language is deeply tied to cultural identity and personal heritage. When someone grows up speaking two or more languages, they often develop what is called a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$dual identity.” This dual identity allows people to shift between cultures, adopting different customs, expressions, and ways of thinking depending on the language they are using.\n\nStudies have shown that being bilingual or multilingual can enhance cognitive skills, such as problem-solving and creativity. However,  $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$  also comes with challenges. In some communities, people may feel pressured to use a dominant language to fit in, risking the loss of  $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ original language and, consequently, part of their cultural identity.\n\nPreserving one's native language can be a form of resistance against cultural assimilation. $\\underline{\\textrm{It}}$ allows individuals to stay connected to their roots and pass on traditions to future generations. In an increasingly globalised world, maintaining a strong connection to one's linguistic heritage can strengthen a person's sense of belonging and identity.\n\nLanguage, therefore, is not just about words — it is about who we are and how we define ourselves.\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for 1 mark.** [5]\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Speaking multiple languages guarantees an individual will have a stronger sense of identity.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Speaking a dominant language can sometimes pressure individuals to abandon their native language    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Maintaining one's native language contributes to preserving cultural identity.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Language only serves as a method of communication and has no effect on identity.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Individuals who speak more than one language can adopt different perspectives based on the language they are using.    [1 mark]\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.** \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$However, $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$ also comes with…\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$…the loss of $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ original language…\n\n    [1 mark]\n\n$8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\underline{\\textrm{It}}$ allows individuals…\n\n    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They often develop what is called a $\\text{\\textquoteleft}$dual identity'.” \\\n\nThe text implies that bilingualism allows for cultural shifts but does not guarantee a stronger sense of identity.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True\n\n}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$People may feel pressured to use a dominant language to fit in”.\\\n\nThe text indicates a risk to the loss of their original language.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Preserving one's native language can be a form of resistance against cultural assimilation,\"\\\n\nThis shows that using our native language supports cultural identity.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Language is more than a means of communication” \n\n- This indicates language also significantly impacts identity.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This dual identity allows people to shift between cultures” \n\n- This highlights how bilingual individuals can adopt different perspectives based on the language they use.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{being bilingual or multilingual}$\n\n- *It* here refers to the challenge or experience of bilingualism, as mentioned in the discussion of how it can come with both benefits and challenges.  [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{people }$\n\n- *Their* refers to the individuals/people within certain communities who feel pressure to abandon their native language to fit in with the dominant culture.   [1 mark]\n\n$8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Preserving one's native language}$\n\n- *It* refers to the act of preserving one's native language, which serves as a connection to cultural heritage and identity.     [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0014","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0014\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{The Lost Word}}$\n\nIn a small village, there lived an elderly woman named Elena, known for her rich storytelling. She spoke a unique dialect that combined words from her native language with phrases borrowed from neighbouring cultures. This dialect was a tapestry of her life experiences, connecting her to her heritage and the world around her.\n\nOne day, a group of young students visited Elena to learn about her stories. They were fascinated by the colourful language she used, which held meanings that went beyond mere words. However, they struggled to understand some of the terms, as they were unfamiliar with the nuances of her dialect.\n\nElena noticed this and decided to teach them a special word from her language: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sukari,” which meant both $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sweetness” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$belonging.” She explained how language shapes identity; every word carries emotions and memories. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When you understand a word, you connect to the heart of the culture it comes from,” she said.\n\nInspired, the students began to explore their own languages, discovering the richness of their identities. They realised that language isn't just about communication; it's a key to understanding themselves and their place in the world.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the list that completes each gap in the following text.**\n\nIn a small village, Elena was known for her storytelling, speaking a dialect that mixed words from her native language **[-1-]** expressions from other cultures. A group of students visited to hear her tales, but they struggled to understand some of her words. Elena shared $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sukari,” meaning both sweetness, and belonging **[-2-]** a community and she explained how language shapes identity. The students realised that **[-3-]** appreciating their roots, they could connect more deeply with themselves. Elena demonstrated how language links **[-4-]** cultural heritage and identity and gives an understanding **[-5-]** different perspectives.\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$of\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$while\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$with\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$in\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$and\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$to\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$by\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$for\n\n- I.$\\hspace{1em}$which\n\n- J.$\\hspace{1em}$how\n\n[5 marks]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The author's style in the text is...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$reflective\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$persuasive\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$critical\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$narrative","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *With* indicates the way Elena's dialect combines her native language with expressions from other cultures. It emphasises the integration of these elements in her storytelling.\\\n\nWhile **Option E**, *and*, may seem like an option, it implies addition rather than integration and misses the nuance of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mixing.\"\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *In* conveys the sense of belonging within a community, specifying a space or context where $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$sukari\" has meaning.\\\n\n**Option F**, *to*, could be a possible word to fill in this space, however, the meaning of the phrase would imply a more formal relationship with a community. Additionally, *to* is used later on, which is also a clue not to use in this space.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *By* is the correct word, as this means *through* the appreciation of their culture.\\\n\nThough *while* (**Option B**) may seem like an option, this would imply a contrast in the following statement.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *To* shows the relationship between language and cultural heritage, suggesting a link or connection and specifying that language acts as the pathway connecting cultural heritage and identity.\\\n\nWhile *with* (**Option C**) can seem like an option, it would not be grammatically correct, as the correct collocation is *link to*.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Of* expresses the connection between understanding and different perspectives, indicating that understanding implies or is related to various viewpoints.\\\n\n*For* might seem like a possible option. However, if we used *for* (**Option H**) instead of *of*, as in $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$an understanding for different perspectives,\" it would imply that the understanding is intended for the benefit of different perspectives.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text is a narrative, as it tells a story about Elena and her interactions with the students, illustrating the themes of language and identity through personal experience. \n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, C\n\n- **A**: This is not completely correct, while the text does encourage thought about language and identity, it is primarily a story, not an introspective piece. \n\n- **B**: The text is not persuasive as it does not attempt to convince the reader of a particular viewpoint; instead, it shares a narrative about cultural connection.\n\n- **C**: The text doesn't critique or analyse issues in a negative manner; it focuses on celebrating the richness of language and identity."},{"ref":"EB0015","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0015\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Why Language Matters: A Journey of Identity}}$\n\nHave you ever considered how the languages we speak shape who we are? For many, language is more than just a means of communication; it's a key part of our identity, connecting us to our culture and heritage.\n\nGrowing up in a bilingual household, I often felt torn between two worlds. At home, I spoke Spanish with my family, while outside, I switched to English with my friends. It wasn't always easy, and sometimes I felt I had to choose one identity over the other. However, as I matured, I realised that navigating both languages enriched my life.\n\nLanguage has a powerful ability to bring people together. When I started sharing my experiences in both Spanish and English, I noticed how others connected to my stories differently depending on the language I used. Each language carries its own unique perspective.\n\nNow, I celebrate my bilingual identity. Switching languages fills me with pride in my heritage. Embracing both sides has helped me grow personally and connect with a wider community. So, next time you engage in conversation, remember: language is more than words; it's a bridge to understanding and connection.\n\n**Choose an appropriate ending from the list that completes each sentence.**\n\n[3 marks]\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Language...    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Identity...    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Bilingualism...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$can present challenges, making individuals feel divided between different cultural influences.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$plays a significant role in defining our personal experiences and how we relate to others.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$is fundamental to how we connect with our backgrounds and communities.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$creates a sense of division between different groups, often complicating personal connections.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$enables individuals to navigate diverse cultural landscapes, enriching their lives.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$is merely a tool for communication without deeper significance.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What phrase in the text indicates the speaker was sometimes confused due to their bilingualism?    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, what impact does language have on people?     [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$language is a key part of our identity, connecting us to our culture and heritage,” supports the idea that language fundamentally links individuals to their backgrounds and communities, emphasising its importance for cultural connection.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it's a key part of our identity, connecting us to our culture and heritage,” justifies that identity plays a significant role in shaping personal experiences and relationships, reinforcing how deeply language is tied to personal and cultural identity.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Switching languages fills me with pride in my heritage,” shows how bilingualism enriches the speaker's life by enabling them to navigate diverse cultural experiences and connections, underscoring its value beyond simple communication.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, D, F\n\n- **A**: The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$navigating both languages enriched my life,” suggests that, while bilingualism may involve navigating two cultures, it is ultimately presented as a positive and enriching experience rather than one that creates a sense of division.\n\n- **D**: The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$language has a powerful ability to bring people together,” directly contradicts the idea of division. Instead, the text emphasises bilingualism's unifying effect, promoting connection rather than complication or separation.\n\n- **F**: The quote, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$language is more than words; it's a bridge to understanding and connection,” clearly opposes the notion of language being $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$merely a tool for communication.” The text stresses language's deeper role in shaping identity and building cultural connections.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Torn between two worlds }$   [1 mark]\n\n- “I often felt torn between two worlds. At home, I spoke Spanish with my family, while outside, I switched to English\"\n\n- This directly reflects the speaker's internal conflict and confusion while navigating two languages and cultures by suggesting a feeling of being pulled in different directions. \n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{ability to bring people together}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept:  (It/ Language has) (a powerful) ability to bring people together.\n\n- This emphasises how language goes beyond communication to create shared understanding and bonds between people depending on the language used."},{"ref":"EB0093","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0093\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a young woman reflecting on her experience of taking her husband’s name.**\n\n:audio{#6c7a42ea-f3f9-461d-85d3-95ef0be6910e aid=\"6c7a42ea-f3f9-461d-85d3-95ef0be6910e\"}\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the speaker initially feel about taking her husband's surname?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Indifferent\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Excited\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Regretful\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What did the speaker associate her maiden name with?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Her family’s history and her identity\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Her husband’s family\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$A new beginning\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What theme does the speaker explore regarding her surname change?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The conflict between tradition and modernity\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The importance of social status\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The balance between tradition and individuality\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the speaker view the act of adopting her husband’s surname over time?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$As a complete loss of her identity\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$As a fusion of their stories\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$As an unnecessary change\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the author express about her roots and her new legacy?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$She cherishes her roots while building a new legacy.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$She has completely abandoned her roots.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$She feels conflicted and unhappy about both.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “At first, I was excited about the unity it represented”\nThe speaker mentions feeling excited about the unity that taking her husband’s surname represented.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: is incorrect, as the speaker feels strongly about the change.\n- **C**: is also incorrect, as the speaker seems just reflective and nostalgic, not regretful about the name change.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “My maiden name held my family’s history and my own identity”\n\n- The text states that her maiden name held her family’s history and her own identity\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C\n\n- **B**: is incorrect, because it misrepresents the author's connection to her maiden name; it is tied to her own family, not her husband's.\n- **C**: is also incorrect, as the text specifically connects her maiden name to her family’s history rather than framing it as merely a new beginning.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Adopting his surname made me reflect on the balance between tradition and individuality”\n\n- The speaker reflects on the balance between tradition and individuality in the context of changing her surname.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B\n\n- **A**: is incorrect, because the text does not focus on modernity but rather on balancing tradition with individuality.\n- **B**: is also incorrect, as the text does not address social status as a theme.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Over time, she comes to see adopting his surname as a fusion of their stories.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C\n\n- **A**: is incorrect,  because while she initially felt some loss, she ultimately views it positively as a fusion of their stories.\n- **C**: is also incorrect, as it contradicts the author's reflective and positive perspective on adopting her husband's surname.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “It’s a reminder that while I cherish my roots, I’m also building a new legacy together”\n\n- The author expresses that she cherishes her roots but is also building a new legacy together with her husband.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C\n\n- **B**: is incorrect, because the author explicitly states that she cherishes her roots even while embracing a new legacy.\n- **C**: is also incorrect, as while she experiences some conflict, she ultimately finds value in both aspects of her identity."},{"ref":"EB0094","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0094\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a young teenager describing her experience of being bilingual.**\n\n:audio{#58206469-2184-454d-9c52-33923fc5ee55 aid=\"58206469-2184-454d-9c52-33923fc5ee55\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.** [5 marks]\n\nSpeaking English and Spanish is like having two homes: the English one is to **[-1-]** my dreams, while the Spanish one lets me appreciate our **[-2-]** culture. Oftentimes, it feels like this gift means **[-3-]** two identities, but it's this **[-4-]** of two languages that makes me feel alive, as being. lingual does truly **[-5-]** my life .","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{chase}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker expresses that using English allows her to pursue her aspirations and goals. The word *chasing* conveys an active engagement with her dreams, highlighting the dynamic nature of his identity as he navigates both languages.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{vibrant}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The word *vibrant* describes the rich and lively traditions that Spanish connects her to. This emphasizes the cultural significance of her  heritage and how it shapes her identity, reinforcing the idea that language is tied to personal and familial connections.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{juggling}$   [1 mark]\n\n-  The term *juggling* illustrates the complexity of managing two identities simultaneously. It suggests a balance between different aspects of her life, reflecting the challenges and joys of being bilingual in a multicultural environment.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{blend}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The word *blend* signifies the harmonious integration of her identities as she navigates both languages. This term captures the essence of her experience, suggesting that being bilingual creates a unique and cohesive sense of self.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{enrich}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The verb *enrich* indicates that each language adds depth and value to her life experiences. This highlights the positive impact of bilingualism on her personal growth and understanding of the world around her."},{"ref":"EB0095","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0095\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about a family custom connected with naming sons.**\n\n:audio{#0bd33cf6-0140-4ece-8371-9223564a36fa aid=\"0bd33cf6-0140-4ece-8371-9223564a36fa\"}\n\n1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the five true statements.** [5 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker’s grandfather, father, and brother are all named Richard.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Grandpa Richard was a kind and humorous person.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Richard Jr. is often stressed about work.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Richard III is hardworking.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker is the only child in their family.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The narrator felt lost and not unique growing up with so many Richards.\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The narrator’s family has a strong tradition of naming their sons Richard.\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$People aren’t surprised when they find out the speaker’s name is Richard.","markScheme":"1 - 5.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers 1 to 5 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, C, F, G}$   [5 marks]\n\n- **A**. The text explicitly states the names of the three generations of Richards.\n\n- **B**. Grandpa Richard is literally described as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the kindest soul”\n\n- **C**. Richard Jr. is characterized as a workaholic who is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$always stressed about deadlines.\"\n\n- **F**. The narrator expresses feelings of being lost and not unique due to the shared name.\n\n- **G**. as the consistent naming pattern suggests a strong family tradition.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: D, E, H\n\n- **D**.  Richard III is compared to a sloth, indicating laziness.\n\n- **E**. The text mentions a little brother, Richard III, indicating that the narrator is not an only child.\n\n- **H**. as the text mentions people are surprised by his name when meeting him."},{"ref":"EB0371","marks":"6","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0371\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\nDear Tom,\n\nYou will not believe what I just did! You know I'm a Scrabble fanatic.  I've been playing for years here in our London Scrabble club!  But last weekend, I took part in a French Scrabble tournament in Lyon. You know I do not know French!! But four years of Latin at school gave me enough confidence to sign up - and it was amazing!\n\nThe competition itself was both terrifying and thrilling. My opponents were friendly and very confident as they quickly placed tiles to form words I could not pronounce!  I placed my own tiles relying entirely on my memory of Latin roots and the patterns I know that make up so many words in multiple languages. Needless to say, I lost every round I played!\n\nIn the end, what was really remarkable was how little spoken language mattered once the game began. Everyone understood the game - the rules and strategy - and it turns out the French give the same little smile when their opponent plays a great word as we do in London.  \n\nI lost every game - and I can't wait to go back for the next competition!  Do you want to come with me?\n\nYours, James\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True or False, then justify it using words as they appear in the text. Both parts are required for [1 mark].** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James is an experienced Scrabble player.    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James speaks French fluently.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James studied Latin for four years.    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James found the experience of competing both exciting and frightening.    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James communicated with his opponents in French during the games.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$James is hoping to return to Lyon for another tournament.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I've been playing for years\" \\\n\nJames tells his friend that he has been a member of a London Scrabble club $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$for years”, which means a significant (long) period of time.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I do not know French\" \\\n\nJames admits that he does not know French, neither writing, nor speaking.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$four years of Latin at school”\\\n\nJames states clearly that he studied Latin for four years before entering the tournament.  He believes his knowledge of Latin gives him a basic understanding of languages which stem from Latin, like French.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The competition itself was both terrifying and thrilling\" \\\n\nJames describes the experience using two contrasting emotions, one negative and one positive. Terrifying and frightening are synonyms and so are thrilling and exciting.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$how little spoken language mattered\" \\\n\nJames indicates that spoken language was not important during the games, because everyone knew the rules and how to play. And, when communicating with the other player, a smile worked perfectly - there was no need to speak at all.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$    [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I can't wait to go back for the next competition\" \\\n\nJames expresses a clear desire to return to Lyon for another French Scrabble tournament."},{"ref":"EB0372","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0372\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**Lost in Translation...**\n\nIf you’ve been following my posts, you’ll know I’m always looking for ways to relax after school. A few weeks ago, I thought I’d found the perfect solution: joining the local knitting club. Peaceful. No Stress. Right?'\n\nWrong.\n\nWithin five minutes, everyone around me was “frogging their WIPs” and debating how to show off a FO. I smiled and nodded for two hours, trying to keep my yarn untangled while listening to the conversation and in the end - I understood absolutely nothing. I went home feeling more stressed than before!\n\nSo I did what any self-respecting teenager would do - I went online and looked up ‘knitting jargon’. Turns out “FO” means “finished object” and “frogging” means unravelling your work to start again? And WIP? Work in progress, of course!\n\nLast week I went back, armed with needles and new words. And you know what? It felt completely different. Suddenly, I was part of the conversation - when my new friends gave me help, I could understand AND do cool new things! I love knitting!\n\nHave you ever cracked the code of a new hobby? Tell me below!\n\n#JargonLife #KnittingClub\n\n**Answer the following questions.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What was the writer hoping to find after school?    [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What were the club members debating how to show off?    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did the writer feel when she left the club?    [1 mark]\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in paragraphs 3 and 4.** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Determined to understand what the knitters were talking about, the writer turned to the internet to research...    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The process of undoing knitted work to begin again is called...    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$On her return visit, fellow club members offered the writer...    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$With new knowledge, the writer could not only follow the discussion but also...    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{ways to relax}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “I'm always looking for ways to relax after school”\\\n\nThe writer was searching for activities that would help her unwind after school.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a FO}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “debating how to show off a FO”\\\n\nThe writer heard club members discussing a term she did not yet understand - a FO, which she later discovered means a finished object.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{more stressed than before}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “I went home feeling more stressed than before”\\\n\nThe knitting club had the opposite effect to what the writer had hoped - rather than relaxing her, the experience increased her stress levels.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{knitting jargon}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “I went online and looked up $`$knitting jargon'”\\\n\n*Jargon* is the specialised vocabulary used within a particular community. The writer needed to research this independently as no one at the club had explained the terms to her.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{frogging}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “frogging means unravelling your work to start again”\\\n\nThis term is specific to the knitting community.  It describes undoing stitches to correct a mistake, something the writer had never seen or heard of before joining the club.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{help}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “when my new friends gave me help”\\\n\nOn her return visit, fellow club members supported the writer. This help, combined with her new understanding of the vocabulary, allowed her to participate fully for the first time.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{do cool new things}$    [1 mark]\n\n- “I could understand AND do cool new things”\\\n\nUnderstanding the jargon transformed the writer's experience. She went from confused outsider to engaged participant, able to both follow and take part in club activities."},{"ref":"EB0373","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0373\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**The Last Whistlers**\n\nHey guys! If you've been following my posts, you'll know I've been spending a lot of time with my grandmother lately. What you may not know is that my grandmother is one of the last people in our village who still speaks our $\\underline{\\textrm{traditional}}$ whistled language. I think she can only $`$talk' to three or four neighbors now and she's worried about it completely disappearing - so I asked her to tell me more about it.\n\nShe said it was once $\\underline{\\textrm{vital}}$ to daily life in the $\\underline{\\textrm{steep}}$ mountain valleys around our village.  People communicated with each other from very far away - and a simple whistled melody could contain a lot of information. Farmer's wives could tell $\\underline{\\textrm{distant}}$ husbands that sheep were missing, and mothers could call children to dinner from kilometers away! Each village had their own language and proudly passed it down from generation to generation, which created a really strong sense of community.\n\nBut here's the really exciting part - a new project has been started to document and teach $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$ to younger villagers. And $\\underline{\\textrm{we}}$'re both going to take part!! Isn't that amazing?\n\nDo you have family stories about languages? Leave a comment! Love ya! \\\n\nStela\n\n#OurLanguage #CommunityRoots \n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.**[4 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$traditional |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$indigenous |\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$vital |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$quiet |\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$steep |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$tired |\n\n|4.$\\hspace{1em}$distant |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$internal |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$remote |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$sharp |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$G.$\\hspace{1em}$hereditary |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$H.$\\hspace{1em}$essential |\n\n**To whom or to what do the underlined words refer? Answer using words as they appear in the text.** \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... teach $\\underline{\\textrm{it}}$ to younger villagers.    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... $\\underline{\\textrm{we}}$'re both going to take part!!    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$our traditional whistled language\"\\\n\n*Indigenous* means something that belongs to a community. It is a good replacement word for *traditional*, as it means that the language has been part of the community for a long time.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option G** is the distractor. *Hereditary* refers to something passed down through generations, but it is usually associated with genetics.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{H}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it was once vital to daily life\" \\\n\n*Vital* describes something that is absolutely necessary. *Essential* has the same meaning - something that cannot be done without.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option D** does not work here. Although *internal* can mean the same thing as *vital* in some uses (vital organs, for example), the text uses the word *vital* to show the importance of language to daily life.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the steep mountain valleys around our village\" \\\n\n*Steep* describes land that rises or drops at a sharp angle. *Sharp* has the same meaning when describing a sudden change in height or direction.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option B** is the distractor. Although mountain valleys might seem *quiet*, the text describes a different physical aspect of the area - its steepness - and does not tell us if it is also quiet.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Farmer's wives could tell distant husbands that sheep were missing\" \\\n\n*Distant* describes someone or something that is far away. *Remote* is a close synonym, showing that they were located a long way from their homes.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C** does not work here. Although farmers working in mountain valleys might be *tired*, the text says nothing about how the husbands felt, only about how far away they were.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the (traditional whistled) language}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a new project has been started to document and teach it to younger villagers\" \\\n\nThe pronoun *it* refers back to the whistled language mentioned throughout the blog post.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{the blogger and her grandmother}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$And we're both going to take part!!\" \\\n\n*We* refers to the two people discussed in the final paragraph, the blogger and her grandmother, who have both joined the project."},{"ref":"EB0407","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0407\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**You are going to hear a radio feature about changing attitudes to accents at the BBC.**\n\n:audio{#d5635ed3-a5b3-4d87-abd1-3a99cbd688d0 aid=\"d5635ed3-a5b3-4d87-abd1-3a99cbd688d0\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.** [5 marks]\n\n**Broadcast Notes** \n\nOur presenter has personal experience of this story, having grown up in the **[-1-]**. Starting out in the industry **[-2-]**, they were encouraged to change the way their accent sounded. The BBC had long required presenters to adopt a standardised accent, widely regarded as **[-3-]**. Any presenter whose accent fell outside this standard was considered **[-4-]**. In recent years, the BBC has taken steps to bring in voices from a wider range of **[-5-]**. For many listeners, hearing a familiar accent on national broadcasts has meant feeling that their **[-6-]** is valued and recognised.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{north of England}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I grew up in a small town in the north of England\" \\\n\nThe presenter tells us directly where they grew up, establishing their personal connection to the story.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{twenty years ago}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$when I got my first job in broadcasting twenty years ago\" \\\n\nThe presenter gives a specific timeframe for when they began their career in broadcasting. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{neutral}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a single, formal accent — clear, southern, and considered $`$neutral'\" \\\n\nThe traditional BBC accent was regarded as the standard or *neutral* way of speaking, against which all other accents were compared.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{unprofessional}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$anything that sounded different was seen as unprofessional\" \\\n\nPresenters with regional accents were considered to lack the professional standard required by the BBC.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{regions and backgrounds}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the BBC has made great effort to include presenters from different regions and backgrounds\" \\\n\nThe BBC has actively worked to ensure that presenters reflect the diversity of the country, both in terms of where they come from and their personal backgrounds.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{identity}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hearing their own accent on national broadcasts makes them feel that their identity is finally being acknowledged\" \\\n\nWhen listeners hear an accent like their own on national radio or television, it gives them a sense that who they are - their *identity* - is recognised and valued."},{"ref":"EB0408","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0408\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a student talking about a recent holiday.**\n\n:audio{#a938ba9c-51e1-4576-8c8c-74687778556e aid=\"a938ba9c-51e1-4576-8c8c-74687778556e\"}\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Pencarrow Cove is a small coastal village. \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The student visited Pencarrow Cove the previous month. \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Families in the village share one of four surnames.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The Pendrys arrived in the village in the 1900s. \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The Trevean family owns the boatyard. \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The boatyard has been owned by the same family for around two hundred years.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The student's grandmother greeted someone in the bakery.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The Hockings are bakers. \n\n**Answer the following question.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How long have the Smyths lived in Pencarrow Cove?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1 - 4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, E, G, H}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a tiny fishing village on the coast of Cornwall\" \\\n\nPencarrow Cove is described as tiny, which means it is a *small* village, and it is on the coast, making it a *coastal* village.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the boatyard has belonged to the Trevean family for around four hundred years\" \\\n\nThe text confirms that the boatyard is owned by and has belonged to the Trevean family for a very long time.\n\n- **G**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$my grandmother waved at the woman in the bakery, Mrs. Hocking\" \\\n\nThe speaker's grandmother recognised Mrs. Hocking in the bakery and waved at her.  To greet in this context means to acknowledge.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Her family... has been baking the village bread ever since\" \\\n\nThe Hocking family has been baking bread for the village for a long time. They are *bakers*.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C, D, F\n\n- **B**: The speaker says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$last week,\" not the previous month. \n\n- **C**: The text says three family names, not four.\n\n- **D**: The text says the Pendrys came $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in the 1800s,\" not the 1900s. \n\n- **F**: The text says the Treveans have owned the boatyard for around four hundred years, not two hundred.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{fifty years}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$we Smyths have only been here fifty years\" \\\n\nThe grandfather confirms that the Smyth family has lived in Pencarrow Cove for fifty years, a very short time compared to the other village families."}]},{"name":"Lifestyles","questions":[{"ref":"EB0001","marks":"5","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0001\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Virtual influencers: meet the AI-generated figures posing as your new online friends}}$\n\nVirtual influencers are transforming the way content is created, consumed, and marketed online. They represent an electrifying dance between cutting-edge technology and our desire for connection. Recent advances in technology have thrust these digital personalities into the spotlight, with examples like the virtual Japanese popstar Kyoko Date dating back to 1996. Created by 3D artists using CGI and AI tools, virtual influencers can be tailored to align with specific target audiences.\n\nTheir popularity has prompted marketing agencies to embrace them as a cost-effective promotional strategy. Unlike real influencers who demand exorbitant fees, virtual influencers like Lil Miquela charge much less, making them an attractive option for brands. However, they also raise concerns about transparency. As virtual influencers become more human-like, distinguishing them from real people becomes increasingly difficult, particularly in advertising.\n\nThis ambiguity highlights the need for clear guidelines regarding the use and disclosure of such content. Countries like India are already addressing this issue by mandating that influencers, including virtual ones, disclose promotional content. The rise of virtual influencers offers both opportunities and challenges for the future of digital marketing and content creation.\n\n[Nguyen, M. (2023, September 19). *Virtual influencers: Meet the AI-generated figures posing as your new online friends - as they try to sell you stuff*. Griffith University. The Conversation. https://www.theconversation.com/virtual-influencers-meet-the-ai-generated-figures-posing-as-your-new-online-friends-as-they-try-to-sell-you-stuff-212001. Accessed October 22, 2024.]\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What are virtual influencers primarily designed to do?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Promote human connection through shared experiences.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Generate profits through marketing strategies.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Encourage healthy lifestyles among users.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Provide emotional support to followers.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$blurred realities\" imply in the context of the text?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Virtual influencers are indistinguishable from real influencers.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The distinction between virtual and human influencers is becoming unclear.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$All influencers are now created digitally.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The online world looks more authentic than the real world.\n\n**Find the word or phrase in the text that means the following.**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$imitating    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$customized    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$enforcing    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text discusses how virtual influencers are used by marketing agencies as a promotional strategy, highlighting their role in generating revenue for brands.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **A**: While virtual influencers may aim to connect with audiences, their primary function in the text is tied to marketing.\n\n- **C**: The text does not discuss health or lifestyles but focuses on marketing and engagement.\n\n- **D**: The text indicates that virtual influencers may struggle to connect in a human way, suggesting they do not offer genuine emotional support.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The phrase *blurred realities* indicates that as virtual influencers become more human-like, it becomes increasingly difficult to tell them apart from real people.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **A**: While this option seems close, it oversimplifies the context by implying total indistinguishability rather than a gradual blurring.\n\n- **C**: The text  specifically discusses virtual influencers but does not imply all influencers are virtual\n\n- **D**: The text does not suggest that the online world is more authentic but rather highlights the complexities of authenticity in digital marketing.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{posing}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Posing* suggests these influencers are imitating or representing themselves as friends.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{tailored}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Both *customized* and *tailored* convey the idea of something that has been created to target a specific audience.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{mandating}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Both words imply that the Indian government is making it obligatory for influencers to inform the audience of promotional content."},{"ref":"EB0002","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0002\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Is social media making you unhappy? The answer is not so simple}}$\n\nSocial media is a big part of many people's lives, but does it make us happier? Studies show the answer is not so clear. Some people feel worse when they spend a lot of time online, especially if they compare themselves to others. This can lead to feelings of envy and sadness. But the cause might not be social media itself. Instead, how you feel about using it matters.\n\nIf you feel like you are using social media in a way that works for you, it can be a good experience. For example, young people often use social media to stay connected with friends and express themselves. This can make them feel more positive.\n\nOn the other hand, if you feel like social media is controlling you, it can lead to negative feelings. It's important to remember that social media is just one part of your online life. It's best to use it in a balanced way and stay mindful of what you see and how it makes you feel. If social media starts making you unhappy, it might be time to change how you use it.\n\n[Humphries, M., & Mitchell, L. (2024, June 25). Is social media making you unhappy? The answer is not so simple. University of Adelaide. The Conversation. https://www.theconversation.com/is-social-media-making-you-unhappy-the-answer-is-not-so-simple-232490. Accessed October 23, 2024.]\n\n1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the four true statements.**[4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Social media use can subtly shape users' perceptions of success and happiness over time.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$People often feel that their online presence enhances their self-worth.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Many young users view social media as a tool for personal growth and identity exploration.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Users may feel a sense of emptiness despite high engagement with social media platforms.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Social media is primarily a source of entertainment and does not impact emotional health.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Being aware of how social media impacts you can help maintain emotional balance.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$Users frequently report that social media allows them to escape their daily stressors.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Many users find that limiting their social media use does not improve their emotional state.\n\n**Choose an appropriate word from the text that completes each gap in the following sentences.**\n\nTo avoid emotional distress, it's important to remain **[-5-]** of how social media content affects you.    [1 mark]\n\nWhen users feel in control of their social media use, they are more likely to experience **[-6-]** emotions. [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 4.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers 1 to 4 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, D, F}$\n\n- **A**. The text implies that frequent exposure to curated online content can influence how individuals define their own success and happiness. [1 mark]\n\n- **C**. While not explicitly stated, the text implies that social media serves as a platform for self-discovery and expression, especially among young people. [1 mark]\n\n- **D.** The text hints that increased interaction can lead to feelings of dissatisfaction, suggesting a disconnect between engagement and emotional fulfilment. [1 mark]\n\n- **F**. The text highlights the importance of being mindful of social media's effect on emotions, suggesting that mindfulness can improve ones mental state.\n\n    *Incorrect options*: B, E, G, and H.\n\n- **B**. This is incorrect because the text indicates that many users may feel worse when comparing themselves to others rather than feeling empowered.\n\n- **E**. This overlooks the text's indication that social media significantly affects emotional well-being, as it highlights both positive and negative influences.\n\n- **G**. The text suggests that while social media can provide an escape, it also has the potential to create negative feelings, leading to a more complex relationship with emotional well-being.\n\n- **H**. While some users might have varying experiences, the text suggests that changing how one uses social media can lead to a more positive emotional experience, making this statement potentially misleading.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Mindful }$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text advises users to be mindful of what they see and how it affects them emotionally. Being aware or conscious of this impact is key to maintaining emotional balance.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Positive}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text discusses how, when people use social media in a way that works for them, they tend to feel more positive, rather than negative, emotions."},{"ref":"EB0003","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0003\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Rushing or delaying decisions is linked to anxiety and depression in young people}}$\n\nEvery day, young people make  $\\underline{\\textrm{countless}}$ decisions, from what to wear to choosing their future careers. However, how they make these decisions can significantly impact their mental health. Research indicates that decision-making styles contribute to anxiety and depression among young adults.\n\nIn a study involving 1,411 young South Africans aged 15-25, findings revealed that those who adopt a $\\underline{\\textrm{vigilant}}$ decision-making style—evaluating options thoroughly—tend to experience lower levels of anxiety and depression. Conversely, those who rush their decisions or delay making them are at higher risk for mental health issues. Alarmingly, 37.3% of participants were at $\\underline{\\textrm{risk}}$ for major depressive disorder, while 74.2% faced anxiety disorders.\n\nTo support better mental health, young people are encouraged to take their time when making decisions. This can involve identifying the issue, brainstorming solutions, weighing pros and cons, and consulting trusted individuals. By $\\underline{\\textrm{adopting}}$ a careful approach, they can enhance their emotional well-being and improve decision outcomes.\n\n[Davids, E. L. (2024, October 8). Rushing or delaying decisions is linked to anxiety and depression in young people - study. University of Pretoria. The Conversation. https://www.theconversation.com/rushing-or-delaying-decisions-is-linked-to-anxiety-and-depression-in-young-people-study-2024]\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the findings, what is the main potential consequence of delaying decisions?     [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What term describes the negative mental health outcomes associated with poor decision-making?     [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do young people risk by delaying their decisions?    [1 mark]\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [4 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|4.$\\hspace{1em}$countless|$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$utilising|\n\n|5.$\\hspace{1em}$vigilant|$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$numerous|\n\n|6.$\\hspace{1em}$risk    |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$danger|\n\n|7.$\\hspace{1em}$adopting|$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$watchful|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$opportunity|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$harm|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$G.$\\hspace{1em}$possibility|\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$H.$\\hspace{1em}$managing|","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Anxiety disorders}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text highlights in the fourth paragraph that depressive disorders and anxiety disorders are both risk factors, however, at 74.2%, the anxiety factor is the predominant consequence.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Issues / Mental health issues}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text indicates in the third paragraph that rushing decisions or delaying them can lead to higher risks of anxiety and depression, collectively referred to as mental health issues.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Anxiety or Depression}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The first paragraph of the text indicates that delaying decisions can contribute to higher risks of mental health issues, specifically mentioning that those who rush or delay decisions are more likely to experience anxiety and depression.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Countless* means too many to be counted, which aligns with the definition of *numerous*.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Watchful* conveys a similar sense of attentiveness and careful observation as *vigilant*, indicating someone who is alert to potential issues or dangers.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{G}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Risk* refers to the potential for harm or negative outcomes, which is synonymous with *possibility* in this context.\n\n- **Options F and C**, *danger* and *harm*, could be confusing here as they are related to risk, but their meaning is fundamentally different. (*Danger* poses an immediate risk. *Harm* refers to actual damage)\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The term *utilising* conveys a similar meaning to *adopting*, emphasising the practical application of a method or strategy.\n\n- **Option H**, *managing*, could be confusing here, however, its meaning is more related to handling, instead of putting something into use.\n\n- **Option E**, *possibility*, is not a good match for any of the words."},{"ref":"EB0109","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0109\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear somebody criticizing the phenomenon of  ‘kidulting’- adults behaving like a child.**\n\n:audio{#69b025df-075c-49b1-a11e-7ea38a8ffa1b aid=\"69b025df-075c-49b1-a11e-7ea38a8ffa1b\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker seem to find most problematic about \"kidults\"? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Their love of cartoon-themed merchandise\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Their reluctance to embrace adult responsibilities\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Their preference for fancy coffee\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The phrase \"cartoon pajamas\" suggests that “kidults”: [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Watch a lot of cartoons in their pajamas\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Prefer childish clothing and accessories\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Are professional cartoon pajamas designers\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The word \"dignity\" in the text suggests that the speaker believes adults should: [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Always dress formally\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Take life seriously\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Never enjoy playful things.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker imply about previous generations? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They were more mature\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They didn't enjoy fun activities\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They never collected figurines\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The speaker's tone can best be described as: [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Angry and aggressive\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Nostalgic and sentimental\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Mildly frustrated and humorous","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- This matches the text's central criticism about avoiding responsibilities (eg $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Where's the dignity? ... Where's the responsibility?\")\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: is incorrect, as it is too specific and narrow.\n- **C**: is a minor detail, not the main critique.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text implies “kidults” choose childish clothing and accessories. The speaker mentions “cartoon pajamas” as well as “a Pikachu backpack.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- Both **Option A** and **C** are incorrect, as the text does not mention watching cartoons in pyjamas nor does it talk about professional cartoons design.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The speaker implies adults should approach life more seriously, the way they did “in their (my) time.” The speaker mentions “ In my time, we became adults at 18 and stayed that way.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **A**: is too narrow an interpretation.\n- **C**: contradicts the speaker's overall message about maturity.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text suggests previous generations matured faster,  as the speaker mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$In my time, we became adults at 18 and stayed that way\"\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **B**: is incorrect as the text doesn't suggest previous generations were joyless.\n- **C**: is not addressed in the passage.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text uses humorous, exaggerated language showing mild frustration with phrases like $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Good grief\" and “Kids these days, I tell you!​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​​” and an overall exclamatory tone.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **A**: is not correct as it suggests too much anger.\n- **B**: is also wrong, as it implies sentimentality not present in the text."},{"ref":"EB0110","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0110\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on to-do-lists enthusiasts**\n\n:audio{#1f39dffd-14a5-4ebb-883f-613f044ae8db aid=\"1f39dffd-14a5-4ebb-883f-613f044ae8db\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions:**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ What do to-do list enthusiasts like breaking into small, easy steps?     [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How do list makers describe big tasks broken into smaller ones?    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What feeling do list makers get when they complete a task?    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do the writers compare list makers' pens to?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How do list makers deal with stress and messiness?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{their notebook (s) }$   [1 mark]\n\n-   “Their notebooks are full of tasks, each one waiting to be ticked off.”\n\n- The text points to notebooks as the place where to-do-lists enthusiasts tick off tasks.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{big jobs}$   [1 mark]\n\n-   “These list lovers break big jobs into small, easy steps\"\n\n- Complicated tasks, big tasks that are broken into smaller ones are called “big jobs” in the text.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{feel like winners}$ or $\\textrm{feel like true winners}$ or $\\textrm{feel winners}$ [1 mark]\n\n-   $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$When they complete something, they feel like true winners\"\n\n- The act of breaking big tasks into smaller ones brings the users a feeling of achievement, closure, victory.\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{magic wands}$ [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Their pens are like magic wands\"\n\n- The text compares pens to magical instruments- wands.\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{smart planning}$ [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$These list makers are like heroes who beat messiness and stress with their smart planning\"\n\n- The text points to smart planning as a way of dealing with stress and messiness."},{"ref":"EB0111","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0111\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about “scrooging”- the\nphenomenon of ending a relationship to save money on presents.**\n\n:audio{#7ba04736-768a-493e-8757-35e8268296c2 aid=\"7ba04736-768a-493e-8757-35e8268296c2\"}\n\n1 - 5.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose 5 correct statements.** [5 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker suggests that modern dating is not cheap.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Scrooging\" involves breaking up to save money on gifts.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The text implies that all temporary relationships should end before gift-giving seasons.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The speaker believes breaking up before Christmas is a form of financial self-defense.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$A Christmas present always costs hundreds of dollars.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$The text portrays breaking up as purely an economic decision.\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The author suggests that “scrooging” is a form of smart relationship management.\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$The text argues that breaking up before gift-giving is emotionally cruel.","markScheme":"1 - 5.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers 1 to 5 can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, D, F, G}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **A**. is true, as in the first sentence it is stated directly that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$modern dating is expensive\"\n\n- **B**. is true, as the fragment  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Scrooging\" is simply financial self-defense- Why invest in an elaborate gift when the relationship might be temporary?” captures the essence of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$scrooging”- breaking up to save on gifts.\n\n- **D**. is true, as the fragment $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Breaking up strategically before gift-giving seasons is pure economic genius\" highlights the economic motivation.\n\n- **F**. is true,  as the fragment  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$split-ups are less about heartbreak and more about smart budgeting\" emphasizes economic reasoning over emotional considerations.\n\n- **G**. is true, as the fragment $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$financially responsible relationship management\" presents \"scrooging\" as a rational strategy.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: C, E, H\n\n- **C**. is false, as it overgeneralises the text’s argument and no text evidence suggests ALL temporary relationships should end.\n\n- **E**. is false, as the text does not specify an exact cost, it just says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a Christmas present can cost hundreds\", so describes a possibility only.\n\n- **H**. is false, as the text explicitly argues against the idea of being emotionally cruel, especially in the fragment  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It's not cruel - it's calculated self-preservation\"."},{"ref":"EB0275","marks":"6","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0275\n\n**Maximum mark: 6**\n\n**Finding Balance Between Work and Life**\n\nI used to think that working whenever I could was the key to career progress. I worked late in the office, read e-mail during dinner, and stayed up to catch up on some unfinished tasks. I always thought I would be able to get some sleep later. But later never came, and I ended up burnt out and alienated from friends and family.\n\nI found myself missing important moments: birthdays, weekends, even a fun dinner-table conversation. And that's when I made up my mind: I needed to change. I started setting firm boundaries, such as cutting off work at 6 p.m. and leaving my phone away from the dinner table. It was strange, at first, but gradually I could feel a change.\n\nWith more time off, I started meeting friends for coffee. I slept better, laughed harder, and even enjoyed working less.\n\nI've learned that work-life balance is as easy as making space for life outside of work. For me, it means valuing my time, saying no when necessary, and remembering that success is about happiness and health. My life is better now, not because I'm working fewer hours, but because I'm living more.\n\n**Choose the correct option.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What belief motivated the writer’s work routine?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$That working at any time would help their career progress.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$That time with family was more important than work.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$That resting regularly was essential for success.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$That avoiding emails during dinner would improve productivity.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What was the main problem the writer found before changing their routine?     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They didn't have enough work to do.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They felt exhausted and disconnected from loved ones.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They wanted to spend more time in the office.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$They disliked their job.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What did the writer do to improve their work-life balance?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$They change their work.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$They set themselves limits.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$They felt strange.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$They enjoyed time with their family.\n\n**Find the word or phrase in paragraphs 3 and 4 which means the following:**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$free time    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$appreciating    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$keeping in mind    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I used to think that working whenever I could was the key to career progress.”\\\n\nThe phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$working whenever” means the same as working at any time, which suggests being available to work as much as possible. Similarly the phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the key to career progress” refers to something that would help  someone's career progress.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C, D\n\n- **Option B** is incorrect as, according to the text, the writer neglected time with their family in favour of work. They mention becoming $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$alienated from friends and family”, which shows they did not prioritize them. \n\n- **C**: According to the text, the writer sacrificed rest to keep working. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I always thought I would be able to get some sleep later.” This shows they did not value rest as essential for success.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect as the text says the opposite, the writer did read emails during dinner.  $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I worked late in the office, read e-mail during dinner, ...” as they believed constant work was the key to success.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$ended up burnt out, and alienated from friends and family.”\\\n\nThe adjective *burnt out* means constantly tired, which explains why the writer feels exhausted, while the adjective *alienated*, suggesting isolation, explains why the writer feels *disconnected* from friends and family.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **A**: The writer explains that they, in fact, had excess work: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I worked late in the office, reading e-mail during dinner, and at other times stayed up to catch up on some unfinished task.”\n\n- **Option C** is incorrect, as in fact, the writer already spent *too much* time in the office, and this was one, among others, in the list of problems. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I worked late in the office”\n\n- ***Option D** is not possible. There is no evidence in the text that the writer disliked their job. The main concern is the excess of work, not that he did not like it.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I started setting firm boundaries”\\\n\n*Limits* and *boundaries* are synonyms. The writer mentions they began to set boundaries, like leaving work earlier.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C, D\n\n- **Option A** is incorrect because the writer does not mention changing their job, but making changes in their life.\n\n- **C**: The writer mentions feeling strange, but this is not an action they took to improve their work-life balance.\n\n- **Option D** is incorrect, as the writer mentions that they had missed time with family, but the action they took was $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$setting boundaries” so they could spend more time with their family.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{time off}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$With more time off, I started meeting friends for coffee.”\\\n\n*Time off* and *free time* refer to time when the writer was not working, was free from work responsibilities. \n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{valuing}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it means valuing my time”\\\n\n*Appreciating* is a good replacement word for *valuing*, as it means to give importance to something, to value it.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{remembering}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$remembering that success is about happiness and health”\\\n\n*Keeping in mind* and *remembering* both imply staying aware of something important, not forgetting."},{"ref":"EB0276","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0276\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**Cycling: Not Just a Hobby**\n\nFor many people, cycling is not something you do on the weekend; it's a way of life. I discovered this a few years ago when a friend invited me out to ride with a local cycling club. At the beginning, it was simply a way to get some exercise and enjoy the outdoors. But the longer I rode, the more it began to affect my health and social circle.\n\nCycling has several benefits. Physically, it makes me fit and agile. Mentally, it provides a feeling of freedom and allows me to let go of the tension of a long day. Cycling out in the open with nothing but the sound of the wind relaxes me.\n\nIt also introduced me to people with the same interests who enjoy the same hobby. We meet early on weekends, plan far-off tours, and encourage each other to perform better. I also learned about bike repair, safety on the road, and interesting cycling routes in my country.\n\nWhat began as a hobby has become a way of life that fills my time and helps me feel energized. Cycling is not something I do—it's who I am.\n\n**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer has been practicing this activity for a long time.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer joined a local cycling club because he wanted something to do on the weekends. \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Cycling became the writer's passion from the very beginning.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$The writer felt riding had a negative impact on their life.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Cycling helps the writer stay in good physical shape.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Cycling helps the writer relax after a hard day.\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in the text.** [2 marks]\n\nThe writer mentions that, apart from meeting new people, they also learned about bike maintenance, **[-5-]** and routes.\n\nThe writer says that what once started as a hobby has now turned into a lifestyle that occupies their days and leaves them feeling **[-6-]**.","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, E, F}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I discovered this a few years ago”\\\n\nThe writer mentions they have been cycling for some years, which is a long time.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it makes me fit and agile”\\\n\nThe writer mentions that one of the advantages of cycling is being in good shape (fit and agile).\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$allows me to let go of the tension of a long day.”\\\n\nThe writer says that one of the advantages of cycling is that it helps them *relax* (let go of the tension).\n\n- *Incorrect options:*   B, C, D\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a friend invited me”\\\n\nThe writer mentions they joined the club becasue they were invited by a friend. They also say $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$cycling is not something you do on the weekend” which means it is not just a weekend hobby.\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$At the beginning, it was simply a way to get some exercise and enjoy the outdoors.”\\\n\nThe writer says that at first, it was a way to stay active and spend time outside.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Cycling has several benefits”\\\n\nThe writer mentions there are benefits or advantages to cycling, which is the opposite of what the sentence says (a *negative impact*)\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{safety}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: safety on the road\\\n\n\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I also learned about bike repair, safety on the road”\\\n\nThe writer explains that cycling taught him different things, one of which is safety on the road.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{energized}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$helps me feel energized”\\\n\nThe writer mentions that cycling, which was once a hobby, has now become a way of life that helps them feel *energized* (full of energy)."},{"ref":"EB0277","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0277\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\nGood morning everyone,\n\nToday I want to talk about something that affects us all: how we live and how it impacts our world. Sustainable living is not impossible; it can become a  lifestyle that fights to reduce harm to the environment while improving our health.\n\nWhen I started to live more sustainably, I found it wasn't about making huge $\\underline{\\textrm{sacrifices}}$. I began to use $\\underline{\\textrm{reusable}}$ bags, cut down on plastics, and walk or take the bus instead of driving everywhere. At first, these small adjustments felt like small adjustments, but together, they had a big $\\underline{\\textrm{impact}}$.\n\nSustainable living is also about thinking about what we buy: where it comes from, how it is made, and if it'll last. I started to buy from local businesses and farmers, which not only helps the world but also contributes to my community.\n\nThis lifestyle choice has given me a sense of purpose. I feel more connected to the planet and more hopeful for the future.\n\nI am not a perfectionist, and you don't have to be, either. But if we all make tiny, conscious steps, we can create a way of life that respects the earth and is healthy for all. Thank you for listening!\n\n**What do the following words mean in the context of the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$sacrifices  |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$arrangements |\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$reusable |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$compromises |\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$impact |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$repercussion |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$effect |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$steps |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$changes|\n\n**Answer the following questions.** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did this change of lifestyle make the writer feel about the future?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the speaker encourage everyone to do?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Sacrifices* and *compromises* are similar in meaning; they both refer to giving up something valuable for the sake of another goal.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option F**, *changes*, could fit Question 1. However, *changes* is a broad term and does not imply that something has been given up or sacrificed.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{E}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Steps*  is a good replacement word for *adjustments* because it focuses on small actions toward a goal.\n\n\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option A** is incorrect. *Arrangements* does not describe personal habit shifts or lifestyle decisions.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Effect* is a good replacement word for *impact* as they both mean the result of an action, often suggesting a positive one.\\\n\n\\\n\n**Option C**, *repercussion* is a distractor for Question 3, however, it is usually associated with a negative result.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{more hopeful}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I feel more connected to the planet and more hopeful for the future.”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe writer mentions that after their change of lifestyle, they feel more *hopeful* (optimistic) about the future.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{make tiny, conscious steps}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$But if we all make tiny, conscious steps, we can create a way of life that respects the earth and is healthy for all.”\\\n\n\\\n\nThe writer explains that by taking small steps, everyone can contribute to a more sustainable way of life."},{"ref":"EB0314","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0314\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about their life as a luxury pet sitter.**\n\n:audio{#d2a6d179-0db3-42a5-b52d-453b5ddf6938 aid=\"d2a6d179-0db3-42a5-b52d-453b5ddf6938\"}\n\n**Complete the following gaps. Use no more than three words or each gap.** [5 marks]\n\n**Luxury Pet Sitting Services**\n\nI take care of your beloved pets while you are **[-1-]**.\\\n\nI follow their routines, respect their diet, and go for **[-2-]**.\\\n\nI send photo and video updates every day. \\\n\nI believe every pet is unique and they should feel relaxed and **[-3-]**.\\\n\nI make sure they have what they need. Whether it is cozy bed or exercises and **[-4-]**.\\\n\nExpert care to give your pet what they **[-5-]**.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{away}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$My job is to take care of pets when their owners are away.”\\\n\nThe text mentions that the pet sitter looks after pets when the owners are away, not home.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{walks}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I give them the right food, play with them, and take them for walks or other activities”\\\n\nThe text says the pet sitter takes care of different aspects of the pet's routine, like food and exercise. The preposition *for* indicates that the activity must be something you $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$go for”\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{comfortable}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I want them to feel calm and comfortable.”\\\n\nThe word *calm* is similar to *relaxed*, so the word *comfortable* fits this space.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(fun) games}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Others need fun games and exercise.”\\\n\nThe text mentions different needs for different pets and compares the options ($\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$soft beds and quiet music” and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$fun games and exercise”) In the exercise the word *whether* introduces two possible options, just like in the original text.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{deserve}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$If you want someone who is careful, kind, and professional, I am here to help. Your pets deserve the best”\\\n\nThe word *expert* summarizes the idea of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$someone who is careful, kind, and professional,” and the word *deserve* completes the idea from the original sentence."},{"ref":"EB0315","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0315\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a person talking about bouldering\\*.**\n\n:audio{#571c9181-04c9-4691-abdf-2f291005b383 aid=\"571c9181-04c9-4691-abdf-2f291005b383\"}\n\n**Choose the three true statements.**[3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Bouldering pushes you physically and mentally. \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$When you climb, you have to think and find solutions. \n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Each route is designed to follow exact steps. \n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Bouldering makes you more relaxed. \n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Climbers are very competitive. \n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Climbing is a solitary sport. \n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Bouldering is a great way to...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$release stress.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$be balanced.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$improve your health.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Some people feel bouldering...    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$is a great pastime.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$is a part of their identity.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$is stressful.","markScheme":"$1 - 3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, B, D}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It's a sport that challenges both your body and your mind.”\\\n\nThe original sentence says the sport $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$challenges both your body and your mind.” The new version uses *pushes* instead of *challenges* and replaces $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$body and mind” with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$physically and mentally.”\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Bouldering teaches problem-solving. Each route is like a puzzle.”\\\n\nThe new sentence replaces $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$problem-solving” with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$think and find solutions.”\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$After a stressful day, climbing \\\n\nThe new sentence simplifies this idea by using $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$makes you more relaxed.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  C, E, F\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Each route is like a puzzle, and you need to find the best way to reach the top.”\\\n\nThe new sentence adds $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$exact steps,” which is incorrect because the text suggests creativity, not fixed moves.\n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Climbers support each other”\\\n\nThe new sentence changes the meaning by saying $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$competitive,” which is the opposite of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$support each other.”\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It's a social sport”\\\n\nThe new sentence uses $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$solitary,” which is wrong because the text shows climbing is social.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$After a stressful day, climbing helps you focus and disconnect.” \\\n\nThis clearly means it helps reduce or release stress.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C\n\n- Option **B** is confusing. The text says: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Finally, bouldering gives you balance.” This refers to life balance. However, the statement is about what bouldering is good for.\n\n- Option **C** is incorrect. The text mentions improved strength and flexibility, which are health benefits, so this is partly true, but stress relief is the most explicit benefit mentioned.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$it becomes part of who they are.” \\\n\nThis option is correct as the phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$part of their identity” matches the original idea of becoming $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$part of who they are”\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C\n\n- Option **A** is incorrect.  The text mentions that bouldering is $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$not just a hobby” or a *pastime*; it says it becomes something deeper than a hobby.\n\n- Option **C** is incorrect. The text says the opposite: it helps after a stressful day, so this is wrong."},{"ref":"EB0316","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0316\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a grandma talking about organizing Thanksgiving.**\n\n:audio{#10a981f1-75c4-409d-b1ec-3f85d22fe5ac aid=\"10a981f1-75c4-409d-b1ec-3f85d22fe5ac\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the first step to organize Thanksgiving?     [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How does the family ensure everything is organized?     [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why does the speaker prepare extra food?     [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do they do if the weather is not nice?     [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does grandma say she likes most about this day?     [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{making a clear plan}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the starting point is making a clear plan”\\\n\nIn the audio the speaker uses the phrase $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the starting point” which is similar in meaning to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the first step.”\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{use a group chat (to share updates)}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$We use a group chat to share updates”\\\n\nThe speaker mentions the group chat makes sure $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$nothing is forgotten” which relates to the idea of organization.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{someone will bring a friend}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$I always prepare extra food because someone will bring a friend!”\\\n\nGrandma knows that there will be extra people, so she prepares extra food.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{move inside}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$and if it's cold, we move inside”\\\n\nWhen the weather is not nice (it is cold) they move inside.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(take) a big family photo}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$we take a big family photo, and that's my favorite tradition”\\\n\nGrandma says that the $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$big family photo” is her favorite tradition, or $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$what she likes the most.”"}]},{"name":"Subcultures","questions":[{"ref":"EB0010","marks":"6","difficulty":"hard","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0010\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Subcultures and Countercultures}}$\n\nGroups in societies known as subcultures and countercultures play crucial roles in shaping personal and collective identity. Such groups are characterized by shared values, beliefs, styles, and behaviors that set them apart from the mainstream. \n\nSubcultures share interests in  music, fashion, interests, activities among others. Examples include skateboarders, Goths, and hip-hop lovers with different ways of dressing, speaking, and thinking. Some subcultures are based on the members' experience; biker culture and  the body modification subculture are some examples. Subcultures help people to develop individual identities, yet the individuals continue to be a part of the wider culture.\n\nWhile countercultures are different from the dominant culture they go against it and develop opposite values and beliefs that are usually seen as a threat to society. For instance, the Hippie subculture of the 1960s protested against materialism, militarism and the conventional norms of society, thus embracing peace, love and saving the environment. Countercultures give people an opportunity to think out of the box and challenge the existing paradigms which in the long run may bring changes in the society. Some countercultures form societies that work outside the norms of the society. \n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What, according to the text, do members of subcultures have in common? [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$According to the text, how do $\\underline{\\textrm{subcultures}}$ and $\\underline{\\textrm{countercultures}}$ differ in relation to the mainstream? [2 marks]\n\n**Find a word/phrase from paragraph 3 that means the following:**\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$defy    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$status quo    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ \n\n- a.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{music, fashion, interests, activities among others}$ [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions that “subcultures share interests” which is another way of saying that they have these in common.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ \n\n- a.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{subcultures continue to be part of the mainstream.}$  [1 mark]\n\n- The word *mainstream* refers to ideas and attitudes that are shared by most people. In this sense, subcultures continue to follow society’s rules and conventions.\n\n- b.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{countercultures work outside the norms of society.}$  [1 mark]\n\n- As opposed to subcultures, countercultures, do not follow conventional rules.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{challenge}$\n\n- *challenge* the existing paradigms means to resist the rules, therefore, defy (the paradigms) is a good replacement word. [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{existing paradigms}$\n\n- *status quo* refers to the present state of affairs (things $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$as they are”), the text uses the existing paradigms to describe this concept.  [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0011","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0011\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Goth subculture: A GUIDE TO THE GOTHIC}}$\n\nI remember telling my father I was getting my Ph.D. in Gothic literature and he replied, confused, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gothic like Goths? The people who wear all black?” While technically true, this definitely wasn't my area of study and as I tried to explain I realized how difficult it can be to define what is gothic. Historically, the Goths were a group of Germanic tribes that toppled the Roman Empire and plunged Europe into the Dark Ages. The term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gothic” was first used to describe the architecture that developed during this period which was considered barbaric and garish by Renaissance artists. Borrowing the term's association with darkness and superstition, Horace Walpole used it to describe his novel The Castle of Otranto: A Gothic Story (1765). In the 1970s, bands like Joy Division and The Cure adopted the genre's macabre elements to create Gothic rock, a genre of atmospheric post-punk rock with a thriving Goth subculture. At each stage in its evolution, the Gothic has exceeded existing boundaries to influence literature, film, popular culture and fashion to the point that a pair of black Crocs festooned in metal spikes and chains can now be described as $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$gothic”.\n\n[Source:Laredo, J. A. (n.d.). Guide to Gothic literature. Pressbooks. https://pressbooks.pub/guidetogothic/front-matter/introduction/ (Accessed November 3, 2024). CC BY 4.0. ]\n\n**The following statements are either true or false. Write True of False then justify it using words that appear in the text. Both parts are required for 1 mark.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The writer's father immediately supported his choice of topic for his PHD.     [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Horace Walpole coined the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gothic” for his novel in the 18th century.    [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The term “gothic” is nowadays limited to a literary genre.       [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is the aim of this text?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$to persuade \n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$to inform\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$to criticize\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$to advise\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$The text is a…    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$letter\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$speech\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$blog\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$news report","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification:\n\n(His father) replied, confused, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gothic, like Goths? The people who wear all black?”\\\n\nThe adjective confused implies the father's hesitation about the writer's choice of topic and the 2 questions reinforce his lack of knowledge about the topic.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{True}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Borrowing the term's association with darkness and superstition, Horace Walpole used it to describe his novel”\\\n\nThe text discusses the origin and usage of the term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$gothic”. The reference can be found in the previous sentence $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$The term $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Gothic” was first used…”\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{False}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Justification: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the Gothic has exceeded existing boundaries to influence literature, film, popular culture, and fashion”\\\n\nAlthough the term was initially used to refer to literary works with certain characteristics, the text mentions it is nowadays used in other areas such as film, popular culture, and fashion.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text informs about the origins of the Gothic movement. The tone is informative and the language is impersonal.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D\n\n- **A**: This option is incorrect, as no elements of persuasion are present in the text, such as rhetorical questions, rule of 3, or repetition. \n\n- **C**: This option is incorrect, as there is no evidence of criticism as \n\nthe writer uses impartial, objective language.\n\n- **D**: This option is incorrect as the text does not use modal verbs or modality to express advice.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text follows the characteristics of the blog as it is informative, personal and it uses informal language.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B, D\n\n- **A**: is not the correct option as the main conventions: addressee and addresser are not included.\n\n- **B**: is not the correct option as the aim of the text is not to persuade and it\n\ndoes not include the characteristic opening salutation (Fellow students, Ladies and gentlemen) from the speeches.\n\n- **D**: is not the correct option as the text does not inform about a specific event. Instead, it shares a personal anecdote and, at the same time, it explains the origin of this subculture."},{"ref":"EB0012","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0012\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n$\\Large{\\textrm{Countercultures}}$\n\nA counterculture developed in the United States in the late 1960s, lasting from approximately 1964 to 1972 and coinciding with America's involvement in Vietnam. It was characterized by the rejection of conventional social norms, specifically regarding racial segregation and support for the Vietnam War.\n\nAs the 1960s progressed, widespread tensions developed in American society that tended to flow along generational lines regarding the war in Vietnam, race relations, women's rights, traditional modes of authority, and a materialist interpretation of the American Dream. Thanks to widespread economic prosperity, white, middle-class youth—who made up the bulk of the counterculture—had sufficient leisure time to turn their attention to social issues.\n\nThe counterculture reached its peak in the 1967 $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Summer of Love,\" when thousands of young people flocked to the Haight-Ashbury district of San Francisco. \n\n[Adapted from Counterculture by Boundless used under CC BY-SA 4.0. \n\nBoundless. (n.d.). Counterculture. \\\n\nRetrieved from https://ftp.worldpossible.org/endless/eos-rachel/RACHEL/RACHEL/modules/en-boundless-static/www.boundless.com/u-s-history/textbooks/boundless-u-s-history-textbook/the-sixties-1960-1969-29/counterculture-221/counterculture-1232-9277/index.html (Accessed November 4, 2024). CC BY-SA 4.0.]\n\n**Find the word or phrase which means the following:** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$participation   [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$denial   [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$conservative   [1 mark]\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Which political event occurred at the same time the counterculture of the 1960s developed?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What caused the counterculture to have so many middle-class supporters?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**involvement**]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$coinciding with America's involvement in Vietnam”\n\n- *Involvement* and *participation* are synonyms in this context, meaning that America took part in the Vietnam war. \n\n  [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**rejection**]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$the rejection of conventional social norms”\n\n- *Denial* and *rejection* imply that this new counterculture did not agree with $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$racial segregation and support for the Vietnam War”\n\nconventional/traditional\n\n [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**conventional/traditional**] \n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$rejection of conventional social norms”\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$traditional modes of authority”\n\n- Both words *conventional* (paragraph 1) and *traditional* (paragraph 2) have a similar meaning to *conservative*. [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**the Vietnam War**] \n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A counterculture developed [...] coinciding with America's involvement in Vietnam”\n\n- The text mentions that during the years 1964 to 1972, both events, the emergence of counterculture and the Vietnam War took place in the USA. *Coinciding* means happening at the same time.[1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ :answer[**economic prosperity**] \n\n- Also accept: widespread economic prosperity\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Thanks to widespread economic prosperity, [...] had sufficient leisure time to turn their attention to social issues.”\n\n- As middle-class people benefited from the economic boom of that time, they had spare time to join the cause against the war. [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0149","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0149\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on skateboarding.**\n\n:audio{#fea36647-584f-4f1b-8d3d-5a4f4587a087 aid=\"fea36647-584f-4f1b-8d3d-5a4f4587a087\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What was skateboarding originally called? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Board surfing\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Sidewalk skating\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Sidewalk surfing\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How did skateboarding become a subculture? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It was featured in the Olympics\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It developed its own style in clothing and music\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$It was banned in Los Angeles\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does the passage suggest about skateboarding today? [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It is only for professional athletes\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It has lost its cultural significance\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$It remains a strong community and lifestyle\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What **two** things does skateboarding represent as a lifestyle?      [2 marks]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Over time, skateboarding has become a competitive sport. Give **two** examples to support this statement.     [2 marks]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text tells us the name: “sidewalk surfing” and explains that it described the purpose of the new activity: to give surfers (who usually needed to be in the water to practice), the chance to practice on dry land.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A** and **Option B** are both incorrect combinations of the words “skateboard” and “sidewalk”.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “Skateboarders … had soon created their own culture, complete with music and fashion.”  As skateboarders moved from the ocean to land, the clothing style we now think of as ‘boarding developed.  This unique look, and the music they listened to while practicing, became part of the culture that teens take part in today.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, C\n\n- **Option A**: “Although skateboarding is now a professional sport and an Olympic event”  Skateboarding began in the 1950s and the subculture began at that time: the text tells us that it became an Olympic sport sometime later.\n- **Option C**: “became a subculture on the streets of Los Angeles” Skateboarding and the culture that grew from it started in Los Angeles.  *Banned* means officially stopped or not allowed to happen, which is the opposite of what the text states.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “...many teens still find community in the lifestyle that ‘boarding represents…”  Skateboarding has grown in popularity over the years and become a professional sport, but it remains popular with teens because it still represents freedom and fun, more than 70 years later.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* A, B\n\n- **Option A**: “Although skateboarding is now a professional sport…” The text states that skateboarding has developed over time, but also tells us that it is still loved by teens, therefore, it is not only for professional athletes.\n- **Option B**:  “...many teens still find community in the lifestyle that ‘boarding represents - freedom, and a love of movement.”  The lifestyle and culture surrounding skateboarding is still relevant for today’s teens.\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{freedom}$\n-   $\\textrm{love of movement}$\n\n- “... lifestyle that ‘boarding represents - freedom, and a love of movement.” Skateboarding culture reflected the California surfer lifestyle. Freedom was represented in clothing and music and could be easily adopted by anyone, anywhere.  Skateboards allow people to move easily, both as a form of transportation as well as a sporting activity, which many teens find attractive. [2 marks]\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{(It is now) A professional sport}$\n-   $\\textrm{(It is now) An Olympic event. }$\n\n- Skateboarding began as a way for individuals to practice surfing and was not a competitive sport.  However, as it became more popular, it eventually became a professional and then an Olympic sport, both of which are competitive.  *Competitive* (from the verb *compete*) in this text describes  sports which require athletes to defeat or beat another to win or be the best.  [2 marks]"},{"ref":"EB0150","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0150\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a reporter explain the popularity of K-Pop.**\n\n:audio{#c2e314d9-ffc5-4063-8551-eef1886e9157 aid=\"c2e314d9-ffc5-4063-8551-eef1886e9157\"}\n\n1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$K-Pop fans are referred to as “stans”.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$K-Pop fans only support their favorite idols by attending concerts.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Social media helps fans connect with artists.\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Some fans learn Korean to better understand lyrics.\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$K-Pop music is only popular in Korea.\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$K-Pop fan communities do not engage in charitable activities.\n\n**Complete the following sentences using no more than three words from the text.**\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Fans can **[-4-]** in real-time on social media sites.[1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Fans around the world  **[-5-]** with others who also love the genre.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$ *Answers can be written in any order.* $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, D}$   [4 marks]\n\n- **A**. “K-Pop fans or “stans”...\n\n- *Referred to* is a synonym for *called*.\n\n- **C**. “Social media plays a huge role, allowing fans to interact with artists…”\n\n- *Interact with* and *connect with* are synonyms in this context, showing how social media creates opportunities for fans and musicians to create relationships with each other.\n\n- **D**. “Fan communities… study Korean…”\n\n- K-Pop is a global *sensation* (a sensation in this context is a person or thing that raises a lot of interest), which means that many fans are not Korean.  Many fans enjoy the music without fully understanding the words (*lyrics*), but many others learn the language to better connect with the musicians and the community.\n\n- *Incorrect options*: B, E, F\n\n- **B**.  “They attend concerts, stream music, buy albums, and vote in competitions…”  Fans support their favorite artists in several different ways.\n\n- **E**. “K-Pop, or Korean pop music, has become a global sensation.” It is popular with fans all around the world, not only in Korea.\n\n- **F**. “Fan communities… even raise money for charity.”  Giving to charity in the name of their favorite star is another way fans show support. *Engage in charitable activities* means that fans are active in doing this.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Interact with artists}$\n\n- *Accept other answers with similar meaning.*  [1 mark]\n\n- “Social media plays a huge role, allowing fans to interact with artists in real-time.”  The spread of K-Pop from Korea to the world was helped by social media, by creating a direct connection between fans and the musicians themselves.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{Form friendships (through K-Pop)}$\n\n- *Accept other answers with similar meaning.*.  [1 mark]\n\n- “Many fans find joy in the community, forming friendships with people worldwide who share their love for K-Pop.”\nThis means they might become friends online as they share similar interests."},{"ref":"EB0151","marks":"7","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0151\n\n**Maximum mark: 7**\n\n**You are going to hear a short text on a group of influential book lovers.**\n\n:audio{#2e424d7a-9e2c-4d43-b85e-93f689b9b8ef aid=\"2e424d7a-9e2c-4d43-b85e-93f689b9b8ef\"}\n\n**Choose the correct answer.**\n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is BookTok?\n [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$A community of book lovers.\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$A platform for selling books.\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$A new type of social media.\n\n**Answer the following questions.**\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What type of content do BookTok members post? Give **two** examples.     [2 marks]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Where can a new book be found after a popular BookTok video?    [1 mark]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$In what two ways do writers interact with readers on BookTok?  Give **two** examples.    [2 marks]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How is the experience of sharing books described in the text?    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- “A community of book lovers on TikTok…”  \\\nThe text tells us what BookTok is in the opening sentence.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **Option B**: the text states that “...a single viral video can push an unknown novel onto bestseller lists,” however that is not the purpose of the community. The group does not sell books, but their comments can encourage others to buy books with high recommendations.  A *viral video* is one that is seen by millions of people and can influence what people do.\n\n- **Option C**: BookTok is on TikTok, it is not a new type of social media.\n\n$2$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{Book reviews }$\n-   $\\textrm{recommendations}$\n\n- “Members post short videos reviewing books and sharing recommendations.” \\\nThis quote explains the group's main purpose. [2 marks]\n\n$3$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{(Onto) bestseller lists}$\n\n- “a single viral video can push an unknown novel onto bestseller lists.” \\\nA *bestseller* is a book or other item that is very popular at a given time.  [1 mark]\n\n$4$.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n-   $\\textrm{responding to comments}$\n-   $\\textrm{creating content}$\n\n- “Many authors interact with fans directly, responding to comments and creating content about their own books.” \\\nIt is this connection between writer and reader that makes the community unique. [2 marks]\n\n$5$.$\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{a dynamic, interactive way}$\n\n- *Accept other words with similar meaning.*\n\n- “It’s more than just a social media trend—it’s a dynamic, interactive way for people to experience stories together.” \\\nHaving a community in which to discuss books, share opinions and interact with writers and other readers has created a lot of excitement for members of the group. [1 mark]"},{"ref":"EB0269","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0269 \n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**Culture Uncovered: Therians**\n\nHey everyone, and welcome back to $`$Culture Uncovered'! Today, we're discovering the fascinating world of Therians!\n\nA Therian is someone who identifies, on a deep, personal level, as a non-human animal. This isn't about dressing up - it's an internal identity, a core part of who they are. They might have a $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$theriotype,” which is their specific animal, and experience $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$shifts,” where they feel more connected to their animal side.\n\nIt's a vibrant, diverse community all about exploring identity beyond the human experience. From wolves to house cats, their experiences are totally unique.\n\nSo, what do you think? Have you ever heard of Therians before? Drop a comment and let me know! It's a truly wild and wonderful part of our world.\n\n**Choose the three correct statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$A Therian views themselves as an animal.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Therians mostly dress up in animal costumes.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$When a Therian relates closely to their animal identity, they might feel changes.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$A $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$theriotype” is the creature a Therian identifies with.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$The Therian community focuses on wild animals.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Therians feel their identity is a small part of who they are.\n\n**Find the words that complete the following sentences. Answer using the words as they appear in paragraph 4.** [2 marks]\n\nThe Therian subculture is **[-4-]**, allowing its members to discover their own identity.\n\nEvery Therian's experience is personal and **[-5-]**.","markScheme":"1 - 3.$\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, D}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A Therian is someone who identifies, on a deep, personal level, as a non-human animal.\"\\\n\nA Therian deep down views themselves or identifies as an animal. \n\n- **C**: They might [...] experience $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$shifts,” where they feel more connected to their animal side.\\\n\nThe text mentions Therians might experience shifts or changes when they are connected (relate closely) to their animal identity.\n\n- **D**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They might have a $`$theriotype', which is their specific animal.”\n\nTheriotype is the given animal identity of a Therian.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*   B, E, F\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$This isn't about dressing up - it's an internal identity.\"\\\n\nBeing a Therian is not wearing animal costumes, but an integral part of some people's identity. \n\n- **E**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$From wolves to house cats, their experiences are diverse.\"\\\n\nThe text mentions different types of animals, not just wild animals, and adds the experiences are diverse.\n\n- **F**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... a core part of who they are.\"\\\n\nBeing a Therian is a part of the core of their existence, not only a part of their identity.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{vibrant and diverse}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: vibrant, diverse \\\n\n\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$It's a vibrant, diverse community all about exploring identity beyond the human experience. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$\\\n\nThe community is described as colourful and energetic - vibrant and diverse. Subculture is similar in meaning to community.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{unique}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Every Therian's experience is $`$totally unique' and meaningful.\" \\\n\nThe word unique, one of a kind, fits the sentence both logically and grammatically."},{"ref":"EB0270","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0270\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**Rising Tide of Hustle Culture Shapes a Generation**\n\nIn cities across the globe, $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture\" is rapidly growing among young professionals and students. Glorifying long work hours, hustle culture encourages relentless work and less downtime, as the key to personal and financial success.\n\nSocial media platforms such as TikTok and Instagram play a major role, with influencers documenting 16-hour days and constant side projects. Some celebrate the movement for promoting ambition and innovation. However,  mental health experts warn of its darker side, chronic stress, and a work-life balance that is often sacrificed in the name of achievement.\n\nOrganizations report many workers struggle to disconnect from work, with after-hours emails and $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$productivity hacks” becoming the norm. Yet, for many young people, especially Gen Z and Millennials, the pressure to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$always be busy” defines $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ sense of identity and success.\n\n**Choose the correct answer:** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What is hustle culture mainly known for?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Encouraging less relaxation and rest.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Promoting work over rest.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Glorifying team spirit at work.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Supporting work-life balance.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do hustle culture influencers use social media platforms for?     [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$to show their constant business.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$to get more side projects.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$to play a major role on TikTok and Instagram.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$to promote 16-hour work days.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do mental health experts warn about hustle culture?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$It destroys healthy work-life balance completely.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$It can lead to chronic stress and burnout.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$It forces people to work more hours.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$It discourages workers from taking regular breaks.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What challenge do organizations report regarding employees?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Employees find it easy to forget about work.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Employees struggle to disconnect due to after-hours emails.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Employees prefer to work only during office hours.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Employees work fewer hours than expected.\n\n**To what or whom does the underlined word refer in the text?** [1]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$... defines :u[their] sense of identity...    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture encourages relentless work and less downtime\"\\\n\nThe text mentions that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture” *encourages* or *promotes* relentless (non-stop) work over *time to rest* or *downtime*.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, c, D\n\n- **A**: Less rest is mentioned as part of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture” but this option is less precise than B, as it does not mention long work hours.\n\n- **C**: The text does not mention team spirit.\n\n- **D**: *Supporting*, in this case, means helping. $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Hustle culture” does not help work-life balance; it harms it.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$... with influencers documenting 16-hour days and constant side projects.”\\\n\nInfluencers record and *show* (*document*) their extremely long days on social media platforms.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, C, D\n\n- **B**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Side projects” are mentioned, but they are meant to be shown/documented, not acquired.\n\n- **C**: The text mentions that TIkTok and Instagram play a major role, not the other way around as the sentence suggests.\n\n- **D**: Influencers document their work-day, but the word *promote* is not used. \n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$mental health experts warn of its darker side: chronic stress...\"\n\nThe text mentions a series of unwanted consequences of $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture” one of which is *chronic (long-term)* stress.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, C, D\n\n- **A**: This is partially true but too extreme. The text does not say mental health is completely destroyed.\n\n- **C**: The warning is about the negative impacts of overwork, not an obligation to work more.\n\n- **D**: The text does not say $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$hustle culture” discourages workers from taking breaks.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{D}$   [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Organizations report many workers struggle to disconnect from work\"\\\n\nThe text mentions employees $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$struggle to disconnect from work\", which means they $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$keep working after hours\". \n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, B, C\n\n- **A**: The opposite is true; employees *struggle*, not find it easy, to disconnect.\n\n- **B**: The text does not mention employee preferences but highlights difficulties in disconnecting.\n\n- **C**: The text implies increased work and stress, not fewer hours.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{many young people}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: young people, Gen Z and Millenials.\\\n\n\\\n\n$\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Yet, for many young people, especially Gen Z and Millennials, the pressure to $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$always be busy” defines $\\underline{\\textrm{their}}$ sense of identity and success.\"\\\n\nThe word $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$their” refers to the young people or Gen Z and Millenials mentioned previously in the sentence."},{"ref":"EB0271","marks":"7","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Reading","type":"RCQ","body":"# EB0271\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**Why Teens Don't Pick Up the Phone Anymore**\n\nOkay, let's be real—most of us hate answering phone calls. It's not that we're $\\underline{\\textrm{rude}}$, it's just... $\\underline{\\textrm{weird}}$. Like, why call when you can text, send a voice note, or DM?\n\nTalking on the phone feels $\\underline{\\textrm{intense}}$. You have to pick up and talk *right away*, and sometimes you don't know what to say. With texting, you get time to think, chill, and reply when you're ready, and you can delete a message before it is read if needed! Another plus? You can use emojis or stickers to show how you feel.\n\nSome adults think not picking up is bad manners…well, they did have landlines, but for us, it's just normal. We're not ignoring people, we're setting boundaries. If it's super urgent, sure, call. But most of the time, a quick message works better.\n\nAnother good option is texting before calling. It's polite and gives people a heads-up. No one likes surprise calls!\n\nSo yeah, phone calls aren't dead, but for teens, they reserved for serious conversations and grandma calls. We're just changing how we communicate—and that's totally okay.\n\n**What do the following words mean in the text? Choose the appropriate words from the list.** [3 marks]\n\n|||\n\n|:--|:--|\n\n|1.$\\hspace{1em}$rude |$\\hspace{1em}$A.$\\hspace{1em}$crazy  |\n\n|2.$\\hspace{1em}$weird |$\\hspace{1em}$B.$\\hspace{1em}$impolite |\n\n|3.$\\hspace{1em}$intense |$\\hspace{1em}$C.$\\hspace{1em}$uncomfortable |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$D.$\\hspace{1em}$mean |\n\n|$\\hspace{1em}$ |$\\hspace{1em}$E.$\\hspace{1em}$too much |\n\n||$\\hspace{1em}$F.$\\hspace{1em}$stressful |\n\n**Answer the following question(s).** \n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why do teens dislike phone calls?    [1 mark]\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$How can you express emotions in texts?    [1 mark]\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why teens do not answer every call?    [1 mark]\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Why is texting before calling a good option? Give **one** reason.    [1 mark]","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Impolite* and *rude* mean not showing good manners. \\\n\n**Option D**, *mean*, could be confusing. However, *mean* implies someone is being intentionally rude or hurtful.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{C}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The correct replacement word for *weird* is *uncomfortable*. They both describe a feeling of awkwardness or tension, in this case related to picking up the phone.\\\n\n**Option A**, *crazy*, is not correct. Though *weird* and *crazy* could be similar, in this case, *crazy* is too strong.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{F}$   [1 mark]\n\n- *Stressful* is the correct replacement word for *intense*. Both words express that phone calls are a source of stress and discomfort.\\\n\n**Option E**, *too much*, could be a good replacement phrase. However, in the context of the sentence the full phrase should be $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$feels $\\underline{\\textrm{like}}$ too much” so the phrase does not fit grammatically.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{you have to pick up and talk right away}$   [1 mark]\n\n- Also accept: you have to talk right away\\\n\n\\\n\nThe text mentions that this urgency is the main reason why teens feel talking on the phone is intense.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{emojis or stickers}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions these as a way of communicating *how you feel*, your *emotions*.\n\n$6.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{setting boundaries}$   [1 mark]\n\n- The text mentions that teens do not answer every call because they are setting their boundaries, not ignoring people or being rude.\n\n$7.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{it's polite}$\n\n- $\\textrm{gives people a heads-up}$    \n\n- $\\textrm{no one likes surprise calls}$        [1 mark]\n\n- These are the reasons given in the text for texting before calling. Only **one** reason is requested."},{"ref":"EB0350","marks":"5","difficulty":"easy","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0350\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear an advertisement for student-made products.**\n\n:audio{#7f7dfe67-b7ba-448d-976d-76413dd884e5 aid=\"7f7dfe67-b7ba-448d-976d-76413dd884e5\"}\n\n**Choose the four true statements.** [4 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$The pop-up event will take place in the school gym.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Students will be selling brand-new clothing.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$The group turns used clothes into new items.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$You and your friends can wear matching clothes.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Nothing costs more than twenty dollars.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Students can pay only in cash.\n\n- G.$\\hspace{1em}$The money is used to help a local shelter.\n\n- H.$\\hspace{1em}$Students are accepting donations of unwanted clothes.\n\n**Choose the correct option.**\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What are students encouraged to do at the end of the ad?    [1 mark]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Come shop and donate unwanted clothes.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Buy only second-hand jeans and jewelry.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Join the group only if they have experience.","markScheme":"$1 - 4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A, C, G, H}$        [4 marks]\n\n- **A**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$come to the first ever Thrift-Flip Pop-Up, this Friday after school in the gym foyer.” \\\n\nThe advertisement clearly states the event location.\n\n- **C**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$students take second-hand clothes and redesign them into something totally new.” \\\n\nThis explains that the group *redesigns* (*turns*) *second-hand* or *used* clothing into new items.\n\n- **G**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$All profits go to a local shelter” \\\n\nThe text mentions that *profits*, or the *money* that is made, will be given to a local shelter.\n\n- **H**: $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$we'll also be collecting clean clothes to donate.” \\\n\nThe advertisement explains that donations of clothes will be accepted.\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  B, D, E, F\n\n- **B**: The speaker describes the students as using $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$second-hand clothes,” to create new items. They are not selling new clothes.\n\n- **D**: The speaker says that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$every item is one-of-a-kind” and that $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$no one at school will have the same look as you”, which is the opposite of this statement.\n\n- **E**: The ad says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$nothing costs more than fifteen,” not twenty.\n\n- **F**: The ad says students can pay $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$in cash or by card,” so cash is not the only option.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{A}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$come shop, clear out your closet, and support creative students”\\\n\nThis line encourages students to shop and also donate clothes they no longer want.\n\n- *Incorrect options:* B, C\n\n- **B**: The ad gives examples of products (jeans into bags, jewellery into pins), but it does not say students should buy only those items.\n\n- **C**: The ad says $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$You don't need experience,” which is the opposite of this option."},{"ref":"EB0351","marks":"5","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0351\n\n**Maximum mark: 5**\n\n**You are going to hear a short news report about fan-subbers.**\n\n:audio{#ed130164-5e83-457d-98a9-d0075b739c0b aid=\"ed130164-5e83-457d-98a9-d0075b739c0b\"}\n\n**Complete the gaps. Use no more than three words for each gap.** [5 marks]\n\nTeens are becoming more active in a **[-1-]** called $`$fan-subbers'.\n\nFan-subbers translate foreign TV shows and series and add **[-2-]**. \n\nCreating subtitles is complicated and requires many skills, so fan-subbers work **[-3-]**.\n\nMedia companies argue that fan-subbers distribute **[-4-]** without permission. \n\nWhen the official subtitles are published, fan-subbers delete **[-5-]**.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{subculture}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$A growing online subculture is gaining attention among teenagers: fan-subbers.”\\\n\nA subculture is a group of people who share an interest in a very specific topic or activity.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{subtitles}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Fan-subbers translate foreign TV shows and series... and add subtitles”\n\nThe report defines $`$fan-subbers' by stating what they do.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{in teams}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They work in teams, with different people translating, checking meaning, and timing the subtitles so they match the dialogue.”\\\n\nMaking subtitles is not a simple process, so several people work together to make sure the subtitles match what the actors actually say.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{copyrighted content}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$Media companies argue that fan-subbers share copyrighted content without permission.”\\\n\nThe report identifies what companies claim is being distributed illegally.\n\n$5.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{their (online) content}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$remove their online content once official subtitles become available.”\\\n\nFan-subbers say that they are not doing anything illegal and, when official subtitles are released, they *remove* or *delete* the subtitles they created."},{"ref":"EB0352","marks":"8","difficulty":"medium","calculator":false,"paper":"Paper 2: Listening","type":"LCQ","body":"# EB0352\n\n**Maximum mark: 8**\n\n**You are going to hear a podcast about teenagers who collect vinyl records.**\n\n:audio{#4550ce56-0a4c-42e7-bb49-0fbad31b0a17 aid=\"4550ce56-0a4c-42e7-bb49-0fbad31b0a17\"}\n\n**Answer the following questions.** \n\n$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What do vinyl-teens prefer?     [1 mark]\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$What does buying vinyl offer that streaming does not?     [1 mark]\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name **two** things teens like about vinyls.     [2 marks]\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$Name **one** way vinyl-teens save money in their collections.    [1 mark]\n\n**Choose the three true statements.** [3 marks]\n\n- A.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco believes the growing interest in vinyl is mainly about music quality.\n\n- B.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco says the interest in vinyl is not only about music.\n\n- C.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco suggests that buying vinyl can help support artists and local shops.\n\n- D.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco believes most teenagers prefer large online platforms.\n\n- E.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco says vinyl collecting can help build a sense of identity and community.\n\n- F.$\\hspace{1em}$Marco believes teens feel isolated rather than connected through vinyl.","markScheme":"$1.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{records}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$young people choosing records”\\\n\nThe podcast mentions $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$vinyl-teens” choose or prefer records to streaming.\n\n$2.$ $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{physical interaction}$    [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$buying vinyl offers teens a physical interaction that streaming cannot match”\\\n\nMarco believes that the teens like being able to physically interact with the music in a way they cannot do with streamed music.\n\n$3.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any two of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{the cover art }$\n\n- $\\textrm{the printed lyrics}$    \n\n- $\\textrm{the posters}$        [2 marks]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$They love the cover art, the printed lyrics, even the posters...”\\\n\nMarco lists these as the physical extras that come with vinyl records that teens really enjoy.\n\n$4.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*any one of the following answers:*   \n\n- $\\textrm{(they) buy second-hand }$\n\n- $\\textrm{(they) share collections }$        [1 mark]\n\n- $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$many teenagers buy second-hand copies or share collections with friends”\\\n\nMarco mentions records are expensive, so teens find ways to save money when collecting by buying second-hand or sharing collections.\n\n$5 - 8.$ $\\hspace{1em}$*The answers can be written in any order.*\n\n- $\\hspace{1em}$ $\\textrm{B, C, E}$        [3 marks]\n\n- **B**: Marco states that the interest $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$isn't only about music.”\n\n- **C**: Marco believes that part of the interest in buying vinyl comes from young people's interest in supporting local communities.\n\n- **E**: The text explains that buying and collecting vinyl helps build $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$a sense of identity and community with like-minded people.”\n\n- *Incorrect options:*  A, D, F\n\n- **A**: The text says the trend $\\text{\\textquotedblleft}$isn't only about music,” not mainly about music quality.\n\n- **D**: The text suggests teens prefer to support local businesses and NOT buy from online platforms or large (global) companies.\n\n- **F**: The text describes building community, not isolation."}]}]}]};
